not read OKF path: oracle/read.md"]],["code",["string","ERR_OKF_READ"]],["path",["string","oracle/read.md"]],["cause",["object",493,"object-prototype",[["present",["boolean",false]]]]]]]],["1",["object",494,"object-prototype",[["ownEnumerableKeys",["array",495,4,[["0",["string","code"]],["1",["string","path"]],["2",["string","name"]],["3",["string","field"]]]]],["ownPropertyNames",["array",496,5,[["0",["string","message"]],["1",["string","code"]],["2",["string","path"]],["3",["string","name"]],["4",["string","field"]]]]],["name",["string","PrepareError"]],["message",["string","Cannot parse OKF concept: oracle/parse.md (frontmatter)"]],["code",["string","ERR_OKF_PARSE"]],["path",["string","oracle/parse.md"]],["field",["string","frontmatter"]],["cause",["object",497,"object-prototype",[["present",["boolean",false]]]]]]]],["2",["object",498,"object-prototype",[["ownEnumerableKeys",["array",499,4,[["0",["string","code"]],["1",["string","path"]],["2",["string","name"]],["3",["string","field"]]]]],["ownPropertyNames",["array",500,6,[["0",["string","message"]],["1",["string","cause"]],["2",["string","code"]],["3",["string","path"]],["4",["string","name"]],["5",["string","field"]]]]],["name",["string","PrepareError"]],["message",["string","Invalid OKF field: oracle/field.md"]],["code",["string","ERR_OKF_FIELD"]],["path",["string","oracle/field.md"]],["field",["string",""]],["cause",["object",501,"object-prototype",[["present",["boolean",true]],["category",["string","Error"]]]]]]]]]]],["demo",["object",502,"object-prototype",[["manifestFingerprint",["object",503,"object-prototype",[["documents",["number","4045000000000000"]],["bytes",["number","40ebd9c000000000"]],["manifestFingerprint",["string","294ebf09df161737de19cdf92c51a2a7a66593a72b88f78a899f93ab495c3c27"]]]]],["read",["array",504,42,[["0",["object",505,"object-prototype",[["path",["string","ceramics/bisque-inspection.md"]],["markdown",["string","---\ntype: concept\ntitle: Bisque Inspection\ndescription: Checks for dryness, strength, and surface problems before bisque-fired ware enters the glaze area.\ntags:\n  - bisque\n  - inspection\n  - glaze\nstatus: stable\n---\n\n# Bisque Inspection\n\n## Check Surfaces\n\nHandle each piece over a padded table and inspect the foot, rim, attachments, and inside corners. Look for cracks, sharp flakes, kiln wash stuck to the base, loose shelves, pinholes, and pieces fused to neighbors. Run a clean fingertip around the rim; it should not catch on a burr. Brush away loose crumbs outdoors or with the approved damp method, not by blowing.\n\n## Sort for Glaze\n\nSeparate sound ware from pieces needing repair, testing, or disposal. Confirm that the clay body and firing temperature match the glaze plan. Wipe the foot with a slightly damp sponge and allow it to dry before applying glaze. If a piece is unusually dark, soft, smoky, or much heavier than expected, hold it for the firing lead because it may not have been fully fired or cooled correctly.\n\n## Record Problems\n\nMark the maker's work clearly and photograph defects that are easy to miss. Record the piece ID, defect, shelf position, and recommended action on the inspection sheet. Never hide a crack beneath glaze or send questionable ware into a shared firing without the maker's informed decision and lead approval.\n"]]]]],["1",["object",506,"object-prototype",[["path",["string","ceramics/clay-reclaim.md"]],["markdown",["string","---\ntype: concept\ntitle: Clay Reclaim\ndescription: A practical cycle for sorting, rehydrating, and returning usable clay to studio stock.\ntags:\n  - clay\n  - reclaim\n  - studio\nstatus: stable\n---\n\n# Clay Reclaim\n\n## Sort Before Slaking\n\nCollect trimmings, failed pieces, and throwing scraps in the labeled reclaim buckets. Remove plaster, bisque, wood, glaze chips, and plastic before adding anything. Keep white, buff, red, and dark bodies separate unless the studio manager has approved a mixed batch. Clay that has touched glaze should go into the waste stream because fired colorants can contaminate new work.\n\n## Reclaim Workflow\n\nBreak dry scraps into pieces smaller than a fist and cover them with water. Let the bucket stand until the pieces are soft throughout, then pour off excess water and blend the slurry with the reclaim mixer. Spread the mixture on plaster bats or a canvas board in a thin layer. Turn it when the surface firms, wedge it when it reaches a workable consistency, and return it to an airtight bag.\n\n## Drying and Return\n\nMark each bag with body type and date. Test a small lump before restocking; it should wedge smoothly without sour odor, hard cores, or gritty inclusions. Quarantine questionable batches for review rather than mixing them into clean stock.\n"]]]]],["2",["object",507,"object-prototype",[["path",["string","ceramics/dust-control.md"]],["markdown",["string","---\ntype: concept\ntitle: Dust Control\ndescription: Studio habits that prevent dry ceramic particles from becoming airborne or entering drains.\ntags:\n  - dust\n  - safety\n  - cleanup\nstatus: stable\n---\n\n# Dust Control\n\n## Prevent Dust\n\nKeep powdered clay, glaze, and dry sanding to the designated area, with local ventilation running. Wear the respiratory protection required by the studio risk assessment when handling powders. Avoid shaking dusty aprons, sweeping dry floors, using compressed air, or blowing across shelves. Store powders closed and clean spills before they are tracked through the room.\n\n## Clean Without Spreading\n\nUse a damp sponge, wet mop, or studio-approved HEPA vacuum for settled material. Work from clean surfaces toward the dirtiest area, and rinse cleaning water into the solids-collection system rather than a regular drain. Clean shelves and wheel splash pans before residue dries. Wash hands and forearms after cleanup, even when gloves were worn, and keep food and drinks outside the workroom.\n\n## Respond to Exposure\n\nStop the activity if a cloud forms or ventilation fails. Move people away from the source, close the container if safe, and notify the studio lead. Anyone with breathing irritation should leave the area and follow the posted first-aid or medical guidance. Record the material, location, and cleanup steps so the next shift can correct the cause.\n"]]]]],["3",["object",508,"object-prototype",[["path",["string","ceramics/exhibition-packing.md"]],["markdown",["string","---\ntype: concept\ntitle: Exhibition Packing\ndescription: A protective packing method for moving finished ceramic work from studio shelves to an exhibition venue.\ntags:\n  - exhibition\n  - packing\n  - transport\nstatus: stable\n---\n\n# Exhibition Packing\n\n## Plan the Package\n\nMeasure the work, choose a rigid box with room for cushioning, and gather clean paper, foam, tape, labels, and a packing list. Photograph the piece before wrapping, including its foot and any existing flaw. Do not use loose packing peanuts or materials that shed dust. For a group shipment, assign each package an ID that matches the inventory and installation plan.\n\n## Wrap and Cushion\n\nWrap handles, rims, and protrusions separately with soft tissue or paper before adding a larger layer. Surround the object with resilient cushioning on every side, keeping pressure off delicate edges. Place it in the center of the box so it cannot touch the walls. Close the box temporarily and perform a gentle movement test; if the piece shifts, reopen it and add fitted support rather than tighter pressure.\n\n## Label and Handoff\n\nSeal the package with tape that can be removed without contacting the artwork. Mark orientation, fragile handling, sender, recipient, package ID, and unpacking instructions. Keep the inventory, insurance value, and tracking number with the shipment record, not loose inside the box. Photograph the closed package at handoff and obtain a named receiver's confirmation.\n"]]]]],["4",["object",509,"object-prototype",[["path",["string","ceramics/firing-schedule.md"]],["markdown",["string","---\ntype: concept\ntitle: Firing Schedule\ndescription: A queueing practice for assigning community-studio work to safe bisque and glaze firing windows.\ntags:\n  - firing\n  - scheduling\n  - kiln\nstatus: draft\n---\n\n# Firing Schedule\n\n## Build the Queue\n\nSubmit work by the studio cutoff with the maker's name, clay body, firing type, target cone, piece count, and contact details. Confirm that each piece is bone dry, identified on its underside, and within the posted size and weight limits. Group compatible work by program; do not combine loads merely to fill empty space when temperatures or glaze risks differ.\n\n## Choose a Program\n\nThe firing lead reviews the queue against kiln capacity, shelf space, element condition, and upcoming events. Use the approved bisque or glaze program rather than changing ramps casually. Add a slower schedule for thick sections only when the lead has assessed the load. Flag low-fire, mid-fire, test, and uncertain work clearly so a mistaken program is less likely.\n\n## Communicate Results\n\nPost the planned kiln number, load date, program, and expected unload date. If work is delayed, cracked, stuck, or held for inspection, tell the maker and update the queue instead of guessing. After unloading, record actual peak temperature, hold time, witness-cone results, and any abnormal alarms. Retain failed test pieces for troubleshooting when space permits.\n"]]]]],["5",["object",510,"object-prototype",[["path",["string","ceramics/glaze-mixing.md"]],["markdown",["string","---\ntype: concept\ntitle: Glaze Mixing\ndescription: A repeatable studio practice for preparing, testing, labeling, and storing ceramic glaze.\ntags:\n  - glaze\n  - materials\n  - testing\nstatus: stable\n---\n\n# Glaze Mixing\n\n## Gather and Check\n\nRead the recipe card and verify that every ingredient is present, correctly labeled, and within its approved storage area. Wear the studio respirator or mask, gloves, eye protection, and apron required for powdered materials. Record the batch size, lot numbers, mixer setting, and date before opening bags. Never substitute an ingredient without a revised test plan.\n\n## Mix and Sieve\n\nMeasure powders on the designated scale, keeping the container on its tray to catch spills. Add water to the mixing bucket before powders, then blend slowly until no dry pockets remain. Pass the slurry through the specified sieve, working in small portions and rinsing the screen between colors. Measure specific gravity or viscosity when the recipe calls for it, and adjust only with documented additions.\n\n## Label and Store\n\nPut the glaze name, batch number, firing range, hazards, and mixing date on the bucket. Keep a retained test tile and a small sample when the batch is new. Seal the lid, wipe the rim, and return tools to the glaze area. Log settling, crawling, unusual color, or contamination before anyone applies the batch.\n"]]]]],["6",["object",511,"object-prototype",[["path",["string","ceramics/kiln-loading.md"]],["markdown",["string","---\ntype: concept\ntitle: Kiln Loading\ndescription: A shared-studio method for spacing, supporting, and documenting ware before a kiln firing.\ntags:\n  - kiln\n  - firing\n  - safety\nstatus: stable\n---\n\n# Kiln Loading\n\n## Prepare Shelves\n\nConfirm the firing type, target cone, and kiln shelf size before moving ware. Brush loose dust from shelves outdoors or with the studio's approved damp method. Check that each shelf is flat, dry, and free of glaze drips. Add posts of equal height at three or four stable points, leaving enough clearance for heat circulation and the lid.\n\n## Arrange Ware\n\nLoad only fully dry pieces with no trapped water, damp clay, or unfired glaze on their bases. Keep glazed surfaces from touching shelves, posts, or neighboring work. Place taller forms toward the center when possible, and leave a finger-width gap around pieces so they can expand and contract. Use kiln cookies under pieces that may run, but do not hide a damaged foot beneath one.\n\n## Close and Record\n\nPhotograph the finished load if the arrangement is unusual. Record shelf levels, owner names, firing program, and any known risks on the kiln sheet. Inspect the lid seal and element path, then ask the firing lead to approve the load before starting it.\n"]]]]],["7",["object",512,"object-prototype",[["path",["string","ceramics/studio-opening.md"]],["markdown",["string","---\ntype: concept\ntitle: Studio Opening\ndescription: A short opening routine that makes a community ceramics studio safe and usable for the first session.\ntags:\n  - opening\n  - safety\n  - studio\nstatus: stable\n---\n\n# Studio Opening\n\n## Enter and Inspect\n\nUnlock the studio using the current access procedure and sign in. Walk the room before turning on equipment. Look for water on the floor, blocked exits, damaged cords, kiln alarms, chemical spills, and tools left in unsafe places. Photograph or isolate anything that could injure someone, then contact the designated lead instead of quietly working around it.\n\n## Start Shared Equipment\n\nCheck that ventilation is clear and operating before opening glaze materials. Turn on only the lights, wheels, slab roller, or other equipment needed for the session. Confirm that the kiln area is restricted while a firing is hot, and review the firing sheet for an active program. Keep doors and fire extinguishers accessible throughout the day.\n\n## Prepare Work Areas\n\nRestock clean bats, ribs, sponges, and hand-washing supplies. Set out damp-cleaning tools rather than dry brooms. Confirm that reclaim, scrap, glaze, and general waste containers are labeled and not full. Note shortages on the studio board, then welcome arriving members with a reminder about orientation requirements and today's hazards.\n"]]]]],["8",["object",513,"object-prototype",[["path",["string","ceramics/tool-checkout.md"]],["markdown",["string","---\ntype: concept\ntitle: Tool Checkout\ndescription: A simple custody routine for borrowing, using, and returning shared ceramics tools.\ntags:\n  - tools\n  - checkout\n  - stewardship\nstatus: draft\n---\n\n# Tool Checkout\n\n## Before Borrowing\n\nFind the tool in its labeled drawer or rack and inspect it before signing it out. Note missing handles, cracked ribs, bent wires, loose blades, and dried clay. Record the tool ID, your name, checkout time, and expected return when the studio uses a paper or digital register. Ask before taking specialty tools reserved for classes, repairs, or exhibition work.\n\n## During Use\n\nUse each tool for its intended material and keep it on a tray when moving between stations. Do not leave sharp needles, fettling knives, or cutting wires hidden under clay. Rinse tools at the designated sink or wipe them with a damp cloth, keeping plaster and glaze solids out of drains. If a tool breaks, stop using it and save the pieces for the steward.\n\n## Return and Repair\n\nRemove all clay, dry the tool, and return it to its marked location before ending the session. Sign it back in with the actual return time. Tag damaged equipment with a short description instead of placing it back in circulation. Report a missing item promptly; a clear record is more useful than replacing a tool twice.\n"]]]]],["9",["object",514,"object-prototype",[["path",["string","ceramics/wheel-maintenance.md"]],["markdown",["string","---\ntype: concept\ntitle: Wheel Maintenance\ndescription: Routine care that keeps shared pottery wheels clean, quiet, and ready for the next maker.\ntags:\n  - wheels\n  - maintenance\n  - cleanup\nstatus: stable\n---\n\n# Wheel Maintenance\n\n## Daily Cleanup\n\nSwitch off and unplug the wheel before removing splash pans or reaching near the belt. Scrape wet clay into the reclaim bin while it is still soft; never wash chunks into the drain. Wipe the pan, bat pins, wheel head, seat, and nearby floor with a damp sponge. Check that the foot pedal is dry and that the power cord is not under a chair or table leg.\n\n## Weekly Service\n\nThe studio steward should remove the splash pan, clean the drain channel, and inspect the belt area for packed clay. Tighten loose bat pins and chair hardware with the correct tool. Listen for grinding, pulsing speed, unusual heat, or wobble during a short empty test. Keep lubricants and water away from motor openings unless the manufacturer's instructions specifically allow them.\n\n## Faults and Handover\n\nTag a wheel that behaves unpredictably and move its plug to the off position. Write the wheel number, symptom, date, and last user in the maintenance log. Do not open electrical covers or return the wheel to service until a qualified person has checked it.\n"]]]]],["10",["object",515,"object-prototype",[["path",["string","gardens/autumn-garden-checklist.md"]],["markdown",["string","---\ntype: plan\ntitle: Autumn Garden Checklist\ndescription: Tasks that prepare a garden for cooler weather.\ntags: [garden, autumn, maintenance]\n---\n\n# Autumn Garden Checklist\n\n- Collect seeds from mature plants.\n- Add fallen leaves to the compost pile.\n- Mulch perennial beds before the first hard frost.\n- Clean and store garden tools.\n"]]]]],["11",["object",516,"object-prototype",[["path",["string","gardens/composting.md"]],["markdown",["string","---\ntype: concept\ntitle: Composting\ntags:\n  - compost\n  - organic-matter\n  - waste\nstatus: stable\n---\n# Composting\n\nThe garden compost system turns plant trimmings and approved kitchen scraps into a soil amendment. A pile needs a mix of moist, nitrogen-rich materials and dry, carbon-rich materials, plus air and enough moisture for microbes to work. Keep the system simple so plot holders can use it correctly.\n\n## What Goes In\n\nAdd disease-free leaves, weeds before they set seed, spent annual plants, chopped stems, coffee grounds, and untreated grass clippings. Balance wet greens with dry leaves, shredded cardboard, or straw. Do not add meat, dairy, pet waste, glossy paper, chemically treated wood, or invasive weeds. Put questionable material in the trash or ask the garden coordinator.\n\n## Manage the Pile\n\nBuild the pile at least three feet wide, and keep it as damp as a wrung-out sponge. Turn or loosen it with a fork when the center is compacted, smells sour, or stays cold after fresh material is added. A covered bin prevents flooding while still allowing airflow. Keep paths clear and close the lid after each deposit.\n\n## Know When It Is Ready\n\nFinished compost is dark, crumbly, and earthy-smelling; the original ingredients are difficult to recognize. Screen out large pieces and return them to the active pile. Spread the finished material around crops or mix a modest amount into new beds rather than burying a thick layer against stems."]]]]],["12",["object",517,"object-prototype",[["path",["string","gardens/crop-rotation.md"]],["markdown",["string","---\ntype: concept\ntitle: Crop Rotation\ntags:\n  - rotation\n  - plant-families\n  - soil-health\nstatus: stable\n---\n# Crop Rotation\n\nCrop rotation moves related crops between beds each year. It reduces recurring soil pests and diseases while balancing nutrient demand. Rotation works best when the plan tracks plant families, not just common crop names.\n\n## Group Crops by Family\n\nKeep tomatoes, peppers, eggplants, and potatoes together as nightshades; group cabbage, kale, and radishes as brassicas; and group beans and peas as legumes. Cucumbers, squash, and melons are cucurbits. Note perennial crops separately because they should not move each year. A family-labeled bed map is easier to follow.\n\n## Use a Simple Sequence\n\nMove heavy-feeding fruiting crops away from the same bed for at least two seasons when space allows. Follow them with legumes or a cover crop, then use the bed for leafy crops or roots. Rotation is not a cure for contaminated soil, and it cannot replace sanitation, resistant varieties, or good airflow, but it lowers repeated exposure.\n\n## Coordinate the Garden\n\nRecord this year’s families at the shed and copy the map before winter cleanup. If plots are managed separately, coordinate adjacent beds so a disease is not simply moved a few feet away. When space is tight, rotate the most vulnerable families first and use containers or fresh soil for crops that cannot move. Review harvest and disease notes before choosing next year’s placements."]]]]],["13",["object",518,"object-prototype",[["path",["string","gardens/drip-irrigation.md"]],["markdown",["string","---\ntype: concept\ntitle: Drip Irrigation\ntags:\n  - irrigation\n  - water\n  - efficiency\nstatus: stable\n---\n# Drip Irrigation\n\nDrip irrigation delivers water at the soil surface, helping beds use less water while keeping leaves dry. Design each line for the crop spacing and bed length, and label valves so volunteers can shut down one section without guessing. Check the system before the first hot week rather than waiting for wilted plants.\n\n## Set Up the Lines\n\nStart with a filter and pressure regulator if the supply requires them. Lay emitters beside plant roots, not across paths, and secure tubing with pins so it stays in place during weeding. Use a flush end on each line. Separate containers, seedlings, and established crops into zones.\n\n## Water by Observation\n\nRun the system long enough to wet the root zone, then let the surface dry slightly before watering again. Push a finger or trowel several inches into the soil to check moisture; a timer cannot account for rain, mulch, or soil type. Seedlings may need short, frequent cycles, while deep-rooted crops benefit from less frequent soaking.\n\n## Maintain Shared Equipment\n\nInspect for leaks, clogged emitters, and damaged tubing every week. Flush lines at the ends, clean the filter, and repair punctures with connectors. Drain or remove exposed equipment before freezing weather. Record repairs and the normal schedule on the valve box so the next volunteer can continue without resetting the whole system."]]]]],["14",["object",519,"object-prototype",[["path",["string","gardens/experimental-mulch-notes.md"]],["markdown",["string","---\ntype: concept\ntitle: Experimental Mulch Notes\ndescription: Early observations from a trial comparing garden mulches.\ntags: [garden, mulch, experiment]\nstatus: experimental\n---\n\n# Experimental Mulch Notes\n\nThree neighboring beds are testing leaf mold, clean straw, and wood chips. Each bed receives the same watering schedule so surface moisture, weed pressure, and soil temperature can be compared.\n\nThese observations are preliminary. Check beneath each mulch layer weekly, record moisture before watering, and note any slug activity or compacted material before changing the trial.\n"]]]]],["15",["object",520,"object-prototype",[["path",["string","gardens/greenhouse-basics.md"]],["markdown",["string","---\ntype: concept\ntitle: Greenhouse Basics\ntags:\n  - greenhouse\n  - seedlings\n  - ventilation\nstatus: stable\n---\n# Greenhouse Basics\n\nA small greenhouse extends the growing season, but sunlight can make it dangerously hot. Monitor it rather than using it as storage. Keep a thermometer at plant height, assign opening and closing duties, and post a checklist by the door.\n\n## Control Temperature and Air\n\nOpen vents or doors early when the inside temperature begins rising, and close them before a cold night. Use shade cloth during intense summer sun, leaving enough light for sturdy growth. Air movement reduces fungal disease; a fan should circulate air without drying seedlings. Do not crowd trays against glazing or block vents with supplies.\n\n## Water and Organize\n\nWater in the morning so foliage and benches dry before evening. Check each tray individually because different crops and container sizes lose moisture at different rates. Keep hoses off walkways, store tools clean, and raise seedling trays from the floor to improve drainage. Label every flat with crop, variety, sowing date, and owner or destination bed.\n\n## Clean Between Uses\n\nRemove dead leaves and spilled mix immediately. At the end of a crop cycle, empty benches, wash trays, and disinfect reusable containers with an approved solution. Inspect glazing, screens, and heaters before the season starts. If a plant shows an unfamiliar disease or insect outbreak, isolate the tray and notify the greenhouse steward before moving anything else."]]]]],["16",["object",521,"object-prototype",[["path",["string","gardens/harvest-storage.md"]],["markdown",["string","---\ntype: concept\ntitle: Harvest and Storage\ntags:\n  - harvest\n  - storage\n  - preservation\nstatus: stable\n---\n# Harvest and Storage\n\nStorage begins with harvesting at the useful stage and handling produce gently. Pick produce during cool hours, use clean containers, and label donations with the crop, date, and preparation notes. Regular harvests keep plants producing and limit pests.\n\n## Pick for Quality\n\nHarvest leafy greens while leaves are crisp, beans before seeds swell, and tomatoes when they have begun to color if they must travel. Cut squash and herbs with clean tools rather than tearing stems. Keep dry-stored produce unwashed; brush off soil and remove damage. Cool tender crops promptly, but keep onions, garlic, and winter squash dry and warm enough to cure.\n\n## Store by Need\n\nRefrigerate greens, carrots, beets, and most herbs in breathable containers with moisture. Keep potatoes dark and ventilated. Cure onions and garlic in a dry, airy place until skins are papery. Store winter squash where it will not freeze, and inspect it weekly. Keep ethylene-producing ripe fruit away from sensitive greens when possible.\n\n## Preserve and Share\n\nFreeze, dry, ferment, or can surplus only with tested recipes and clean equipment. Mark every jar or bag with contents and date. A harvest board can match extra produce with the community fridge or a scheduled distribution before quality declines. Compost anything unsafe rather than passing it along."]]]]],["17",["object",522,"object-prototype",[["path",["string","gardens/pest-management.md"]],["markdown",["string","---\ntype: concept\ntitle: Pest Management\ntags:\n  - pests\n  - scouting\n  - prevention\nstatus: stable\n---\n# Pest Management\n\nPest management in a community garden starts with prevention and observation, not automatic spraying. A few holes or aphids may be tolerable when plants are growing well. Set an action threshold for each crop, protect young plants, and record what was found so neighboring plot holders can respond consistently.\n\n## Scout Systematically\n\nWalk every bed weekly, checking leaf undersides, stems, new growth, and soil around the plant. Look for chewed edges, stippling, sticky residue, wilt, and clusters of insects. Identify the pest before choosing a treatment; insect damage, nutrient problems, and plant diseases can look similar. Photograph unfamiliar symptoms and ask the garden coordinator before taking action.\n\n## Prevent Problems\n\nRemove diseased leaves promptly and discard them according to the garden’s rules. Use row cover over crops that need protection, keep plants spaced for airflow, and rotate crop families between beds. Water at the soil line and clear fallen fruit that attracts pests. Encourage predators with flowers and avoid broad-spectrum products that harm them.\n\n## Treat Carefully\n\nFirst try hand-picking, a water spray, pruning, or a barrier. If a product is approved, read its label, protect pollinators, spray only the affected crop, and follow the re-entry and harvest interval. Never apply a neighbor’s unlabelled mixture. Log the date, product, bed, and result so future decisions improve."]]]]],["18",["object",523,"object-prototype",[["path",["string","gardens/pollinator-habitat.md"]],["markdown",["string","---\ntype: concept\ntitle: Pollinator Habitat\ntags:\n  - pollinators\n  - flowers\n  - biodiversity\nstatus: stable\n---\n# Pollinator Habitat\n\nA pollinator area gives bees, butterflies, and beneficial insects food and shelter while supporting garden crops. Plan a continuous sequence of blooms from spring through fall, and choose plants that suit the site instead of buying a decorative mix that may not thrive. Keep flowering plants separate from crops that need frequent spraying or digging.\n\n## Choose and Arrange Plants\n\nCombine early flowers such as willow, crocus, or native spring perennials with summer herbs, sunflowers, and fall asters. Cluster each species in patches so insects can find it easily. Include plants with different flower shapes and leave some stems hollow or pithy for nesting. Provide a shallow dish of stones and water, refreshed often, rather than a deep open container.\n\n## Tend the Habitat\n\nAvoid pesticides in the pollinator bed, especially when flowers are open. Hand-pull weeds before they seed, but leave a few non-invasive host plants when they support caterpillars. Do not deadhead every plant in autumn; seed heads feed birds and standing stems shelter insects. Cut old growth in late winter after temperatures rise, and compost only material that is free of disease.\n\n## Share the Space\n\nPost a small sign naming bloom periods and asking visitors not to disturb nesting areas. A monthly count of visiting insects can guide future plant choices and help children participate in stewardship."]]]]],["19",["object",524,"object-prototype",[["path",["string","gardens/seasonal-planning.md"]],["markdown",["string","---\ntype: concept\ntitle: Seasonal Planning\ntags:\n  - planning\n  - seasons\n  - crops\nstatus: stable\n---\n# Seasonal Planning\n\nSeasonal planning turns a shared garden’s beds, tools, and volunteer time into a manageable crop calendar. Begin with the harvests members want, then fit varieties to the site, last-frost estimate, water capacity, and labor. Leave room for weather delays instead of treating every date as fixed.\n\n## Map the Year\n\nDraw the beds and note sun, shade, paths, irrigation zones, and perennial plants. For each crop, record days to maturity, preferred temperature, spacing, and whether it follows or precedes another crop. Schedule cool-season greens and peas before warm-season tomatoes, peppers, or beans. Reserve at least one bed for flowers, cover crops, or a late-season community planting.\n\n## Stagger and Share\n\nSow small batches of quick crops every two or three weeks rather than planting the whole bed at once. Staggering harvests prevents a glut and spreads watering and picking tasks. Post the plan at the shed, identify a bed steward, and mark who will start, transplant, weed, and harvest each crop. Use a shared calendar for workdays and irrigation changes.\n\n## Review the Plan\n\nAt the end of each season, compare intended and actual harvest dates, note varieties that struggled, and record empty periods or overfilled beds. Carry useful observations into the next calendar, but change one or two variables at a time so the garden learns what actually improved."]]]]],["20",["object",525,"object-prototype",[["path",["string","gardens/seed-starting.md"]],["markdown",["string","---\ntype: concept\ntitle: Seed Starting\ntags:\n  - seeds\n  - seedlings\n  - propagation\nstatus: stable\n---\n# Seed Starting\n\nStarting seeds indoors lets a community garden grow reliable transplants and spread limited greenhouse space fairly. Match the start date to the crop and the local last-frost estimate, then count backward from the date seedlings should move outside. Keep a log of variety, sowing date, germination, and transplant performance.\n\n## Sow Evenly\n\nUse clean trays or reused pots with drainage holes and a fine, damp seed-starting mix. Plant at the depth on the packet; tiny seeds often need light or only a dusting of mix. Label every container with crop and variety. Cover trays to hold moisture, but uncover them as soon as most seedlings emerge so stems do not stretch or rot.\n\n## Give Seedlings What They Need\n\nPlace seedlings under a light close enough to prevent leaning, and run it on a consistent daily schedule. Water from below or with a gentle stream when the surface begins to dry. A small fan moving air across the leaves encourages sturdy stems and lowers disease risk. Thin crowded starts by snipping extras rather than pulling neighboring roots.\n\n## Harden Off\n\nAbout a week before planting, move trays outdoors for increasing periods in sheltered shade. Gradually introduce sun and wind, and bring them in during cold nights. Transplant after the bed is prepared and the weather matches the crop, then water thoroughly."]]]]],["21",["object",526,"object-prototype",[["path",["string","gardens/soil-health.md"]],["markdown",["string","---\ntype: concept\ntitle: Soil Health\ntags:\n  - soil\n  - nutrients\n  - gardening\nstatus: stable\n---\n# Soil Health\n\nHealthy soil gives the community garden strong roots, steady growth, and fewer irrigation problems. Treat it as a living system rather than an inert planting medium. Observe it each season, add organic matter gradually, and avoid practices that leave beds bare or compacted.\n\n## Check Before Planting\n\nSqueeze a handful of moist soil. It should form a loose ball that crumbles when tapped, not a sticky lump or dusty handful. Look for earthworms, roots, and a range of crumb sizes. A simple soil test can show pH and available nutrients; record results by bed so amendments are based on evidence. Never add lime, sulfur, or fertilizer just because a neighboring plot used it.\n\n## Build Fertility\n\nSpread finished compost in a thin, even layer before planting, then mix it into only the top few inches if the bed needs incorporation. Use cover crops or leaf mulch to feed organisms and reduce erosion. Rotate heavy feeders such as tomatoes with beans or leafy greens, and leave plant residue only when it is healthy.\n\n## Protect the Bed\n\nKeep foot traffic on paths, not growing soil. Use boards or designated stepping stones for shared beds. Mulch exposed ground, and water slowly enough that soil structure stays intact."]]]]],["22",["object",527,"object-prototype",[["path",["string","observatory/calibration-frames.md"]],["markdown",["string","---\ntype: concept\ntitle: Calibration Frames\ntags:\n  - calibration\n  - imaging\n  - detectors\nstatus: draft\n---\n# Calibration Frames\n\n## Purpose\nCalibration frames measure detector behavior so science exposures can be corrected without erasing real signal. The observatory collects bias, dark, flat, and, when spectroscopy is scheduled, arc-lamp frames for each relevant instrument setup.\n\n## Collection\nTake at least  fifteen bias frames at the shortest supported exposure and the same readout mode as the science run. Capture five darks at each science exposure time, with the shutter closed and the detector at its regulated temperature. For flats, aim for counts near half the detector's linear range; take twilight flats for imaging and lamp flats for a stable spectrograph response. Change the calibration set whenever filter, binning, gain, grating, or detector temperature changes. Never combine frames from different hardware configurations merely because their filenames look similar.\n\n## Quality checks\nInspect the median level, saturated-pixel fraction, gradients, and cosmic-ray contamination. Reject frames with shutter motion, unstable lamp output, or counts outside the linear range. Store masters with their input list, combine method, temperature, and processing version. Link the master set to the science sequence before handoff so reduction software can select it reproducibly.\n"]]]]],["23",["object",528,"object-prototype",[["path",["string","observatory/data-handoff.md"]],["markdown",["string","---\ntype: concept\ntitle: Data Handoff\ntags:\n  - data\n  - provenance\n  - handoff\nstatus: stable\n---\n# Data Handoff\n\n## Purpose\nA data handoff transfers an observing night from the control room to the person reducing or archiving it. The handoff should let another operator identify what happened without relying on memory or private messages.\n\n## Package contents\nPlace raw files in a read-only night directory named with the observing date and run identifier. Include the final schedule, night log, target list, calibration frames, instrument configuration, weather summary, and any alert messages. Generate a file manifest with relative paths, byte sizes, checksums, and acquisition timestamps. Separate successful, partial, and failed sequences, but retain failed files when they explain a gap or instrument problem.\n\n## Review and receipt\nThe observer reviews filenames, target coordinates, filters, exposure times, and detector temperature before announcing the package. Add a README that states known defects, missing calibrations, clock concerns, and the software version used at acquisition. The recipient verifies the checksum and confirms receipt in the handoff record. Corrections are made by adding a dated note or replacement file; do not rewrite raw data in place. Keep the original package and its receipt together in the archive.\n"]]]]],["24",["object",529,"object-prototype",[["path",["string","observatory/dome-shutdown.md"]],["markdown",["string","---\ntype: concept\ntitle: Dome Shutdown\ntags:\n  - dome\n  - shutdown\n  - safety\nstatus: stable\n---\n# Dome Shutdown\n\n## Purpose\nDome shutdown leaves the observatory dry, powered safely, and ready for the next crew. The duty astronomer starts it after the final exposure, or immediately when a weather gate requires closure.\n\n## Sequence\nStop acquisitions and wait for the camera to finish writing. Park the mount at its marked home position, return the instrument selector to its safe setting, and cap the telescope and finder. Remove temporary cables from walkways and secure the remaining loops so the dome cannot catch them. Close and latch the shutter, then switch off nonessential instrument power. Keep ventilation or environmental controls running when required by the camera or building plan; shutdown is not the same as turning off every breaker.\n\n## Verification\nWalk the room with a flashlight: check that no person or loose object remains in the rotation path, the floor is dry, emergency access is clear, and the rain sensor is unobstructed. Confirm the shutter indicator agrees with the physical latch and record the final humidity, temperature, mount state, and time. If any step fails, leave the affected system in its safest known state, call the site lead, and document the fault before leaving.\n"]]]]],["25",["object",530,"object-prototype",[["path",["string","observatory/equipment-inventory.md"]],["markdown",["string","---\ntype: concept\ntitle: Equipment Inventory\ntags:\n  - equipment\n  - inventory\n  - maintenance\nstatus: stable\n---\n# Equipment Inventory\n\n## Purpose\nThe equipment inventory is the current map of observatory hardware, consumables, and loaned accessories. It lets operators find a compatible component quickly and gives maintenance staff a traceable history when something fails.\n\n## Required fields\nGive every item a stable inventory identifier and record manufacturer, model, serial number, location, owner, condition, and last inspection date. For cameras and mounts, include firmware version and the cables or adapters required for normal operation. Mark safety-critical items such as ladders, extinguishers, and lifting straps with inspection due dates. A missing serial number is recorded as unknown rather than guessed from a label or purchase order.\n\n## Updates and checks\nUpdate the record when an item moves, is repaired, loaned, retired, or found damaged. Attach a short note and work-order reference to each change. At the start of each quarter, compare the list with a physical count and resolve duplicate identifiers. Quarantine unsafe equipment with a visible tag and remove it from the available pool. Before an observing run, the operator checks the requested setup against this inventory and reports substitutions in the night log.\n"]]]]],["26",["object",531,"object-prototype",[["path",["string","observatory/night-schedule.md"]],["markdown",["string","---\ntype: concept\ntitle: Night Schedule\ntags:\n  - scheduling\n  - observing\n  - operations\nstatus: stable\n---\n# Night Schedule\n\n## Purpose\nA night schedule turns competing science, maintenance, and outreach requests into an executable sequence. The duty astronomer owns the schedule after the afternoon handoff and may reorder it when conditions or instrument health change.\n\n## Planning\nReserve the first 30 minutes for startup, alignment, and a focus check. Group targets by sky position to limit dome rotation and unnecessary slews. Place time-critical observations, such as an exoplanet ingress or a transient follow-up, ahead of flexible deep fields. Add a ten-minute buffer after every instrument change and a longer weather buffer near the horizon. Each block names a target, filter or grating, exposure plan, cadence, estimated duration, and required calibration.\n\n## Handoff\nPublish the schedule before sunset in the observatory workspace and print one copy for the control room. During the night, mark each block complete, skipped, or interrupted with a short reason. A replacement block must state what it displaces. At dawn, attach the final schedule to the data handoff so analysts can distinguish planned gaps from instrument failures.\n"]]]]],["27",["object",532,"object-prototype",[["path",["string","observatory/spectral-imaging.md"]],["markdown",["string","---\ntype: concept\ntitle: Spectral Imaging\ntags:\n  - spectroscopy\n  - imaging\n  - filters\n  - analysis\nstatus: stable\n---\n# Spectral Imaging\n\n## Purpose\nSpectral imaging combines a spatial image with measurements through selected filters or a low-resolution disperser. At the observatory it is used to compare nebular structure, stellar colors, and emission-line regions with a consistent instrument setup.\n\n## Acquisition\nBefore the sequence, record the filter wheel position, grating or prism state, detector mode, focus, and target orientation. Take a short acquisition image, then center the target without changing the planned position angle. Observe filters in an order that limits airmass changes, and include a nearby standard star when calibrated fluxes are needed. Keep individual exposures below the detector's saturation limit; use repeated exposures rather than one very long frame so cosmic rays and tracking failures can be identified.\n\n## Reduction notes\nApply bias or dark correction, flat-field each filter separately, and correct bad pixels before alignment. For dispersed data, save the wavelength solution and arc exposure with the image set. Record atmospheric conditions and any filter changes in the sequence metadata. Do not compare raw counts between filters without accounting for exposure time, detector response, and standard-star calibration. Export a preview with scale, orientation, and units so a collaborator can interpret it without opening the control software.\n"]]]]],["28",["object",533,"object-prototype",[["path",["string","observatory/telescope-alignment.md"]],["markdown",["string","---\ntype: concept\ntitle: Telescope Alignment\ntags:\n  - telescope\n  - alignment\n  - optics\nstatus: stable\n---\n# Telescope Alignment\n\n## Purpose\nTelescope alignment keeps the small observatory's pointing model trustworthy across the night. The operator should align after a mount restart, a pier intervention, or any move that could disturb the optical tube. A good alignment reduces reacquisition time and prevents a target from drifting out of a narrow instrument field.\n\n## Procedure\nLevel the pier check, confirm the tube and finder are firmly seated, and focus on a bright star near the night's first target. Center that star with the mount controls, then record two additional stars separated in azimuth and altitude. Save the resulting model under the date and mount serial number rather than overwriting yesterday's file. Verify the model on a fourth star: slew to it, let tracking settle for two minutes, and measure the offset in arcseconds. If the offset exceeds 30 arcseconds, repeat the alignment and inspect balance, time settings, and cable drag before observing.\n\n## Record\nLog the operator, alignment stars, residual offset, and model filename in the night log. Keep failed models for troubleshooting, but mark them inactive.\n"]]]]],["29",["object",534,"object-prototype",[["path",["string","observatory/transient-alerts.md"]],["markdown",["string","---\ntype: concept\ntitle: Transient Alerts\ntags:\n  - transients\n  - alerts\n  - follow-up\nstatus: draft\n---\n# Transient Alerts\n\n## Purpose\nA transient alert is a time-sensitive notice that a variable or newly appearing object may merit observation. The observatory accepts alerts from approved networks and from the on-call scientist, then records enough context to make a quick, defensible decision.\n\n## Triage\nThe on-call astronomer checks the alert timestamp, coordinates, uncertainty region, brightness estimate, visibility, and scientific priority. Reject duplicates, stale positions, targets below the local horizon, and requests that conflict with a protected safety hold. If the target is observable, compare its required cadence and filters with the current schedule. The astronomer may interrupt a flexible block, but must document whose time was displaced and why.\n\n## Response\nCreate an observation block with the alert identifier, ephemeris source, coordinate epoch, exposure sequence, and deadline. Use a short acquisition exposure before committing to a long series. Notify the instrument operator and record whether the target was detected, missed, or clouded out. Preserve the original alert alongside any revised coordinates; never silently edit incoming metadata. Within one day, send the requester a concise result with timestamps, limiting magnitude or measured brightness, and links to the raw data and observing log.\n"]]]]],["30",["object",535,"object-prototype",[["path",["string","observatory/visitor-nights.md"]],["markdown",["string","---\ntype: concept\ntitle: Visitor Nights\ntags:\n  - outreach\n  - visitors\n  - safety\nstatus: stable\n---\n# Visitor Nights\n\n## Purpose\nA visitor night gives the public a safe view of observing work without turning the control room into an uncontrolled workspace. The outreach lead coordinates the group, while the duty astronomer retains authority over equipment and weather decisions.\n\n## Before arrival\nLimit groups to the posted room capacity and collect accessibility needs in advance. Prepare a short target list with bright, forgiving objects that remain visible if the schedule shifts. Test the demonstration eyepiece, red lights, handrails, and restroom route before sunset. Keep visitors away from the pier, power strips, moving dome, and exposed cables; do not allow food, drink, or unescorted access in the equipment area.\n\n## During the visit\nGive a five-minute safety briefing, including the location of exits and the rule to stop when staff says stop. Use red light and preserve dark adaptation; explain why phone screens stay covered. One staff member supervises the viewing area while another operates the mount. If wind, rain, or equipment motion makes the visit unsafe, move indoors to a prepared image session. After departure, count loaned items, close doors, and note attendance, incidents, and useful feedback in the outreach log.\n"]]]]],["31",["object",536,"object-prototype",[["path",["string","observatory/weather-gates.md"]],["markdown",["string","---\ntype: concept\ntitle: Weather Gates\ntags:\n  - weather\n  - safety\n  - thresholds\nstatus: stable\n---\n# Weather Gates\n\n## Purpose\nWeather gates define when the dome may open, remain open, or close. They protect people and equipment while giving the duty astronomer predictable decisions instead of relying on intuition during a changing night.\n\n## Opening gate\nOpen only when the forecast and local sensors agree on a dry, stable window. Require no precipitation, relative humidity below 80 percent, wind below 25 km/h, and a temperature-dew-point spread above 3 °C. Check the all-sky camera for cloud movement and inspect the rain sensor for debris. A clear forecast never overrides a wet sensor or a rapidly approaching storm cell.\n\n## Monitoring and closure\nSample sensors every minute and display both current values and ten-minute trends. Enter a warning state at 75 percent humidity, 20 km/h wind, or a 4 °C dew-point spread; pause new exposures and prepare to close. Close the dome immediately for detected rain, lightning within 15 km, wind above 35 km/h, or a failing sensor that prevents safe assessment. Record the trigger, timestamp, and recovery decision. Reopening requires ten dry minutes and a fresh walk-around inspection.\n"]]]]],["32",["object",537,"object-prototype",[["path",["string","railway/brake-test.md"]],["markdown",["string","---\ntype: concept\ntitle: Brake Test\ndescription: A repeatable train-brake check used before departure, after changes, and whenever brake integrity is uncertain.\ntags:\n  - brakes\n  - departure\n  - rollingstock\nstatus: stable\n---\n\n# Brake Test\n\n## Set up\nConfirm the consist list, brake system, cab controls, and required test type with the driver or train preparer. Apply handbrakes or other stationary protection before connecting or separating vehicles. Walk the train with a radio and identify the last vehicle. Check that hoses are coupled, cocks are correctly positioned, reservoirs are charged, and visible brake indicators agree with the intended state. Keep clear of couplers and moving equipment.\n\n## Test and verify\nThe driver applies and releases the train brake using the prescribed sequence. The tester observes the response at the leading, middle, and last vehicle or at the locations specified for that stock. Report a delayed application, incomplete release, audible leak, isolated vehicle, or conflicting indicator immediately. Repeat a test only after the cause and protection are understood; a repeated pass does not erase an unexplained failure.\n\n## Release the train\nRecord test type, time, consist, tester, result, and any restriction. The driver signs acceptance only when the full train is protected by the tested brake system. If the result is incomplete, hold the departure and call control for technical advice.\n"]]]]],["33",["object",538,"object-prototype",[["path",["string","railway/depot-closeout.md"]],["markdown",["string","---\ntype: concept\ntitle: Depot Closeout\ndescription: An end-of-shift routine that leaves a regional railway depot safe, accounted for, and ready for the next crew.\ntags:\n  - depot\n  - closeout\n  - maintenance\nstatus: stable\n---\n\n# Depot Closeout\n\n## Secure the site\nThe duty lead checks that every vehicle is on its assigned road, stationary protection is applied, and movements are complete. Confirm that pits, walkways, fueling points, lifting equipment, and isolation boundaries are clear. Remove tools and temporary barriers only when the responsible maintainer releases them. Close doors and gates, inspect lighting and alarms, and report spills, leaks, or damaged fencing before leaving the area.\n\n## Reconcile the work\nCompare the vehicle position board with the actual consist and record mileage, fuel or charge state, defects, isolation tags, and deferred work. Each open defect needs an owner, safe operating limit, and next action; “watch” is not a substitute for a decision. Attach test sheets and parts information to the correct vehicle record. Escalate anything that could affect the first diagram to control and the incoming maintenance lead.\n\n## Hand over\nBrief the next shift on vehicles unavailable, equipment isolated, unusual noises, contractors on site, and inspections due. Read back critical restrictions, sign the closeout log, and keep the radio available until the handoff is accepted.\n"]]]]],["34",["object",539,"object-prototype",[["path",["string","railway/incident-radio.md"]],["markdown",["string","---\ntype: concept\ntitle: Incident Radio\ndescription: A short radio protocol for reporting operational incidents without losing the essential facts.\ntags:\n  - radio\n  - incidents\n  - control\nstatus: stable\n---\n\n# Incident Radio\n\n## Make the first call\nUse the designated emergency channel when there is immediate danger; otherwise use the operating channel. Start with the recipient, your role and location, then say “emergency” or “priority” and wait for acknowledgment when required. State what happened, which line or platform is affected, whether anyone is injured, and whether a train or vehicle is moving. If you do not know a detail, say so instead of guessing.\n\n## Keep instructions clear\nThe signaller or control lead repeats the protection instruction. Read it back word for word, including signal numbers, line limits, and any prohibition on movement. Use plain verbs such as stop, protect, evacuate, or hold. Do not transmit while another person is giving a protection instruction. If the channel is busy, send the minimum message needed to stop harm and then move to the assigned channel.\n\n## Update and close\nGive updates when the situation, access, or casualty status changes. Identify each new message with the incident reference and current location. Control closes the event only after the responsible lead confirms that people are safe, the route is protected or released, and the written incident record is complete.\n"]]]]],["35",["object",540,"object-prototype",[["path",["string","railway/passenger-recovery.md"]],["markdown",["string","---\ntype: concept\ntitle: Passenger Recovery\ndescription: A practical playbook for restoring passenger journeys after a regional railway disruption.\ntags:\n  - passengers\n  - disruption\n  - recovery\nstatus: stable\n---\n\n# Passenger Recovery\n\n## Stabilize the service\nControl first establishes what is safe: affected trains, blocked routes, available platforms, crew locations, and the next credible movement. Avoid promising a time before the signaller and operating lead agree on the plan. Station teams provide water, accessibility support, and a calm explanation of what is known. Record passengers needing an assisted transfer, medical help, or a connection that cannot wait.\n\n## Offer usable alternatives\nChoose options that can actually be staffed and dispatched: a short turnback, an accepting route, a replacement bus, or an endorsed ticket arrangement. Give passengers the action, destination, departure point, and next update time. Keep the message consistent across announcements, displays, web updates, and staff briefings. Do not send people onto an alternative route until capacity, accessibility, and ticket acceptance are confirmed.\n\n## Recover and learn\nWhen trains resume, prioritize stranded passengers and publish the revised stopping pattern. Tell station teams when normal service is genuinely restored. Close the response with a count of cancellations, assisted passengers, substitute vehicles, and unresolved claims; capture passenger reports while details are fresh.\n"]]]]],["36",["object",541,"object-prototype",[["path",["string","railway/platform-dispatch.md"]],["markdown",["string","---\ntype: concept\ntitle: Platform Dispatch\ndescription: A disciplined departure check that aligns the dispatcher, driver, and platform before a train leaves.\ntags:\n  - dispatch\n  - platform\n  - passenger\nstatus: stable\n---\n\n# Platform Dispatch\n\n## Prepare the departure\nThe dispatcher checks the working timetable, platform indicator, booked calling points, and any active operating notice. Confirm that the driver has the correct service identity and departure time. Scan the platform from the rear of the train toward the stopping point: passengers must be clear of doors, ramps must be withdrawn, and no object may project into the train path. Resolve a crowd, unattended item, or door fault before giving a signal.\n\n## Give and monitor the signal\nMake the departure indication using the agreed hand signal, lamp, or dispatch equipment while maintaining a clear view of the train. The driver acknowledges using the prescribed method. Watch the first vehicle roll away and check that every door is closed, the platform edge is clear, and no person is running alongside. If visibility is lost, the driver reports an abnormal condition, or a door reopens, instruct the driver to stop and inform the signaller.\n\n## Record exceptions\nLog late dispatch, assistance provided, door defects, and any stop instruction. Pass a concise update to control so the next station knows what to expect. Never trade a safety check for timetable recovery.\n"]]]]],["37",["object",542,"object-prototype",[["path",["string","railway/signal-inspection.md"]],["markdown",["string","---\ntype: concept\ntitle: Signal Inspection\ndescription: A field check for confirming that signals display, communicate, and recover as expected.\ntags:\n  - signals\n  - inspection\n  - safety\nstatus: stable\n---\n\n# Signal Inspection\n\n## Before approaching\nReview the work notice, signal identification, control area, and protection granted by the signaller. Carry a radio, identification light, and the latest defect sheet. Do not enter the track or signal structure until the signaller repeats the protection and the inspector reads it back. Treat an unexpected aspect, dark lamp, or conflicting instruction as a failure rather than a nuisance.\n\n## Field checks\nFrom the normal viewing distance, verify the signal head, route indicator, sighting line, and surrounding obstruction. Compare the displayed aspect with the signaller’s stated route; do not test by requesting a movement unless the work notice authorizes it. Check lenses for damage, condensation, dirt, or a loose hood. At the equipment case, look for water ingress, heat marks, exposed wiring, and an unlocked door. Record signal ID, aspect, time, weather, and exact symptom. Photograph only when permitted by the work instruction.\n\n## Closeout\nReport defects immediately over the designated channel, then repeat the restriction or protection that follows. Leave covers secured and tools counted. The signaller closes the inspection only after receiving the written finding and confirming whether the signal is serviceable, restricted, or out of use.\n"]]]]],["38",["object",543,"object-prototype",[["path",["string","railway/switch-freeze-response.md"]],["markdown",["string","---\ntype: concept\ntitle: Switch Freeze Response\ndescription: A response pattern for points that cannot move because of ice, snow, or compacted debris.\ntags:\n  - points\n  - winter\n  - disruption\nstatus: stable\n---\n\n# Switch Freeze Response\n\n## Protect the movement\nWhen points fail to prove or move in freezing conditions, stop movements over the affected route. Tell the signaller the point number, last known position, attempted command, and whether a vehicle is fouling the clearance. The signaller protects the route and confirms the restriction. Do not force a switch with a locomotive, kick snow into the mechanism, or rely on an unverified indication.\n\n## Clear and assess\nA qualified points technician or authorized response crew inspects the switch under the granted protection. Remove loose snow and ice with approved tools, keeping hands clear of the stretcher and drive rods. Check the stock rail, stretcher, detection contacts, and drainage for compacted material or standing water. If heat tracing is fitted, confirm its status through the approved control rather than opening electrical equipment in wet conditions. Attempt a movement only after the technician and signaller agree that the route is protected.\n\n## Restore service\nRun the prescribed proving cycle and record the final position. If detection remains unreliable, impose the published temporary speed or hand-signalling arrangement. Notify control, affected crews, and passenger information staff with the next review time.\n"]]]]],["39",["object",544,"object-prototype",[["path",["string","railway/timetable-change.md"]],["markdown",["string","---\ntype: concept\ntitle: Timetable Change\ndescription: A controlled method for turning an amended working timetable into an understood operating plan.\ntags:\n  - timetable\n  - planning\n  - communications\nstatus: draft\n---\n\n# Timetable Change\n\n## Validate the change\nControl compares the amended timetable with the current service plan, traincrew diagrams, rollingstock availability, engineering possessions, and station staffing. Confirm the effective date, service identifiers, public calling pattern, pathing constraints, and any temporary speed restrictions. A change is not operationally usable until a named owner confirms the source and version.\n\n## Brief the railway\nPublish one controlled notice rather than sending conflicting extracts. Brief signallers, drivers, guards, dispatchers, station teams, maintenance leads, and passenger-information staff using the same service identity and times. Ask each role to repeat the part that affects its work: crossing arrangements, platform, turnback, connections, or last-train commitment. Mark superseded copies and remove them from shared boards and cab packs.\n\n## Monitor first use\nFor the first affected operating period, control watches departures, platform conflicts, crew readiness, and passenger messages. Log deviations against the amended plan, not against an obsolete schedule. At the review point, retain the approved notice, record unresolved impacts, and either confirm the change or issue a corrected version with a new effective time.\n"]]]]],["40",["object",545,"object-prototype",[["path",["string","railway/track-walk.md"]],["markdown",["string","---\ntype: concept\ntitle: Track Walk\ndescription: A protected walking inspection for finding defects before they become a train movement hazard.\ntags:\n  - track\n  - inspection\n  - patrol\nstatus: draft\n---\n\n# Track Walk\n\n## Plan and protect\nThe patrol leader checks the work order, limits, weather, nearby movements, and safe access point. Obtain formal protection from the signaller and read back the exact track section, line, and release condition. Carry a charged radio, high-visibility clothing, torch, measuring tools, and a way to mark location. Enter only after the signaller confirms that protection is active; keep the escape route visible throughout the walk.\n\n## Inspect systematically\nWalk in one direction and call out each reference point, such as a kilometre post, joint, crossing, or bridge. Look for rail cracks, displaced fasteners, broken sleepers, ballast washout, standing water, vegetation, trespass damage, and objects fouling the gauge. Compare unusual geometry with the permitted inspection limits rather than judging by appearance. Photograph or mark a defect only when the work instruction allows it, and never leave a marker that could be mistaken for railway equipment.\n\n## Report and hand back\nGive control the location, defect, immediate risk, and recommended restriction in that order. Stop the patrol if weather, visibility, or radio coverage changes. Count tools, clear the line, and obtain explicit release confirmation before normal movements resume.\n"]]]]],["41",["object",546,"object-prototype",[["path",["string","railway/yard-handoff.md"]],["markdown",["string","---\ntype: concept\ntitle: Yard Handoff\ndescription: A concise transfer routine for making shunting work visible and safe between crews.\ntags:\n  - yard\n  - handoff\n  - safety\nstatus: stable\n---\n\n# Yard Handoff\n\n## Purpose\nA yard handoff transfers responsibility, not just a list of tasks. The outgoing shunter briefs the incoming operator beside the working plan before either person moves a vehicle. Confirm the current track, locomotive, consist, brake status, protection limits, and any vehicles that must not be coupled. State which routes are occupied, which points are clipped or locked, and whether a signal, radio, or crossing fault changes the plan.\n\n## Handoff sequence\nRead back the movement order and repeat the next intended move in plain language. Walk to the control board or yard diagram and mark completed, active, and deferred work. The incoming operator checks the radio channel, handbrake count, and visible clearance at the first route. If information is missing, stop the transfer and call the yard controller; never infer that an old note still applies.\n\n## Record\nLog the time, names, locomotive number, outstanding hazards, and agreed next move. The outgoing operator remains available until the incoming operator confirms acceptance.\n"]]]]]]]],["prepared",["array",547,42,[["0",["object",548,"object-prototype",[["conformance",["string","strict"]],["identity",["object",549,"object-prototype",[["path",["string","ceramics/bisque-inspection.md"]],["documentId",["string","ceramics/bisque-inspection"]]]]],["type",["string","concept"]],["metadata",["object",550,"object-prototype",[["title",["string","Bisque Inspection"]],["description",["string","Checks for dryness, strength, and surface problems before bisque-fired ware enters the glaze area."]],["tags",["array",551,3,[["0",["string","bisque"]],["1",["string","inspection"]],["2",["string","glaze"]]]]],["sourceText",["string",""]]]]],["facets",["object",552,"object-prototype",[["status",["object",553,"object-prototype",[["classified",["boolean",true]],["value",["string","stable"]]]]],["trust",["object",554,"object-prototype",[["classified",["boolean",true]],["value",["string","unverified"]]]]],["staleness",["object",555,"object-prototype",[["classified",["boolean",true]]]]]]]],["sections",["array",556,4,[["0",["object",557,"object-prototype",[["id",["string","ceramics/bisque-inspection#bisque-inspection"]],["headingPath",["string","Bisque Inspection"]],["text",["string",""]],["startLine",["number","4028000000000000"]],["endLine",["number","4028000000000000"]]]]],["1",["object",558,"object-prototype",[["id",["string","ceramics/bisque-inspection#bisque-inspection-check-surfaces"]],["headingPath",["string","Bisque Inspection > Check Surfaces"]],["text",["string","Handle each piece over a padded table and inspect the foot, rim, attachments, and inside corners. Look for cracks, sharp flakes, kiln wash stuck to the base, loose shelves, pinholes, and pieces fused to neighbors. Run a clean fingertip around the rim; it should not catch on a burr. Brush away loose crumbs outdoors or with the approved damp method, not by blowing."]],["startLine",["number","402c000000000000"]],["endLine",["number","4030000000000000"]]]]],["2",["object",559,"object-prototype",[["id",["string","ceramics/bisque-inspection#bisque-inspection-sort-for-glaze"]],["headingPath",["string","Bisque Inspection > Sort for Glaze"]],["text",["string","Separate sound ware from pieces needing repair, testing, or disposal. Confirm that the clay body and firing temperature match the glaze plan. Wipe the foot with a slightly damp sponge and allow it to dry before applying glaze. If a piece is unusually dark, soft, smoky, or much heavier than expected, hold it for the firing lead because it may not have been fully fired or cooled correctly."]],["startLine",["number","4032000000000000"]],["endLine",["number","4034000000000000"]]]]],["3",["object",560,"object-prototype",[["id",["string","ceramics/bisque-inspection#bisque-inspection-record-problems"]],["headingPath",["string","Bisque Inspection > Record Problems"]],["text",["string","Mark the maker's work clearly and photograph defects that are easy to miss. Record the piece ID, defect, shelf position, and recommended action on the inspection sheet. Never hide a crack beneath glaze or send questionable ware into a shared firing without the maker's informed decision and lead approval."]],["startLine",["number","4036000000000000"]],["endLine",["number","4038000000000000"]]]]]]]],["diagnostics",["array",561,0,[]]],["document",["object",562,"object-prototype",[["id",["string","ceramics/bisque-inspection"]],["type",["string","concept"]],["title",["string","Bisque Inspection"]],["tags",["array",563,3,[["0",["string","bisque"]],["1",["string","inspection"]],["2",["string","glaze"]]]]],["sources",["array",564,0,[]]],["verified",["array",565,0,[]]],["body",["string","\n# Bisque Inspection\n\n## Check Surfaces\n\nHandle each piece over a padded table and inspect the foot, rim, attachments, and inside corners. Look for cracks, sharp flakes, kiln wash stuck to the base, loose shelves, pinholes, and pieces fused to neighbors. Run a clean fingertip around the rim; it should not catch on a burr. Brush away loose crumbs outdoors or with the approved damp method, not by blowing.\n\n## Sort for Glaze\n\nSeparate sound ware from pieces needing repair, testing, or disposal. Confirm that the clay body and firing temperature match the glaze plan. Wipe the foot with a slightly damp sponge and allow it to dry before applying glaze. If a piece is unusually dark, soft, smoky, or much heavier than expected, hold it for the firing lead because it may not have been fully fired or cooled correctly.\n\n## Record Problems\n\nMark the maker's work clearly and photograph defects that are easy to miss. Record the piece ID, defect, shelf position, and recommended action on the inspection sheet. Never hide a crack beneath glaze or send questionable ware into a shared firing without the maker's informed decision and lead approval.\n"]],["extensions",["object",566,"object-prototype",[]]],["description",["string","Checks for dryness, strength, and surface problems before bisque-fired ware enters the glaze area."]],["status",["string","stable"]]]]]]]],["1",["object",567,"object-prototype",[["conformance",["string","strict"]],["identity",["object",568,"object-prototype",[["path",["string","ceramics/clay-reclaim.md"]],["documentId",["string","ceramics/clay-reclaim"]]]]],["type",["string","concept"]],["metadata",["object",569,"object-prototype",[["title",["string","Clay Reclaim"]],["description",["string","A practical cycle for sorting, rehydrating, and returning usable clay to studio stock."]],["tags",["array",570,3,[["0",["string","clay"]],["1",["string","reclaim"]],["2",["string","studio"]]]]],["sourceText",["string",""]]]]],["facets",["object",571,"object-prototype",[["status",["object",572,"object-prototype",[["classified",["boolean",true]],["value",["string","stable"]]]]],["trust",["object",573,"object-prototype",[["classified",["boolean",true]],["value",["string","unverified"]]]]],["staleness",["object",574,"object-prototype",[["classified",["boolean",true]]]]]]]],["sections",["array",575,4,[["0",["object",576,"object-prototype",[["id",["string","ceramics/clay-reclaim#clay-reclaim"]],["headingPath",["string","Clay Reclaim"]],["text",["string",""]],["startLine",["number","4028000000000000"]],["endLine",["number","4028000000000000"]]]]],["1",["object",577,"object-prototype",[["id",["string","ceramics/clay-reclaim#clay-reclaim-sort-before-slaking"]],["headingPath",["string","Clay Reclaim > Sort Before Slaking"]],["text",["string","Collect trimmings, failed pieces, and throwing scraps in the labeled reclaim buckets. Remove plaster, bisque, wood, glaze chips, and plastic before adding anything. Keep white, buff, red, and dark bodies separate unless the studio manager has approved a mixed batch. Clay that has touched glaze should go into the waste stream because fired colorants can contaminate new work."]],["startLine",["number","402c000000000000"]],["endLine",["number","4030000000000000"]]]]],["2",["object",578,"object-prototype",[["id",["string","ceramics/clay-reclaim#clay-reclaim-reclaim-workflow"]],["headingPath",["string","Clay Reclaim > Reclaim Workflow"]],["text",["string","Break dry scraps into pieces smaller than a fist and cover them with water. Let the bucket stand until the pieces are soft throughout, then pour off excess water and blend the slurry with the reclaim mixer. Spread the mixture on plaster bats or a canvas board in a thin layer. Turn it when the surface firms, wedge it when it reaches a workable consistency, and return it to an airtight bag."]],["startLine",["number","4032000000000000"]],["endLine",["number","4034000000000000"]]]]],["3",["object",579,"object-prototype",[["id",["string","ceramics/clay-reclaim#clay-reclaim-drying-and-return"]],["headingPath",["string","Clay Reclaim > Drying and Return"]],["text",["string","Mark each bag with body type and date. Test a small lump before restocking; it should wedge smoothly without sour odor, hard cores, or gritty inclusions. Quarantine questionable batches for review rather than mixing them into clean stock."]],["startLine",["number","4036000000000000"]],["endLine",["number","4038000000000000"]]]]]]]],["diagnostics",["array",580,0,[]]],["document",["object",581,"object-prototype",[["id",["string","ceramics/clay-reclaim"]],["type",["string","concept"]],["title",["string","Clay Reclaim"]],["tags",["array",582,3,[["0",["string","clay"]],["1",["string","reclaim"]],["2",["string","studio"]]]]],["sources",["array",583,0,[]]],["verified",["array",584,0,[]]],["body",["string","\n# Clay Reclaim\n\n## Sort Before Slaking\n\nCollect trimmings, failed pieces, and throwing scraps in the labeled reclaim buckets. Remove plaster, bisque, wood, glaze chips, and plastic before adding anything. Keep white, buff, red, and dark bodies separate unless the studio manager has approved a mixed batch. Clay that has touched glaze should go into the waste stream because fired colorants can contaminate new work.\n\n## Reclaim Workflow\n\nBreak dry scraps into pieces smaller than a fist and cover them with water. Let the bucket stand until the pieces are soft throughout, then pour off excess water and blend the slurry with the reclaim mixer. Spread the mixture on plaster bats or a canvas board in a thin layer. Turn it when the surface firms, wedge it when it reaches a workable consistency, and return it to an airtight bag.\n\n## Drying and Return\n\nMark each bag with body type and date. Test a small lump before restocking; it should wedge smoothly without sour odor, hard cores, or gritty inclusions. Quarantine questionable batches for review rather than mixing them into clean stock.\n"]],["extensions",["object",585,"object-prototype",[]]],["description",["string","A practical cycle for sorting, rehydrating, and returning usable clay to studio stock."]],["status",["string","stable"]]]]]]]],["2",["object",586,"object-prototype",[["conformance",["string","strict"]],["identity",["object",587,"object-prototype",[["path",["string","ceramics/dust-control.md"]],["documentId",["string","ceramics/dust-control"]]]]],["type",["string","concept"]],["metadata",["object",588,"object-prototype",[["title",["string","Dust Control"]],["description",["string","Studio habits that prevent dry ceramic particles from becoming airborne or entering drains."]],["tags",["array",589,3,[["0",["string","dust"]],["1",["string","safety"]],["2",["string","cleanup"]]]]],["sourceText",["string",""]]]]],["facets",["object",590,"object-prototype",[["status",["object",591,"object-prototype",[["classified",["boolean",true]],["value",["string","stable"]]]]],["trust",["object",592,"object-prototype",[["classified",["boolean",true]],["value",["string","unverified"]]]]],["staleness",["object",593,"object-prototype",[["classified",["boolean",true]]]]]]]],["sections",["array",594,4,[["0",["object",595,"object-prototype",[["id",["string","ceramics/dust-control#dust-control"]],["headingPath",["string","Dust Control"]],["text",["string",""]],["startLine",["number","4028000000000000"]],["endLine",["number","4028000000000000"]]]]],["1",["object",596,"object-prototype",[["id",["string","ceramics/dust-control#dust-control-prevent-dust"]],["headingPath",["string","Dust Control > Prevent Dust"]],["text",["string","Keep powdered clay, glaze, and dry sanding to the designated area, with local ventilation running. Wear the respiratory protection required by the studio risk assessment when handling powders. Avoid shaking dusty aprons, sweeping dry floors, using compressed air, or blowing across shelves. Store powders closed and clean spills before they are tracked through the room."]],["startLine",["number","402c000000000000"]],["endLine",["number","4030000000000000"]]]]],["2",["object",597,"object-prototype",[["id",["string","ceramics/dust-control#dust-control-clean-without-spreading"]],["headingPath",["string","Dust Control > Clean Without Spreading"]],["text",["string","Use a damp sponge, wet mop, or studio-approved HEPA vacuum for settled material. Work from clean surfaces toward the dirtiest area, and rinse cleaning water into the solids-collection system rather than a regular drain. Clean shelves and wheel splash pans before residue dries. Wash hands and forearms after cleanup, even when gloves were worn, and keep food and drinks outside the workroom."]],["startLine",["number","4032000000000000"]],["endLine",["number","4034000000000000"]]]]],["3",["object",598,"object-prototype",[["id",["string","ceramics/dust-control#dust-control-respond-to-exposure"]],["headingPath",["string","Dust Control > Respond to Exposure"]],["text",["string","Stop the activity if a cloud forms or ventilation fails. Move people away from the source, close the container if safe, and notify the studio lead. Anyone with breathing irritation should leave the area and follow the posted first-aid or medical guidance. Record the material, location, and cleanup steps so the next shift can correct the cause."]],["startLine",["number","4036000000000000"]],["endLine",["number","4038000000000000"]]]]]]]],["diagnostics",["array",599,0,[]]],["document",["object",600,"object-prototype",[["id",["string","ceramics/dust-control"]],["type",["string","concept"]],["title",["string","Dust Control"]],["tags",["array",601,3,[["0",["string","dust"]],["1",["string","safety"]],["2",["string","cleanup"]]]]],["sources",["array",602,0,[]]],["verified",["array",603,0,[]]],["body",["string","\n# Dust Control\n\n## Prevent Dust\n\nKeep powdered clay, glaze, and dry sanding to the designated area, with local ventilation running. Wear the respiratory protection required by the studio risk assessment when handling powders. Avoid shaking dusty aprons, sweeping dry floors, using compressed air, or blowing across shelves. Store powders closed and clean spills before they are tracked through the room.\n\n## Clean Without Spreading\n\nUse a damp sponge, wet mop, or studio-approved HEPA vacuum for settled material. Work from clean surfaces toward the dirtiest area, and rinse cleaning water into the solids-collection system rather than a regular drain. Clean shelves and wheel splash pans before residue dries. Wash hands and forearms after cleanup, even when gloves were worn, and keep food and drinks outside the workroom.\n\n## Respond to Exposure\n\nStop the activity if a cloud forms or ventilation fails. Move people away from the source, close the container if safe, and notify the studio lead. Anyone with breathing irritation should leave the area and follow the posted first-aid or medical guidance. Record the material, location, and cleanup steps so the next shift can correct the cause.\n"]],["extensions",["object",604,"object-prototype",[]]],["description",["string","Studio habits that prevent dry ceramic particles from becoming airborne or entering drains."]],["status",["string","stable"]]]]]]]],["3",["object",605,"object-prototype",[["conformance",["string","strict"]],["identity",["object",606,"object-prototype",[["path",["string","ceramics/exhibition-packing.md"]],["documentId",["string","ceramics/exhibition-packing"]]]]],["type",["string","concept"]],["metadata",["object",607,"object-prototype",[["title",["string","Exhibition Packing"]],["description",["string","A protective packing method for moving finished ceramic work from studio shelves to an exhibition venue."]],["tags",["array",608,3,[["0",["string","exhibition"]],["1",["string","packing"]],["2",["string","transport"]]]]],["sourceText",["string",""]]]]],["facets",["object",609,"object-prototype",[["status",["object",610,"object-prototype",[["classified",["boolean",true]],["value",["string","stable"]]]]],["trust",["object",611,"object-prototype",[["classified",["boolean",true]],["value",["string","unverified"]]]]],["staleness",["object",612,"object-prototype",[["classified",["boolean",true]]]]]]]],["sections",["array",613,4,[["0",["object",614,"object-prototype",[["id",["string","ceramics/exhibition-packing#exhibition-packing"]],["headingPath",["string","Exhibition Packing"]],["text",["string",""]],["startLine",["number","4028000000000000"]],["endLine",["number","4028000000000000"]]]]],["1",["object",615,"object-prototype",[["id",["string","ceramics/exhibition-packing#exhibition-packing-plan-the-package"]],["headingPath",["string","Exhibition Packing > Plan the Package"]],["text",["string","Measure the work, choose a rigid box with room for cushioning, and gather clean paper, foam, tape, labels, and a packing list. Photograph the piece before wrapping, including its foot and any existing flaw. Do not use loose packing peanuts or materials that shed dust. For a group shipment, assign each package an ID that matches the inventory and installation plan."]],["startLine",["number","402c000000000000"]],["endLine",["number","4030000000000000"]]]]],["2",["object",616,"object-prototype",[["id",["string","ceramics/exhibition-packing#exhibition-packing-wrap-and-cushion"]],["headingPath",["string","Exhibition Packing > Wrap and Cushion"]],["text",["string","Wrap handles, rims, and protrusions separately with soft tissue or paper before adding a larger layer. Surround the object with resilient cushioning on every side, keeping pressure off delicate edges. Place it in the center of the box so it cannot touch the walls. Close the box temporarily and perform a gentle movement test; if the piece shifts, reopen it and add fitted support rather than tighter pressure."]],["startLine",["number","4032000000000000"]],["endLine",["number","4034000000000000"]]]]],["3",["object",617,"object-prototype",[["id",["string","ceramics/exhibition-packing#exhibition-packing-label-and-handoff"]],["headingPath",["string","Exhibition Packing > Label and Handoff"]],["text",["string","Seal the package with tape that can be removed without contacting the artwork. Mark orientation, fragile handling, sender, recipient, package ID, and unpacking instructions. Keep the inventory, insurance value, and tracking number with the shipment record, not loose inside the box. Photograph the closed package at handoff and obtain a named receiver's confirmation."]],["startLine",["number","4036000000000000"]],["endLine",["number","4038000000000000"]]]]]]]],["diagnostics",["array",618,0,[]]],["document",["object",619,"object-prototype",[["id",["string","ceramics/exhibition-packing"]],["type",["string","concept"]],["title",["string","Exhibition Packing"]],["tags",["array",620,3,[["0",["string","exhibition"]],["1",["string","packing"]],["2",["string","transport"]]]]],["sources",["array",621,0,[]]],["verified",["array",622,0,[]]],["body",["string","\n# Exhibition Packing\n\n## Plan the Package\n\nMeasure the work, choose a rigid box with room for cushioning, and gather clean paper, foam, tape, labels, and a packing list. Photograph the piece before wrapping, including its foot and any existing flaw. Do not use loose packing peanuts or materials that shed dust. For a group shipment, assign each package an ID that matches the inventory and installation plan.\n\n## Wrap and Cushion\n\nWrap handles, rims, and protrusions separately with soft tissue or paper before adding a larger layer. Surround the object with resilient cushioning on every side, keeping pressure off delicate edges. Place it in the center of the box so it cannot touch the walls. Close the box temporarily and perform a gentle movement test; if the piece shifts, reopen it and add fitted support rather than tighter pressure.\n\n## Label and Handoff\n\nSeal the package with tape that can be removed without contacting the artwork. Mark orientation, fragile handling, sender, recipient, package ID, and unpacking instructions. Keep the inventory, insurance value, and tracking number with the shipment record, not loose inside the box. Photograph the closed package at handoff and obtain a named receiver's confirmation.\n"]],["extensions",["object",623,"object-prototype",[]]],["description",["string","A protective packing method for moving finished ceramic work from studio shelves to an exhibition venue."]],["status",["string","stable"]]]]]]]],["4",["object",624,"object-prototype",[["conformance",["string","strict"]],["identity",["object",625,"object-prototype",[["path",["string","ceramics/firing-schedule.md"]],["documentId",["string","ceramics/firing-schedule"]]]]],["type",["string","concept"]],["metadata",["object",626,"object-prototype",[["title",["string","Firing Schedule"]],["description",["string","A queueing practice for assigning community-studio work to safe bisque and glaze firing windows."]],["tags",["array",627,3,[["0",["string","firing"]],["1",["string","scheduling"]],["2",["string","kiln"]]]]],["sourceText",["string",""]]]]],["facets",["object",628,"object-prototype",[["status",["object",629,"object-prototype",[["classified",["boolean",true]],["value",["string","draft"]]]]],["trust",["object",630,"object-prototype",[["classified",["boolean",true]],["value",["string","unverified"]]]]],["staleness",["object",631,"object-prototype",[["classified",["boolean",true]]]]]]]],["sections",["array",632,4,[["0",["object",633,"object-prototype",[["id",["string","ceramics/firing-schedule#firing-schedule"]],["headingPath",["string","Firing Schedule"]],["text",["string",""]],["startLine",["number","4028000000000000"]],["endLine",["number","4028000000000000"]]]]],["1",["object",634,"object-prototype",[["id",["string","ceramics/firing-schedule#firing-schedule-build-the-queue"]],["headingPath",["string","Firing Schedule > Build the Queue"]],["text",["string","Submit work by the studio cutoff with the maker's name, clay body, firing type, target cone, piece count, and contact details. Confirm that each piece is bone dry, identified on its underside, and within the posted size and weight limits. Group compatible work by program; do not combine loads merely to fill empty space when temperatures or glaze risks differ."]],["startLine",["number","402c000000000000"]],["endLine",["number","4030000000000000"]]]]],["2",["object",635,"object-prototype",[["id",["string","ceramics/firing-schedule#firing-schedule-choose-a-program"]],["headingPath",["string","Firing Schedule > Choose a Program"]],["text",["string","The firing lead reviews the queue against kiln capacity, shelf space, element condition, and upcoming events. Use the approved bisque or glaze program rather than changing ramps casually. Add a slower schedule for thick sections only when the lead has assessed the load. Flag low-fire, mid-fire, test, and uncertain work clearly so a mistaken program is less likely."]],["startLine",["number","4032000000000000"]],["endLine",["number","4034000000000000"]]]]],["3",["object",636,"object-prototype",[["id",["string","ceramics/firing-schedule#firing-schedule-communicate-results"]],["headingPath",["string","Firing Schedule > Communicate Results"]],["text",["string","Post the planned kiln number, load date, program, and expected unload date. If work is delayed, cracked, stuck, or held for inspection, tell the maker and update the queue instead of guessing. After unloading, record actual peak temperature, hold time, witness-cone results, and any abnormal alarms. Retain failed test pieces for troubleshooting when space permits."]],["startLine",["number","4036000000000000"]],["endLine",["number","4038000000000000"]]]]]]]],["diagnostics",["array",637,0,[]]],["document",["object",638,"object-prototype",[["id",["string","ceramics/firing-schedule"]],["type",["string","concept"]],["title",["string","Firing Schedule"]],["tags",["array",639,3,[["0",["string","firing"]],["1",["string","scheduling"]],["2",["string","kiln"]]]]],["sources",["array",640,0,[]]],["verified",["array",641,0,[]]],["body",["string","\n# Firing Schedule\n\n## Build the Queue\n\nSubmit work by the studio cutoff with the maker's name, clay body, firing type, target cone, piece count, and contact details. Confirm that each piece is bone dry, identified on its underside, and within the posted size and weight limits. Group compatible work by program; do not combine loads merely to fill empty space when temperatures or glaze risks differ.\n\n## Choose a Program\n\nThe firing lead reviews the queue against kiln capacity, shelf space, element condition, and upcoming events. Use the approved bisque or glaze program rather than changing ramps casually. Add a slower schedule for thick sections only when the lead has assessed the load. Flag low-fire, mid-fire, test, and uncertain work clearly so a mistaken program is less likely.\n\n## Communicate Results\n\nPost the planned kiln number, load date, program, and expected unload date. If work is delayed, cracked, stuck, or held for inspection, tell the maker and update the queue instead of guessing. After unloading, record actual peak temperature, hold time, witness-cone results, and any abnormal alarms. Retain failed test pieces for troubleshooting when space permits.\n"]],["extensions",["object",642,"object-prototype",[]]],["description",["string","A queueing practice for assigning community-studio work to safe bisque and glaze firing windows."]],["status",["string","draft"]]]]]]]],["5",["object",643,"object-prototype",[["conformance",["string","strict"]],["identity",["object",644,"object-prototype",[["path",["string","ceramics/glaze-mixing.md"]],["documentId",["string","ceramics/glaze-mixing"]]]]],["type",["string","concept"]],["metadata",["object",645,"object-prototype",[["title",["string","Glaze Mixing"]],["description",["string","A repeatable studio practice for preparing, testing, labeling, and storing ceramic glaze."]],["tags",["array",646,3,[["0",["string","glaze"]],["1",["string","materials"]],["2",["string","testing"]]]]],["sourceText",["string",""]]]]],["facets",["object",647,"object-prototype",[["status",["object",648,"object-prototype",[["classified",["boolean",true]],["value",["string","stable"]]]]],["trust",["object",649,"object-prototype",[["classified",["boolean",true]],["value",["string","unverified"]]]]],["staleness",["object",650,"object-prototype",[["classified",["boolean",true]]]]]]]],["sections",["array",651,4,[["0",["object",652,"object-prototype",[["id",["string","ceramics/glaze-mixing#glaze-mixing"]],["headingPath",["string","Glaze Mixing"]],["text",["string",""]],["startLine",["number","4028000000000000"]],["endLine",["number","4028000000000000"]]]]],["1",["object",653,"object-prototype",[["id",["string","ceramics/glaze-mixing#glaze-mixing-gather-and-check"]],["headingPath",["string","Glaze Mixing > Gather and Check"]],["text",["string","Read the recipe card and verify that every ingredient is present, correctly labeled, and within its approved storage area. Wear the studio respirator or mask, gloves, eye protection, and apron required for powdered materials. Record the batch size, lot numbers, mixer setting, and date before opening bags. Never substitute an ingredient without a revised test plan."]],["startLine",["number","402c000000000000"]],["endLine",["number","4030000000000000"]]]]],["2",["object",654,"object-prototype",[["id",["string","ceramics/glaze-mixing#glaze-mixing-mix-and-sieve"]],["headingPath",["string","Glaze Mixing > Mix and Sieve"]],["text",["string","Measure powders on the designated scale, keeping the container on its tray to catch spills. Add water to the mixing bucket before powders, then blend slowly until no dry pockets remain. Pass the slurry through the specified sieve, working in small portions and rinsing the screen between colors. Measure specific gravity or viscosity when the recipe calls for it, and adjust only with documented additions."]],["startLine",["number","4032000000000000"]],["endLine",["number","4034000000000000"]]]]],["3",["object",655,"object-prototype",[["id",["string","ceramics/glaze-mixing#glaze-mixing-label-and-store"]],["headingPath",["string","Glaze Mixing > Label and Store"]],["text",["string","Put the glaze name, batch number, firing range, hazards, and mixing date on the bucket. Keep a retained test tile and a small sample when the batch is new. Seal the lid, wipe the rim, and return tools to the glaze area. Log settling, crawling, unusual color, or contamination before anyone applies the batch."]],["startLine",["number","4036000000000000"]],["endLine",["number","4038000000000000"]]]]]]]],["diagnostics",["array",656,0,[]]],["document",["object",657,"object-prototype",[["id",["string","ceramics/glaze-mixing"]],["type",["string","concept"]],["title",["string","Glaze Mixing"]],["tags",["array",658,3,[["0",["string","glaze"]],["1",["string","materials"]],["2",["string","testing"]]]]],["sources",["array",659,0,[]]],["verified",["array",660,0,[]]],["body",["string","\n# Glaze Mixing\n\n## Gather and Check\n\nRead the recipe card and verify that every ingredient is present, correctly labeled, and within its approved storage area. Wear the studio respirator or mask, gloves, eye protection, and apron required for powdered materials. Record the batch size, lot numbers, mixer setting, and date before opening bags. Never substitute an ingredient without a revised test plan.\n\n## Mix and Sieve\n\nMeasure powders on the designated scale, keeping the container on its tray to catch spills. Add water to the mixing bucket before powders, then blend slowly until no dry pockets remain. Pass the slurry through the specified sieve, working in small portions and rinsing the screen between colors. Measure specific gravity or viscosity when the recipe calls for it, and adjust only with documented additions.\n\n## Label and Store\n\nPut the glaze name, batch number, firing range, hazards, and mixing date on the bucket. Keep a retained test tile and a small sample when the batch is new. Seal the lid, wipe the rim, and return tools to the glaze area. Log settling, crawling, unusual color, or contamination before anyone applies the batch.\n"]],["extensions",["object",661,"object-prototype",[]]],["description",["string","A repeatable studio practice for preparing, testing, labeling, and storing ceramic glaze."]],["status",["string","stable"]]]]]]]],["6",["object",662,"object-prototype",[["conformance",["string","strict"]],["identity",["object",663,"object-prototype",[["path",["string","ceramics/kiln-loading.md"]],["documentId",["string","ceramics/kiln-loading"]]]]],["type",["string","concept"]],["metadata",["object",664,"object-prototype",[["title",["string","Kiln Loading"]],["description",["string","A shared-studio method for spacing, supporting, and documenting ware before a kiln firing."]],["tags",["array",665,3,[["0",["string","kiln"]],["1",["string","firing"]],["2",["string","safety"]]]]],["sourceText",["string",""]]]]],["facets",["object",666,"object-prototype",[["status",["object",667,"object-prototype",[["classified",["boolean",true]],["value",["string","stable"]]]]],["trust",["object",668,"object-prototype",[["classified",["boolean",true]],["value",["string","unverified"]]]]],["staleness",["object",669,"object-prototype",[["classified",["boolean",true]]]]]]]],["sections",["array",670,4,[["0",["object",671,"object-prototype",[["id",["string","ceramics/kiln-loading#kiln-loading"]],["headingPath",["string","Kiln Loading"]],["text",["string",""]],["startLine",["number","4028000000000000"]],["endLine",["number","4028000000000000"]]]]],["1",["object",672,"object-prototype",[["id",["string","ceramics/kiln-loading#kiln-loading-prepare-shelves"]],["headingPath",["string","Kiln Loading > Prepare Shelves"]],["text",["string","Confirm the firing type, target cone, and kiln shelf size before moving ware. Brush loose dust from shelves outdoors or with the studio's approved damp method. Check that each shelf is flat, dry, and free of glaze drips. Add posts of equal height at three or four stable points, leaving enough clearance for heat circulation and the lid."]],["startLine",["number","402c000000000000"]],["endLine",["number","4030000000000000"]]]]],["2",["object",673,"object-prototype",[["id",["string","ceramics/kiln-loading#kiln-loading-arrange-ware"]],["headingPath",["string","Kiln Loading > Arrange Ware"]],["text",["string","Load only fully dry pieces with no trapped water, damp clay, or unfired glaze on their bases. Keep glazed surfaces from touching shelves, posts, or neighboring work. Place taller forms toward the center when possible, and leave a finger-width gap around pieces so they can expand and contract. Use kiln cookies under pieces that may run, but do not hide a damaged foot beneath one."]],["startLine",["number","4032000000000000"]],["endLine",["number","4034000000000000"]]]]],["3",["object",674,"object-prototype",[["id",["string","ceramics/kiln-loading#kiln-loading-close-and-record"]],["headingPath",["string","Kiln Loading > Close and Record"]],["text",["string","Photograph the finished load if the arrangement is unusual. Record shelf levels, owner names, firing program, and any known risks on the kiln sheet. Inspect the lid seal and element path, then ask the firing lead to approve the load before starting it."]],["startLine",["number","4036000000000000"]],["endLine",["number","4038000000000000"]]]]]]]],["diagnostics",["array",675,0,[]]],["document",["object",676,"object-prototype",[["id",["string","ceramics/kiln-loading"]],["type",["string","concept"]],["title",["string","Kiln Loading"]],["tags",["array",677,3,[["0",["string","kiln"]],["1",["string","firing"]],["2",["string","safety"]]]]],["sources",["array",678,0,[]]],["verified",["array",679,0,[]]],["body",["string","\n# Kiln Loading\n\n## Prepare Shelves\n\nConfirm the firing type, target cone, and kiln shelf size before moving ware. Brush loose dust from shelves outdoors or with the studio's approved damp method. Check that each shelf is flat, dry, and free of glaze drips. Add posts of equal height at three or four stable points, leaving enough clearance for heat circulation and the lid.\n\n## Arrange Ware\n\nLoad only fully dry pieces with no trapped water, damp clay, or unfired glaze on their bases. Keep glazed surfaces from touching shelves, posts, or neighboring work. Place taller forms toward the center when possible, and leave a finger-width gap around pieces so they can expand and contract. Use kiln cookies under pieces that may run, but do not hide a damaged foot beneath one.\n\n## Close and Record\n\nPhotograph the finished load if the arrangement is unusual. Record shelf levels, owner names, firing program, and any known risks on the kiln sheet. Inspect the lid seal and element path, then ask the firing lead to approve the load before starting it.\n"]],["extensions",["object",680,"object-prototype",[]]],["description",["string","A shared-studio method for spacing, supporting, and documenting ware before a kiln firing."]],["status",["string","stable"]]]]]]]],["7",["object",681,"object-prototype",[["conformance",["string","strict"]],["identity",["object",682,"object-prototype",[["path",["string","ceramics/studio-opening.md"]],["documentId",["string","ceramics/studio-opening"]]]]],["type",["string","concept"]],["metadata",["object",683,"object-prototype",[["title",["string","Studio Opening"]],["description",["string","A short opening routine that makes a community ceramics studio safe and usable for the first session."]],["tags",["array",684,3,[["0",["string","opening"]],["1",["string","safety"]],["2",["string","studio"]]]]],["sourceText",["string",""]]]]],["facets",["object",685,"object-prototype",[["status",["object",686,"object-prototype",[["classified",["boolean",true]],["value",["string","stable"]]]]],["trust",["object",687,"object-prototype",[["classified",["boolean",true]],["value",["string","unverified"]]]]],["staleness",["object",688,"object-prototype",[["classified",["boolean",true]]]]]]]],["sections",["array",689,4,[["0",["object",690,"object-prototype",[["id",["string","ceramics/studio-opening#studio-opening"]],["headingPath",["string","Studio Opening"]],["text",["string",""]],["startLine",["number","4028000000000000"]],["endLine",["number","4028000000000000"]]]]],["1",["object",691,"object-prototype",[["id",["string","ceramics/studio-opening#studio-opening-enter-and-inspect"]],["headingPath",["string","Studio Opening > Enter and Inspect"]],["text",["string","Unlock the studio using the current access procedure and sign in. Walk the room before turning on equipment. Look for water on the floor, blocked exits, damaged cords, kiln alarms, chemical spills, and tools left in unsafe places. Photograph or isolate anything that could injure someone, then contact the designated lead instead of quietly working around it."]],["startLine",["number","402c000000000000"]],["endLine",["number","4030000000000000"]]]]],["2",["object",692,"object-prototype",[["id",["string","ceramics/studio-opening#studio-opening-start-shared-equipment"]],["headingPath",["string","Studio Opening > Start Shared Equipment"]],["text",["string","Check that ventilation is clear and operating before opening glaze materials. Turn on only the lights, wheels, slab roller, or other equipment needed for the session. Confirm that the kiln area is restricted while a firing is hot, and review the firing sheet for an active program. Keep doors and fire extinguishers accessible throughout the day."]],["startLine",["number","4032000000000000"]],["endLine",["number","4034000000000000"]]]]],["3",["object",693,"object-prototype",[["id",["string","ceramics/studio-opening#studio-opening-prepare-work-areas"]],["headingPath",["string","Studio Opening > Prepare Work Areas"]],["text",["string","Restock clean bats, ribs, sponges, and hand-washing supplies. Set out damp-cleaning tools rather than dry brooms. Confirm that reclaim, scrap, glaze, and general waste containers are labeled and not full. Note shortages on the studio board, then welcome arriving members with a reminder about orientation requirements and today's hazards."]],["startLine",["number","4036000000000000"]],["endLine",["number","4038000000000000"]]]]]]]],["diagnostics",["array",694,0,[]]],["document",["object",695,"object-prototype",[["id",["string","ceramics/studio-opening"]],["type",["string","concept"]],["title",["string","Studio Opening"]],["tags",["array",696,3,[["0",["string","opening"]],["1",["string","safety"]],["2",["string","studio"]]]]],["sources",["array",697,0,[]]],["verified",["array",698,0,[]]],["body",["string","\n# Studio Opening\n\n## Enter and Inspect\n\nUnlock the studio using the current access procedure and sign in. Walk the room before turning on equipment. Look for water on the floor, blocked exits, damaged cords, kiln alarms, chemical spills, and tools left in unsafe places. Photograph or isolate anything that could injure someone, then contact the designated lead instead of quietly working around it.\n\n## Start Shared Equipment\n\nCheck that ventilation is clear and operating before opening glaze materials. Turn on only the lights, wheels, slab roller, or other equipment needed for the session. Confirm that the kiln area is restricted while a firing is hot, and review the firing sheet for an active program. Keep doors and fire extinguishers accessible throughout the day.\n\n## Prepare Work Areas\n\nRestock clean bats, ribs, sponges, and hand-washing supplies. Set out damp-cleaning tools rather than dry brooms. Confirm that reclaim, scrap, glaze, and general waste containers are labeled and not full. Note shortages on the studio board, then welcome arriving members with a reminder about orientation requirements and today's hazards.\n"]],["extensions",["object",699,"object-prototype",[]]],["description",["string","A short opening routine that makes a community ceramics studio safe and usable for the first session."]],["status",["string","stable"]]]]]]]],["8",["object",700,"object-prototype",[["conformance",["string","strict"]],["identity",["object",701,"object-prototype",[["path",["string","ceramics/tool-checkout.md"]],["documentId",["string","ceramics/tool-checkout"]]]]],["type",["string","concept"]],["metadata",["object",702,"object-prototype",[["title",["string","Tool Checkout"]],["description",["string","A simple custody routine for borrowing, using, and returning shared ceramics tools."]],["tags",["array",703,3,[["0",["string","tools"]],["1",["string","checkout"]],["2",["string","stewardship"]]]]],["sourceText",["string",""]]]]],["facets",["object",704,"object-prototype",[["status",["object",705,"object-prototype",[["classified",["boolean",true]],["value",["string","draft"]]]]],["trust",["object",706,"object-prototype",[["classified",["boolean",true]],["value",["string","unverified"]]]]],["staleness",["object",707,"object-prototype",[["classified",["boolean",true]]]]]]]],["sections",["array",708,4,[["0",["object",709,"object-prototype",[["id",["string","ceramics/tool-checkout#tool-checkout"]],["headingPath",["string","Tool Checkout"]],["text",["string",""]],["startLine",["number","4028000000000000"]],["endLine",["number","4028000000000000"]]]]],["1",["object",710,"object-prototype",[["id",["string","ceramics/tool-checkout#tool-checkout-before-borrowing"]],["headingPath",["string","Tool Checkout > Before Borrowing"]],["text",["string","Find the tool in its labeled drawer or rack and inspect it before signing it out. Note missing handles, cracked ribs, bent wires, loose blades, and dried clay. Record the tool ID, your name, checkout time, and expected return when the studio uses a paper or digital register. Ask before taking specialty tools reserved for classes, repairs, or exhibition work."]],["startLine",["number","402c000000000000"]],["endLine",["number","4030000000000000"]]]]],["2",["object",711,"object-prototype",[["id",["string","ceramics/tool-checkout#tool-checkout-during-use"]],["headingPath",["string","Tool Checkout > During Use"]],["text",["string","Use each tool for its intended material and keep it on a tray when moving between stations. Do not leave sharp needles, fettling knives, or cutting wires hidden under clay. Rinse tools at the designated sink or wipe them with a damp cloth, keeping plaster and glaze solids out of drains. If a tool breaks, stop using it and save the pieces for the steward."]],["startLine",["number","4032000000000000"]],["endLine",["number","4034000000000000"]]]]],["3",["object",712,"object-prototype",[["id",["string","ceramics/tool-checkout#tool-checkout-return-and-repair"]],["headingPath",["string","Tool Checkout > Return and Repair"]],["text",["string","Remove all clay, dry the tool, and return it to its marked location before ending the session. Sign it back in with the actual return time. Tag damaged equipment with a short description instead of placing it back in circulation. Report a missing item promptly; a clear record is more useful than replacing a tool twice."]],["startLine",["number","4036000000000000"]],["endLine",["number","4038000000000000"]]]]]]]],["diagnostics",["array",713,0,[]]],["document",["object",714,"object-prototype",[["id",["string","ceramics/tool-checkout"]],["type",["string","concept"]],["title",["string","Tool Checkout"]],["tags",["array",715,3,[["0",["string","tools"]],["1",["string","checkout"]],["2",["string","stewardship"]]]]],["sources",["array",716,0,[]]],["verified",["array",717,0,[]]],["body",["string","\n# Tool Checkout\n\n## Before Borrowing\n\nFind the tool in its labeled drawer or rack and inspect it before signing it out. Note missing handles, cracked ribs, bent wires, loose blades, and dried clay. Record the tool ID, your name, checkout time, and expected return when the studio uses a paper or digital register. Ask before taking specialty tools reserved for classes, repairs, or exhibition work.\n\n## During Use\n\nUse each tool for its intended material and keep it on a tray when moving between stations. Do not leave sharp needles, fettling knives, or cutting wires hidden under clay. Rinse tools at the designated sink or wipe them with a damp cloth, keeping plaster and glaze solids out of drains. If a tool breaks, stop using it and save the pieces for the steward.\n\n## Return and Repair\n\nRemove all clay, dry the tool, and return it to its marked location before ending the session. Sign it back in with the actual return time. Tag damaged equipment with a short description instead of placing it back in circulation. Report a missing item promptly; a clear record is more useful than replacing a tool twice.\n"]],["extensions",["object",718,"object-prototype",[]]],["description",["string","A simple custody routine for borrowing, using, and returning shared ceramics tools."]],["status",["string","draft"]]]]]]]],["9",["object",719,"object-prototype",[["conformance",["string","strict"]],["identity",["object",720,"object-prototype",[["path",["string","ceramics/wheel-maintenance.md"]],["documentId",["string","ceramics/wheel-maintenance"]]]]],["type",["string","concept"]],["metadata",["object",721,"object-prototype",[["title",["string","Wheel Maintenance"]],["description",["string","Routine care that keeps shared pottery wheels clean, quiet, and ready for the next maker."]],["tags",["array",722,3,[["0",["string","wheels"]],["1",["string","maintenance"]],["2",["string","cleanup"]]]]],["sourceText",["string",""]]]]],["facets",["object",723,"object-prototype",[["status",["object",724,"object-prototype",[["classified",["boolean",true]],["value",["string","stable"]]]]],["trust",["object",725,"object-prototype",[["classified",["boolean",true]],["value",["string","unverified"]]]]],["staleness",["object",726,"object-prototype",[["classified",["boolean",true]]]]]]]],["sections",["array",727,4,[["0",["object",728,"object-prototype",[["id",["string","ceramics/wheel-maintenance#wheel-maintenance"]],["headingPath",["string","Wheel Maintenance"]],["text",["string",""]],["startLine",["number","4028000000000000"]],["endLine",["number","4028000000000000"]]]]],["1",["object",729,"object-prototype",[["id",["string","ceramics/wheel-maintenance#wheel-maintenance-daily-cleanup"]],["headingPath",["string","Wheel Maintenance > Daily Cleanup"]],["text",["string","Switch off and unplug the wheel before removing splash pans or reaching near the belt. Scrape wet clay into the reclaim bin while it is still soft; never wash chunks into the drain. Wipe the pan, bat pins, wheel head, seat, and nearby floor with a damp sponge. Check that the foot pedal is dry and that the power cord is not under a chair or table leg."]],["startLine",["number","402c000000000000"]],["endLine",["number","4030000000000000"]]]]],["2",["object",730,"object-prototype",[["id",["string","ceramics/wheel-maintenance#wheel-maintenance-weekly-service"]],["headingPath",["string","Wheel Maintenance > Weekly Service"]],["text",["string","The studio steward should remove the splash pan, clean the drain channel, and inspect the belt area for packed clay. Tighten loose bat pins and chair hardware with the correct tool. Listen for grinding, pulsing speed, unusual heat, or wobble during a short empty test. Keep lubricants and water away from motor openings unless the manufacturer's instructions specifically allow them."]],["startLine",["number","4032000000000000"]],["endLine",["number","4034000000000000"]]]]],["3",["object",731,"object-prototype",[["id",["string","ceramics/wheel-maintenance#wheel-maintenance-faults-and-handover"]],["headingPath",["string","Wheel Maintenance > Faults and Handover"]],["text",["string","Tag a wheel that behaves unpredictably and move its plug to the off position. Write the wheel number, symptom, date, and last user in the maintenance log. Do not open electrical covers or return the wheel to service until a qualified person has checked it."]],["startLine",["number","4036000000000000"]],["endLine",["number","4038000000000000"]]]]]]]],["diagnostics",["array",732,0,[]]],["document",["object",733,"object-prototype",[["id",["string","ceramics/wheel-maintenance"]],["type",["string","concept"]],["title",["string","Wheel Maintenance"]],["tags",["array",734,3,[["0",["string","wheels"]],["1",["string","maintenance"]],["2",["string","cleanup"]]]]],["sources",["array",735,0,[]]],["verified",["array",736,0,[]]],["body",["string","\n# Wheel Maintenance\n\n## Daily Cleanup\n\nSwitch off and unplug the wheel before removing splash pans or reaching near the belt. Scrape wet clay into the reclaim bin while it is still soft; never wash chunks into the drain. Wipe the pan, bat pins, wheel head, seat, and nearby floor with a damp sponge. Check that the foot pedal is dry and that the power cord is not under a chair or table leg.\n\n## Weekly Service\n\nThe studio steward should remove the splash pan, clean the drain channel, and inspect the belt area for packed clay. Tighten loose bat pins and chair hardware with the correct tool. Listen for grinding, pulsing speed, unusual heat, or wobble during a short empty test. Keep lubricants and water away from motor openings unless the manufacturer's instructions specifically allow them.\n\n## Faults and Handover\n\nTag a wheel that behaves unpredictably and move its plug to the off position. Write the wheel number, symptom, date, and last user in the maintenance log. Do not open electrical covers or return the wheel to service until a qualified person has checked it.\n"]],["extensions",["object",737,"object-prototype",[]]],["description",["string","Routine care that keeps shared pottery wheels clean, quiet, and ready for the next maker."]],["status",["string","stable"]]]]]]]],["10",["object",738,"object-prototype",[["conformance",["string","strict"]],["identity",["object",739,"object-prototype",[["path",["string","gardens/autumn-garden-checklist.md"]],["documentId",["string","gardens/autumn-garden-checklist"]]]]],["type",["string","plan"]],["metadata",["object",740,"object-prototype",[["title",["string","Autumn Garden Checklist"]],["description",["string","Tasks that prepare a garden for cooler weather."]],["tags",["array",741,3,[["0",["string","garden"]],["1",["string","autumn"]],["2",["string","maintenance"]]]]],["sourceText",["string",""]]]]],["facets",["object",742,"object-prototype",[["status",["object",743,"object-prototype",[["classified",["boolean",true]],["value",["string","stable"]]]]],["trust",["object",744,"object-prototype",[["classified",["boolean",true]],["value",["string","unverified"]]]]],["staleness",["object",745,"object-prototype",[["classified",["boolean",true]]]]]]]],["sections",["array",746,1,[["0",["object",747,"object-prototype",[["id",["string","gardens/autumn-garden-checklist#autumn-garden-checklist"]],["headingPath",["string","Autumn Garden Checklist"]],["text",["string","- Collect seeds from mature plants.\n- Add fallen leaves to the compost pile.\n- Mulch perennial beds before the first hard frost.\n- Clean and store garden tools."]],["startLine",["number","4020000000000000"]],["endLine",["number","402a000000000000"]]]]]]]],["diagnostics",["array",748,0,[]]],["document",["object",749,"object-prototype",[["id",["string","gardens/autumn-garden-checklist"]],["type",["string","plan"]],["title",["string","Autumn Garden Checklist"]],["tags",["array",750,3,[["0",["string","garden"]],["1",["string","autumn"]],["2",["string","maintenance"]]]]],["sources",["array",751,0,[]]],["verified",["array",752,0,[]]],["body",["string","\n# Autumn Garden Checklist\n\n- Collect seeds from mature plants.\n- Add fallen leaves to the compost pile.\n- Mulch perennial beds before the first hard frost.\n- Clean and store garden tools.\n"]],["extensions",["object",753,"object-prototype",[]]],["description",["string","Tasks that prepare a garden for cooler weather."]],["status",["string","stable"]]]]]]]],["11",["object",754,"object-prototype",[["conformance",["string","strict"]],["identity",["object",755,"object-prototype",[["path",["string","gardens/composting.md"]],["documentId",["string","gardens/composting"]]]]],["type",["string","concept"]],["metadata",["object",756,"object-prototype",[["title",["string","Composting"]],["tags",["array",757,3,[["0",["string","compost"]],["1",["string","organic-matter"]],["2",["string","waste"]]]]],["sourceText",["string",""]]]]],["facets",["object",758,"object-prototype",[["status",["object",759,"object-prototype",[["classified",["boolean",true]],["value",["string","stable"]]]]],["trust",["object",760,"object-prototype",[["classified",["boolean",true]],["value",["string","unverified"]]]]],["staleness",["object",761,"object-prototype",[["classified",["boolean",true]]]]]]]],["sections",["array",762,4,[["0",["object",763,"object-prototype",[["id",["string","gardens/composting#composting"]],["headingPath",["string","Composting"]],["text",["string","The garden compost system turns plant trimmings and approved kitchen scraps into a soil amendment. A pile needs a mix of moist, nitrogen-rich materials and dry, carbon-rich materials, plus air and enough moisture for microbes to work. Keep the system simple so plot holders can use it correctly."]],["startLine",["number","4024000000000000"]],["endLine",["number","4028000000000000"]]]]],["1",["object",764,"object-prototype",[["id",["string","gardens/composting#composting-what-goes-in"]],["headingPath",["string","Composting > What Goes In"]],["text",["string","Add disease-free leaves, weeds before they set seed, spent annual plants, chopped stems, coffee grounds, and untreated grass clippings. Balance wet greens with dry leaves, shredded cardboard, or straw. Do not add meat, dairy, pet waste, glossy paper, chemically treated wood, or invasive weeds. Put questionable material in the trash or ask the garden coordinator."]],["startLine",["number","402c000000000000"]],["endLine",["number","4030000000000000"]]]]],["2",["object",765,"object-prototype",[["id",["string","gardens/composting#composting-manage-the-pile"]],["headingPath",["string","Composting > Manage the Pile"]],["text",["string","Build the pile at least three feet wide, and keep it as damp as a wrung-out sponge. Turn or loosen it with a fork when the center is compacted, smells sour, or stays cold after fresh material is added. A covered bin prevents flooding while still allowing airflow. Keep paths clear and close the lid after each deposit."]],["startLine",["number","4032000000000000"]],["endLine",["number","4034000000000000"]]]]],["3",["object",766,"object-prototype",[["id",["string","gardens/composting#composting-know-when-it-is-ready"]],["headingPath",["string","Composting > Know When It Is Ready"]],["text",["string","Finished compost is dark, crumbly, and earthy-smelling; the original ingredients are difficult to recognize. Screen out large pieces and return them to the active pile. Spread the finished material around crops or mix a modest amount into new beds rather than burying a thick layer against stems."]],["startLine",["number","4036000000000000"]],["endLine",["number","4038000000000000"]]]]]]]],["diagnostics",["array",767,0,[]]],["document",["object",768,"object-prototype",[["id",["string","gardens/composting"]],["type",["string","concept"]],["title",["string","Composting"]],["tags",["array",769,3,[["0",["string","compost"]],["1",["string","organic-matter"]],["2",["string","waste"]]]]],["sources",["array",770,0,[]]],["verified",["array",771,0,[]]],["body",["string","# Composting\n\nThe garden compost system turns plant trimmings and approved kitchen scraps into a soil amendment. A pile needs a mix of moist, nitrogen-rich materials and dry, carbon-rich materials, plus air and enough moisture for microbes to work. Keep the system simple so plot holders can use it correctly.\n\n## What Goes In\n\nAdd disease-free leaves, weeds before they set seed, spent annual plants, chopped stems, coffee grounds, and untreated grass clippings. Balance wet greens with dry leaves, shredded cardboard, or straw. Do not add meat, dairy, pet waste, glossy paper, chemically treated wood, or invasive weeds. Put questionable material in the trash or ask the garden coordinator.\n\n## Manage the Pile\n\nBuild the pile at least three feet wide, and keep it as damp as a wrung-out sponge. Turn or loosen it with a fork when the center is compacted, smells sour, or stays cold after fresh material is added. A covered bin prevents flooding while still allowing airflow. Keep paths clear and close the lid after each deposit.\n\n## Know When It Is Ready\n\nFinished compost is dark, crumbly, and earthy-smelling; the original ingredients are difficult to recognize. Screen out large pieces and return them to the active pile. Spread the finished material around crops or mix a modest amount into new beds rather than burying a thick layer against stems."]],["extensions",["object",772,"object-prototype",[]]],["status",["string","stable"]]]]]]]],["12",["object",773,"object-prototype",[["conformance",["string","strict"]],["identity",["object",774,"object-prototype",[["path",["string","gardens/crop-rotation.md"]],["documentId",["string","gardens/crop-rotation"]]]]],["type",["string","concept"]],["metadata",["object",775,"object-prototype",[["title",["string","Crop Rotation"]],["tags",["array",776,3,[["0",["string","rotation"]],["1",["string","plant-families"]],["2",["string","soil-health"]]]]],["sourceText",["string",""]]]]],["facets",["object",777,"object-prototype",[["status",["object",778,"object-prototype",[["classified",["boolean",true]],["value",["string","stable"]]]]],["trust",["object",779,"object-prototype",[["classified",["boolean",true]],["value",["string","unverified"]]]]],["staleness",["object",780,"object-prototype",[["classified",["boolean",true]]]]]]]],["sections",["array",781,4,[["0",["object",782,"object-prototype",[["id",["string","gardens/crop-rotation#crop-rotation"]],["headingPath",["string","Crop Rotation"]],["text",["string","Crop rotation moves related crops between beds each year. It reduces recurring soil pests and diseases while balancing nutrient demand. Rotation works best when the plan tracks plant families, not just common crop names."]],["startLine",["number","4024000000000000"]],["endLine",["number","4028000000000000"]]]]],["1",["object",783,"object-prototype",[["id",["string","gardens/crop-rotation#crop-rotation-group-crops-by-family"]],["headingPath",["string","Crop Rotation > Group Crops by Family"]],["text",["string","Keep tomatoes, peppers, eggplants, and potatoes together as nightshades; group cabbage, kale, and radishes as brassicas; and group beans and peas as legumes. Cucumbers, squash, and melons are cucurbits. Note perennial crops separately because they should not move each year. A family-labeled bed map is easier to follow."]],["startLine",["number","402c000000000000"]],["endLine",["number","4030000000000000"]]]]],["2",["object",784,"object-prototype",[["id",["string","gardens/crop-rotation#crop-rotation-use-a-simple-sequence"]],["headingPath",["string","Crop Rotation > Use a Simple Sequence"]],["text",["string","Move heavy-feeding fruiting crops away from the same bed for at least two seasons when space allows. Follow them with legumes or a cover crop, then use the bed for leafy crops or roots. Rotation is not a cure for contaminated soil, and it cannot replace sanitation, resistant varieties, or good airflow, but it lowers repeated exposure."]],["startLine",["number","4032000000000000"]],["endLine",["number","4034000000000000"]]]]],["3",["object",785,"object-prototype",[["id",["string","gardens/crop-rotation#crop-rotation-coordinate-the-garden"]],["headingPath",["string","Crop Rotation > Coordinate the Garden"]],["text",["string","Record this year’s families at the shed and copy the map before winter cleanup. If plots are managed separately, coordinate adjacent beds so a disease is not simply moved a few feet away. When space is tight, rotate the most vulnerable families first and use containers or fresh soil for crops that cannot move. Review harvest and disease notes before choosing next year’s placements."]],["startLine",["number","4036000000000000"]],["endLine",["number","4038000000000000"]]]]]]]],["diagnostics",["array",786,0,[]]],["document",["object",787,"object-prototype",[["id",["string","gardens/crop-rotation"]],["type",["string","concept"]],["title",["string","Crop Rotation"]],["tags",["array",788,3,[["0",["string","rotation"]],["1",["string","plant-families"]],["2",["string","soil-health"]]]]],["sources",["array",789,0,[]]],["verified",["array",790,0,[]]],["body",["string","# Crop Rotation\n\nCrop rotation moves related crops between beds each year. It reduces recurring soil pests and diseases while balancing nutrient demand. Rotation works best when the plan tracks plant families, not just common crop names.\n\n## Group Crops by Family\n\nKeep tomatoes, peppers, eggplants, and potatoes together as nightshades; group cabbage, kale, and radishes as brassicas; and group beans and peas as legumes. Cucumbers, squash, and melons are cucurbits. Note perennial crops separately because they should not move each year. A family-labeled bed map is easier to follow.\n\n## Use a Simple Sequence\n\nMove heavy-feeding fruiting crops away from the same bed for at least two seasons when space allows. Follow them with legumes or a cover crop, then use the bed for leafy crops or roots. Rotation is not a cure for contaminated soil, and it cannot replace sanitation, resistant varieties, or good airflow, but it lowers repeated exposure.\n\n## Coordinate the Garden\n\nRecord this year’s families at the shed and copy the map before winter cleanup. If plots are managed separately, coordinate adjacent beds so a disease is not simply moved a few feet away. When space is tight, rotate the most vulnerable families first and use containers or fresh soil for crops that cannot move. Review harvest and disease notes before choosing next year’s placements."]],["extensions",["object",791,"object-prototype",[]]],["status",["string","stable"]]]]]]]],["13",["object",792,"object-prototype",[["conformance",["string","strict"]],["identity",["object",793,"object-prototype",[["path",["string","gardens/drip-irrigation.md"]],["documentId",["string","gardens/drip-irrigation"]]]]],["type",["string","concept"]],["metadata",["object",794,"object-prototype",[["title",["string","Drip Irrigation"]],["tags",["array",795,3,[["0",["string","irrigation"]],["1",["string","water"]],["2",["string","efficiency"]]]]],["sourceText",["string",""]]]]],["facets",["object",796,"object-prototype",[["status",["object",797,"object-prototype",[["classified",["boolean",true]],["value",["string","stable"]]]]],["trust",["object",798,"object-prototype",[["classified",["boolean",true]],["value",["string","unverified"]]]]],["staleness",["object",799,"object-prototype",[["classified",["boolean",true]]]]]]]],["sections",["array",800,4,[["0",["object",801,"object-prototype",[["id",["string","gardens/drip-irrigation#drip-irrigation"]],["headingPath",["string","Drip Irrigation"]],["text",["string","Drip irrigation delivers water at the soil surface, helping beds use less water while keeping leaves dry. Design each line for the crop spacing and bed length, and label valves so volunteers can shut down one section without guessing. Check the system before the first hot week rather than waiting for wilted plants."]],["startLine",["number","4024000000000000"]],["endLine",["number","4028000000000000"]]]]],["1",["object",802,"object-prototype",[["id",["string","gardens/drip-irrigation#drip-irrigation-set-up-the-lines"]],["headingPath",["string","Drip Irrigation > Set Up the Lines"]],["text",["string","Start with a filter and pressure regulator if the supply requires them. Lay emitters beside plant roots, not across paths, and secure tubing with pins so it stays in place during weeding. Use a flush end on each line. Separate containers, seedlings, and established crops into zones."]],["startLine",["number","402c000000000000"]],["endLine",["number","4030000000000000"]]]]],["2",["object",803,"object-prototype",[["id",["string","gardens/drip-irrigation#drip-irrigation-water-by-observation"]],["headingPath",["string","Drip Irrigation > Water by Observation"]],["text",["string","Run the system long enough to wet the root zone, then let the surface dry slightly before watering again. Push a finger or trowel several inches into the soil to check moisture; a timer cannot account for rain, mulch, or soil type. Seedlings may need short, frequent cycles, while deep-rooted crops benefit from less frequent soaking."]],["startLine",["number","4032000000000000"]],["endLine",["number","4034000000000000"]]]]],["3",["object",804,"object-prototype",[["id",["string","gardens/drip-irrigation#drip-irrigation-maintain-shared-equipment"]],["headingPath",["string","Drip Irrigation > Maintain Shared Equipment"]],["text",["string","Inspect for leaks, clogged emitters, and damaged tubing every week. Flush lines at the ends, clean the filter, and repair punctures with connectors. Drain or remove exposed equipment before freezing weather. Record repairs and the normal schedule on the valve box so the next volunteer can continue without resetting the whole system."]],["startLine",["number","4036000000000000"]],["endLine",["number","4038000000000000"]]]]]]]],["diagnostics",["array",805,0,[]]],["document",["object",806,"object-prototype",[["id",["string","gardens/drip-irrigation"]],["type",["string","concept"]],["title",["string","Drip Irrigation"]],["tags",["array",807,3,[["0",["string","irrigation"]],["1",["string","water"]],["2",["string","efficiency"]]]]],["sources",["array",808,0,[]]],["verified",["array",809,0,[]]],["body",["string","# Drip Irrigation\n\nDrip irrigation delivers water at the soil surface, helping beds use less water while keeping leaves dry. Design each line for the crop spacing and bed length, and label valves so volunteers can shut down one section without guessing. Check the system before the first hot week rather than waiting for wilted plants.\n\n## Set Up the Lines\n\nStart with a filter and pressure regulator if the supply requires them. Lay emitters beside plant roots, not across paths, and secure tubing with pins so it stays in place during weeding. Use a flush end on each line. Separate containers, seedlings, and established crops into zones.\n\n## Water by Observation\n\nRun the system long enough to wet the root zone, then let the surface dry slightly before watering again. Push a finger or trowel several inches into the soil to check moisture; a timer cannot account for rain, mulch, or soil type. Seedlings may need short, frequent cycles, while deep-rooted crops benefit from less frequent soaking.\n\n## Maintain Shared Equipment\n\nInspect for leaks, clogged emitters, and damaged tubing every week. Flush lines at the ends, clean the filter, and repair punctures with connectors. Drain or remove exposed equipment before freezing weather. Record repairs and the normal schedule on the valve box so the next volunteer can continue without resetting the whole system."]],["extensions",["object",810,"object-prototype",[]]],["status",["string","stable"]]]]]]]],["14",["object",811,"object-prototype",[["conformance",["string","degraded"]],["identity",["object",812,"object-prototype",[["path",["string","gardens/experimental-mulch-notes.md"]],["documentId",["string","gardens/experimental-mulch-notes"]]]]],["type",["string","concept"]],["metadata",["object",813,"object-prototype",[["title",["string","Experimental Mulch Notes"]],["description",["string","Early observations from a trial comparing garden mulches."]],["tags",["array",814,3,[["0",["string","garden"]],["1",["string","mulch"]],["2",["string","experiment"]]]]],["sourceText",["string",""]]]]],["facets",["object",815,"object-prototype",[["status",["object",816,"object-prototype",[["classified",["boolean",false]]]]],["trust",["object",817,"object-prototype",[["classified",["boolean",true]],["value",["string","unverified"]]]]],["staleness",["object",818,"object-prototype",[["classified",["boolean",true]]]]]]]],["sections",["array",819,1,[["0",["object",820,"object-prototype",[["id",["string","gardens/experimental-mulch-notes#experimental-mulch-notes"]],["headingPath",["string","Experimental Mulch Notes"]],["text",["string","Three neighboring beds are testing leaf mold, clean straw, and wood chips. Each bed receives the same watering schedule so surface moisture, weed pressure, and soil temperature can be compared.\n\nThese observations are preliminary. Check beneath each mulch layer weekly, record moisture before watering, and note any slug activity or compacted material before changing the trial."]],["startLine",["number","4022000000000000"]],["endLine",["number","402a000000000000"]]]]]]]],["diagnostics",["array",821,1,[["0",["object",822,"object-prototype",[["code",["string","ERR_OKF_FIELD"]],["path",["string","gardens/experimental-mulch-notes.md"]],["field",["string","status"]],["message",["string","Invalid OKF field: gardens/experimental-mulch-notes.md (status)"]]]]]]]]]]],["15",["object",823,"object-prototype",[["conformance",["string","strict"]],["identity",["object",824,"object-prototype",[["path",["string","gardens/greenhouse-basics.md"]],["documentId",["string","gardens/greenhouse-basics"]]]]],["type",["string","concept"]],["metadata",["object",825,"object-prototype",[["title",["string","Greenhouse Basics"]],["tags",["array",826,3,[["0",["string","greenhouse"]],["1",["string","seedlings"]],["2",["string","ventilation"]]]]],["sourceText",["string",""]]]]],["facets",["object",827,"object-prototype",[["status",["object",828,"object-prototype",[["classified",["boolean",true]],["value",["string","stable"]]]]],["trust",["object",829,"object-prototype",[["classified",["boolean",true]],["value",["string","unverified"]]]]],["staleness",["object",830,"object-prototype",[["classified",["boolean",true]]]]]]]],["sections",["array",831,4,[["0",["object",832,"object-prototype",[["id",["string","gardens/greenhouse-basics#greenhouse-basics"]],["headingPath",["string","Greenhouse Basics"]],["text",["string","A small greenhouse extends the growing season, but sunlight can make it dangerously hot. Monitor it rather than using it as storage. Keep a thermometer at plant height, assign opening and closing duties, and post a checklist by the door."]],["startLine",["number","4024000000000000"]],["endLine",["number","4028000000000000"]]]]],["1",["object",833,"object-prototype",[["id",["string","gardens/greenhouse-basics#greenhouse-basics-control-temperature-and-air"]],["headingPath",["string","Greenhouse Basics > Control Temperature and Air"]],["text",["string","Open vents or doors early when the inside temperature begins rising, and close them before a cold night. Use shade cloth during intense summer sun, leaving enough light for sturdy growth. Air movement reduces fungal disease; a fan should circulate air without drying seedlings. Do not crowd trays against glazing or block vents with supplies."]],["startLine",["number","402c000000000000"]],["endLine",["number","4030000000000000"]]]]],["2",["object",834,"object-prototype",[["id",["string","gardens/greenhouse-basics#greenhouse-basics-water-and-organize"]],["headingPath",["string","Greenhouse Basics > Water and Organize"]],["text",["string","Water in the morning so foliage and benches dry before evening. Check each tray individually because different crops and container sizes lose moisture at different rates. Keep hoses off walkways, store tools clean, and raise seedling trays from the floor to improve drainage. Label every flat with crop, variety, sowing date, and owner or destination bed."]],["startLine",["number","4032000000000000"]],["endLine",["number","4034000000000000"]]]]],["3",["object",835,"object-prototype",[["id",["string","gardens/greenhouse-basics#greenhouse-basics-clean-between-uses"]],["headingPath",["string","Greenhouse Basics > Clean Between Uses"]],["text",["string","Remove dead leaves and spilled mix immediately. At the end of a crop cycle, empty benches, wash trays, and disinfect reusable containers with an approved solution. Inspect glazing, screens, and heaters before the season starts. If a plant shows an unfamiliar disease or insect outbreak, isolate the tray and notify the greenhouse steward before moving anything else."]],["startLine",["number","4036000000000000"]],["endLine",["number","4038000000000000"]]]]]]]],["diagnostics",["array",836,0,[]]],["document",["object",837,"object-prototype",[["id",["string","gardens/greenhouse-basics"]],["type",["string","concept"]],["title",["string","Greenhouse Basics"]],["tags",["array",838,3,[["0",["string","greenhouse"]],["1",["string","seedlings"]],["2",["string","ventilation"]]]]],["sources",["array",839,0,[]]],["verified",["array",840,0,[]]],["body",["string","# Greenhouse Basics\n\nA small greenhouse extends the growing season, but sunlight can make it dangerously hot. Monitor it rather than using it as storage. Keep a thermometer at plant height, assign opening and closing duties, and post a checklist by the door.\n\n## Control Temperature and Air\n\nOpen vents or doors early when the inside temperature begins rising, and close them before a cold night. Use shade cloth during intense summer sun, leaving enough light for sturdy growth. Air movement reduces fungal disease; a fan should circulate air without drying seedlings. Do not crowd trays against glazing or block vents with supplies.\n\n## Water and Organize\n\nWater in the morning so foliage and benches dry before evening. Check each tray individually because different crops and container sizes lose moisture at different rates. Keep hoses off walkways, store tools clean, and raise seedling trays from the floor to improve drainage. Label every flat with crop, variety, sowing date, and owner or destination bed.\n\n## Clean Between Uses\n\nRemove dead leaves and spilled mix immediately. At the end of a crop cycle, empty benches, wash trays, and disinfect reusable containers with an approved solution. Inspect glazing, screens, and heaters before the season starts. If a plant shows an unfamiliar disease or insect outbreak, isolate the tray and notify the greenhouse steward before moving anything else."]],["extensions",["object",841,"object-prototype",[]]],["status",["string","stable"]]]]]]]],["16",["object",842,"object-prototype",[["conformance",["string","strict"]],["identity",["object",843,"object-prototype",[["path",["string","gardens/harvest-storage.md"]],["documentId",["string","gardens/harvest-storage"]]]]],["type",["string","concept"]],["metadata",["object",844,"object-prototype",[["title",["string","Harvest and Storage"]],["tags",["array",845,3,[["0",["string","harvest"]],["1",["string","storage"]],["2",["string","preservation"]]]]],["sourceText",["string",""]]]]],["facets",["object",846,"object-prototype",[["status",["object",847,"object-prototype",[["classified",["boolean",true]],["value",["string","stable"]]]]],["trust",["object",848,"object-prototype",[["classified",["boolean",true]],["value",["string","unverified"]]]]],["staleness",["object",849,"object-prototype",[["classified",["boolean",true]]]]]]]],["sections",["array",850,4,[["0",["object",851,"object-prototype",[["id",["string","gardens/harvest-storage#harvest-and-storage"]],["headingPath",["string","Harvest and Storage"]],["text",["string","Storage begins with harvesting at the useful stage and handling produce gently. Pick produce during cool hours, use clean containers, and label donations with the crop, date, and preparation notes. Regular harvests keep plants producing and limit pests."]],["startLine",["number","4024000000000000"]],["endLine",["number","4028000000000000"]]]]],["1",["object",852,"object-prototype",[["id",["string","gardens/harvest-storage#harvest-and-storage-pick-for-quality"]],["headingPath",["string","Harvest and Storage > Pick for Quality"]],["text",["string","Harvest leafy greens while leaves are crisp, beans before seeds swell, and tomatoes when they have begun to color if they must travel. Cut squash and herbs with clean tools rather than tearing stems. Keep dry-stored produce unwashed; brush off soil and remove damage. Cool tender crops promptly, but keep onions, garlic, and winter squash dry and warm enough to cure."]],["startLine",["number","402c000000000000"]],["endLine",["number","4030000000000000"]]]]],["2",["object",853,"object-prototype",[["id",["string","gardens/harvest-storage#harvest-and-storage-store-by-need"]],["headingPath",["string","Harvest and Storage > Store by Need"]],["text",["string","Refrigerate greens, carrots, beets, and most herbs in breathable containers with moisture. Keep potatoes dark and ventilated. Cure onions and garlic in a dry, airy place until skins are papery. Store winter squash where it will not freeze, and inspect it weekly. Keep ethylene-producing ripe fruit away from sensitive greens when possible."]],["startLine",["number","4032000000000000"]],["endLine",["number","4034000000000000"]]]]],["3",["object",854,"object-prototype",[["id",["string","gardens/harvest-storage#harvest-and-storage-preserve-and-share"]],["headingPath",["string","Harvest and Storage > Preserve and Share"]],["text",["string","Freeze, dry, ferment, or can surplus only with tested recipes and clean equipment. Mark every jar or bag with contents and date. A harvest board can match extra produce with the community fridge or a scheduled distribution before quality declines. Compost anything unsafe rather than passing it along."]],["startLine",["number","4036000000000000"]],["endLine",["number","4038000000000000"]]]]]]]],["diagnostics",["array",855,0,[]]],["document",["object",856,"object-prototype",[["id",["string","gardens/harvest-storage"]],["type",["string","concept"]],["title",["string","Harvest and Storage"]],["tags",["array",857,3,[["0",["string","harvest"]],["1",["string","storage"]],["2",["string","preservation"]]]]],["sources",["array",858,0,[]]],["verified",["array",859,0,[]]],["body",["string","# Harvest and Storage\n\nStorage begins with harvesting at the useful stage and handling produce gently. Pick produce during cool hours, use clean containers, and label donations with the crop, date, and preparation notes. Regular harvests keep plants producing and limit pests.\n\n## Pick for Quality\n\nHarvest leafy greens while leaves are crisp, beans before seeds swell, and tomatoes when they have begun to color if they must travel. Cut squash and herbs with clean tools rather than tearing stems. Keep dry-stored produce unwashed; brush off soil and remove damage. Cool tender crops promptly, but keep onions, garlic, and winter squash dry and warm enough to cure.\n\n## Store by Need\n\nRefrigerate greens, carrots, beets, and most herbs in breathable containers with moisture. Keep potatoes dark and ventilated. Cure onions and garlic in a dry, airy place until skins are papery. Store winter squash where it will not freeze, and inspect it weekly. Keep ethylene-producing ripe fruit away from sensitive greens when possible.\n\n## Preserve and Share\n\nFreeze, dry, ferment, or can surplus only with tested recipes and clean equipment. Mark every jar or bag with contents and date. A harvest board can match extra produce with the community fridge or a scheduled distribution before quality declines. Compost anything unsafe rather than passing it along."]],["extensions",["object",860,"object-prototype",[]]],["status",["string","stable"]]]]]]]],["17",["object",861,"object-prototype",[["conformance",["string","strict"]],["identity",["object",862,"object-prototype",[["path",["string","gardens/pest-management.md"]],["documentId",["string","gardens/pest-management"]]]]],["type",["string","concept"]],["metadata",["object",863,"object-prototype",[["title",["string","Pest Management"]],["tags",["array",864,3,[["0",["string","pests"]],["1",["string","scouting"]],["2",["string","prevention"]]]]],["sourceText",["string",""]]]]],["facets",["object",865,"object-prototype",[["status",["object",866,"object-prototype",[["classified",["boolean",true]],["value",["string","stable"]]]]],["trust",["object",867,"object-prototype",[["classified",["boolean",true]],["value",["string","unverified"]]]]],["staleness",["object",868,"object-prototype",[["classified",["boolean",true]]]]]]]],["sections",["array",869,4,[["0",["object",870,"object-prototype",[["id",["string","gardens/pest-management#pest-management"]],["headingPath",["string","Pest Management"]],["text",["string","Pest management in a community garden starts with prevention and observation, not automatic spraying. A few holes or aphids may be tolerable when plants are growing well. Set an action threshold for each crop, protect young plants, and record what was found so neighboring plot holders can respond consistently."]],["startLine",["number","4024000000000000"]],["endLine",["number","4028000000000000"]]]]],["1",["object",871,"object-prototype",[["id",["string","gardens/pest-management#pest-management-scout-systematically"]],["headingPath",["string","Pest Management > Scout Systematically"]],["text",["string","Walk every bed weekly, checking leaf undersides, stems, new growth, and soil around the plant. Look for chewed edges, stippling, sticky residue, wilt, and clusters of insects. Identify the pest before choosing a treatment; insect damage, nutrient problems, and plant diseases can look similar. Photograph unfamiliar symptoms and ask the garden coordinator before taking action."]],["startLine",["number","402c000000000000"]],["endLine",["number","4030000000000000"]]]]],["2",["object",872,"object-prototype",[["id",["string","gardens/pest-management#pest-management-prevent-problems"]],["headingPath",["string","Pest Management > Prevent Problems"]],["text",["string","Remove diseased leaves promptly and discard them according to the garden’s rules. Use row cover over crops that need protection, keep plants spaced for airflow, and rotate crop families between beds. Water at the soil line and clear fallen fruit that attracts pests. Encourage predators with flowers and avoid broad-spectrum products that harm them."]],["startLine",["number","4032000000000000"]],["endLine",["number","4034000000000000"]]]]],["3",["object",873,"object-prototype",[["id",["string","gardens/pest-management#pest-management-treat-carefully"]],["headingPath",["string","Pest Management > Treat Carefully"]],["text",["string","First try hand-picking, a water spray, pruning, or a barrier. If a product is approved, read its label, protect pollinators, spray only the affected crop, and follow the re-entry and harvest interval. Never apply a neighbor’s unlabelled mixture. Log the date, product, bed, and result so future decisions improve."]],["startLine",["number","4036000000000000"]],["endLine",["number","4038000000000000"]]]]]]]],["diagnostics",["array",874,0,[]]],["document",["object",875,"object-prototype",[["id",["string","gardens/pest-management"]],["type",["string","concept"]],["title",["string","Pest Management"]],["tags",["array",876,3,[["0",["string","pests"]],["1",["string","scouting"]],["2",["string","prevention"]]]]],["sources",["array",877,0,[]]],["verified",["array",878,0,[]]],["body",["string","# Pest Management\n\nPest management in a community garden starts with prevention and observation, not automatic spraying. A few holes or aphids may be tolerable when plants are growing well. Set an action threshold for each crop, protect young plants, and record what was found so neighboring plot holders can respond consistently.\n\n## Scout Systematically\n\nWalk every bed weekly, checking leaf undersides, stems, new growth, and soil around the plant. Look for chewed edges, stippling, sticky residue, wilt, and clusters of insects. Identify the pest before choosing a treatment; insect damage, nutrient problems, and plant diseases can look similar. Photograph unfamiliar symptoms and ask the garden coordinator before taking action.\n\n## Prevent Problems\n\nRemove diseased leaves promptly and discard them according to the garden’s rules. Use row cover over crops that need protection, keep plants spaced for airflow, and rotate crop families between beds. Water at the soil line and clear fallen fruit that attracts pests. Encourage predators with flowers and avoid broad-spectrum products that harm them.\n\n## Treat Carefully\n\nFirst try hand-picking, a water spray, pruning, or a barrier. If a product is approved, read its label, protect pollinators, spray only the affected crop, and follow the re-entry and harvest interval. Never apply a neighbor’s unlabelled mixture. Log the date, product, bed, and result so future decisions improve."]],["extensions",["object",879,"object-prototype",[]]],["status",["string","stable"]]]]]]]],["18",["object",880,"object-prototype",[["conformance",["string","strict"]],["identity",["object",881,"object-prototype",[["path",["string","gardens/pollinator-habitat.md"]],["documentId",["string","gardens/pollinator-habitat"]]]]],["type",["string","concept"]],["metadata",["object",882,"object-prototype",[["title",["string","Pollinator Habitat"]],["tags",["array",883,3,[["0",["string","pollinators"]],["1",["string","flowers"]],["2",["string","biodiversity"]]]]],["sourceText",["string",""]]]]],["facets",["object",884,"object-prototype",[["status",["object",885,"object-prototype",[["classified",["boolean",true]],["value",["string","stable"]]]]],["trust",["object",886,"object-prototype",[["classified",["boolean",true]],["value",["string","unverified"]]]]],["staleness",["object",887,"object-prototype",[["classified",["boolean",true]]]]]]]],["sections",["array",888,4,[["0",["object",889,"object-prototype",[["id",["string","gardens/pollinator-habitat#pollinator-habitat"]],["headingPath",["string","Pollinator Habitat"]],["text",["string","A pollinator area gives bees, butterflies, and beneficial insects food and shelter while supporting garden crops. Plan a continuous sequence of blooms from spring through fall, and choose plants that suit the site instead of buying a decorative mix that may not thrive. Keep flowering plants separate from crops that need frequent spraying or digging."]],["startLine",["number","4024000000000000"]],["endLine",["number","4028000000000000"]]]]],["1",["object",890,"object-prototype",[["id",["string","gardens/pollinator-habitat#pollinator-habitat-choose-and-arrange-plants"]],["headingPath",["string","Pollinator Habitat > Choose and Arrange Plants"]],["text",["string","Combine early flowers such as willow, crocus, or native spring perennials with summer herbs, sunflowers, and fall asters. Cluster each species in patches so insects can find it easily. Include plants with different flower shapes and leave some stems hollow or pithy for nesting. Provide a shallow dish of stones and water, refreshed often, rather than a deep open container."]],["startLine",["number","402c000000000000"]],["endLine",["number","4030000000000000"]]]]],["2",["object",891,"object-prototype",[["id",["string","gardens/pollinator-habitat#pollinator-habitat-tend-the-habitat"]],["headingPath",["string","Pollinator Habitat > Tend the Habitat"]],["text",["string","Avoid pesticides in the pollinator bed, especially when flowers are open. Hand-pull weeds before they seed, but leave a few non-invasive host plants when they support caterpillars. Do not deadhead every plant in autumn; seed heads feed birds and standing stems shelter insects. Cut old growth in late winter after temperatures rise, and compost only material that is free of disease."]],["startLine",["number","4032000000000000"]],["endLine",["number","4034000000000000"]]]]],["3",["object",892,"object-prototype",[["id",["string","gardens/pollinator-habitat#pollinator-habitat-share-the-space"]],["headingPath",["string","Pollinator Habitat > Share the Space"]],["text",["string","Post a small sign naming bloom periods and asking visitors not to disturb nesting areas. A monthly count of visiting insects can guide future plant choices and help children participate in stewardship."]],["startLine",["number","4036000000000000"]],["endLine",["number","4038000000000000"]]]]]]]],["diagnostics",["array",893,0,[]]],["document",["object",894,"object-prototype",[["id",["string","gardens/pollinator-habitat"]],["type",["string","concept"]],["title",["string","Pollinator Habitat"]],["tags",["array",895,3,[["0",["string","pollinators"]],["1",["string","flowers"]],["2",["string","biodiversity"]]]]],["sources",["array",896,0,[]]],["verified",["array",897,0,[]]],["body",["string","# Pollinator Habitat\n\nA pollinator area gives bees, butterflies, and beneficial insects food and shelter while supporting garden crops. Plan a continuous sequence of blooms from spring through fall, and choose plants that suit the site instead of buying a decorative mix that may not thrive. Keep flowering plants separate from crops that need frequent spraying or digging.\n\n## Choose and Arrange Plants\n\nCombine early flowers such as willow, crocus, or native spring perennials with summer herbs, sunflowers, and fall asters. Cluster each species in patches so insects can find it easily. Include plants with different flower shapes and leave some stems hollow or pithy for nesting. Provide a shallow dish of stones and water, refreshed often, rather than a deep open container.\n\n## Tend the Habitat\n\nAvoid pesticides in the pollinator bed, especially when flowers are open. Hand-pull weeds before they seed, but leave a few non-invasive host plants when they support caterpillars. Do not deadhead every plant in autumn; seed heads feed birds and standing stems shelter insects. Cut old growth in late winter after temperatures rise, and compost only material that is free of disease.\n\n## Share the Space\n\nPost a small sign naming bloom periods and asking visitors not to disturb nesting areas. A monthly count of visiting insects can guide future plant choices and help children participate in stewardship."]],["extensions",["object",898,"object-prototype",[]]],["status",["string","stable"]]]]]]]],["19",["object",899,"object-prototype",[["conformance",["string","strict"]],["identity",["object",900,"object-prototype",[["path",["string","gardens/seasonal-planning.md"]],["documentId",["string","gardens/seasonal-planning"]]]]],["type",["string","concept"]],["metadata",["object",901,"object-prototype",[["title",["string","Seasonal Planning"]],["tags",["array",902,3,[["0",["string","planning"]],["1",["string","seasons"]],["2",["string","crops"]]]]],["sourceText",["string",""]]]]],["facets",["object",903,"object-prototype",[["status",["object",904,"object-prototype",[["classified",["boolean",true]],["value",["string","stable"]]]]],["trust",["object",905,"object-prototype",[["classified",["boolean",true]],["value",["string","unverified"]]]]],["staleness",["object",906,"object-prototype",[["classified",["boolean",true]]]]]]]],["sections",["array",907,4,[["0",["object",908,"object-prototype",[["id",["string","gardens/seasonal-planning#seasonal-planning"]],["headingPath",["string","Seasonal Planning"]],["text",["string","Seasonal planning turns a shared garden’s beds, tools, and volunteer time into a manageable crop calendar. Begin with the harvests members want, then fit varieties to the site, last-frost estimate, water capacity, and labor. Leave room for weather delays instead of treating every date as fixed."]],["startLine",["number","4024000000000000"]],["endLine",["number","4028000000000000"]]]]],["1",["object",909,"object-prototype",[["id",["string","gardens/seasonal-planning#seasonal-planning-map-the-year"]],["headingPath",["string","Seasonal Planning > Map the Year"]],["text",["string","Draw the beds and note sun, shade, paths, irrigation zones, and perennial plants. For each crop, record days to maturity, preferred temperature, spacing, and whether it follows or precedes another crop. Schedule cool-season greens and peas before warm-season tomatoes, peppers, or beans. Reserve at least one bed for flowers, cover crops, or a late-season community planting."]],["startLine",["number","402c000000000000"]],["endLine",["number","4030000000000000"]]]]],["2",["object",910,"object-prototype",[["id",["string","gardens/seasonal-planning#seasonal-planning-stagger-and-share"]],["headingPath",["string","Seasonal Planning > Stagger and Share"]],["text",["string","Sow small batches of quick crops every two or three weeks rather than planting the whole bed at once. Staggering harvests prevents a glut and spreads watering and picking tasks. Post the plan at the shed, identify a bed steward, and mark who will start, transplant, weed, and harvest each crop. Use a shared calendar for workdays and irrigation changes."]],["startLine",["number","4032000000000000"]],["endLine",["number","4034000000000000"]]]]],["3",["object",911,"object-prototype",[["id",["string","gardens/seasonal-planning#seasonal-planning-review-the-plan"]],["headingPath",["string","Seasonal Planning > Review the Plan"]],["text",["string","At the end of each season, compare intended and actual harvest dates, note varieties that struggled, and record empty periods or overfilled beds. Carry useful observations into the next calendar, but change one or two variables at a time so the garden learns what actually improved."]],["startLine",["number","4036000000000000"]],["endLine",["number","4038000000000000"]]]]]]]],["diagnostics",["array",912,0,[]]],["document",["object",913,"object-prototype",[["id",["string","gardens/seasonal-planning"]],["type",["string","concept"]],["title",["string","Seasonal Planning"]],["tags",["array",914,3,[["0",["string","planning"]],["1",["string","seasons"]],["2",["string","crops"]]]]],["sources",["array",915,0,[]]],["verified",["array",916,0,[]]],["body",["string","# Seasonal Planning\n\nSeasonal planning turns a shared garden’s beds, tools, and volunteer time into a manageable crop calendar. Begin with the harvests members want, then fit varieties to the site, last-frost estimate, water capacity, and labor. Leave room for weather delays instead of treating every date as fixed.\n\n## Map the Year\n\nDraw the beds and note sun, shade, paths, irrigation zones, and perennial plants. For each crop, record days to maturity, preferred temperature, spacing, and whether it follows or precedes another crop. Schedule cool-season greens and peas before warm-season tomatoes, peppers, or beans. Reserve at least one bed for flowers, cover crops, or a late-season community planting.\n\n## Stagger and Share\n\nSow small batches of quick crops every two or three weeks rather than planting the whole bed at once. Staggering harvests prevents a glut and spreads watering and picking tasks. Post the plan at the shed, identify a bed steward, and mark who will start, transplant, weed, and harvest each crop. Use a shared calendar for workdays and irrigation changes.\n\n## Review the Plan\n\nAt the end of each season, compare intended and actual harvest dates, note varieties that struggled, and record empty periods or overfilled beds. Carry useful observations into the next calendar, but change one or two variables at a time so the garden learns what actually improved."]],["extensions",["object",917,"object-prototype",[]]],["status",["string","stable"]]]]]]]],["20",["object",918,"object-prototype",[["conformance",["string","strict"]],["identity",["object",919,"object-prototype",[["path",["string","gardens/seed-starting.md"]],["documentId",["string","gardens/seed-starting"]]]]],["type",["string","concept"]],["metadata",["object",920,"object-prototype",[["title",["string","Seed Starting"]],["tags",["array",921,3,[["0",["string","seeds"]],["1",["string","seedlings"]],["2",["string","propagation"]]]]],["sourceText",["string",""]]]]],["facets",["object",922,"object-prototype",[["status",["object",923,"object-prototype",[["classified",["boolean",true]],["value",["string","stable"]]]]],["trust",["object",924,"object-prototype",[["classified",["boolean",true]],["value",["string","unverified"]]]]],["staleness",["object",925,"object-prototype",[["classified",["boolean",true]]]]]]]],["sections",["array",926,4,[["0",["object",927,"object-prototype",[["id",["string","gardens/seed-starting#seed-starting"]],["headingPath",["string","Seed Starting"]],["text",["string","Starting seeds indoors lets a community garden grow reliable transplants and spread limited greenhouse space fairly. Match the start date to the crop and the local last-frost estimate, then count backward from the date seedlings should move outside. Keep a log of variety, sowing date, germination, and transplant performance."]],["startLine",["number","4024000000000000"]],["endLine",["number","4028000000000000"]]]]],["1",["object",928,"object-prototype",[["id",["string","gardens/seed-starting#seed-starting-sow-evenly"]],["headingPath",["string","Seed Starting > Sow Evenly"]],["text",["string","Use clean trays or reused pots with drainage holes and a fine, damp seed-starting mix. Plant at the depth on the packet; tiny seeds often need light or only a dusting of mix. Label every container with crop and variety. Cover trays to hold moisture, but uncover them as soon as most seedlings emerge so stems do not stretch or rot."]],["startLine",["number","402c000000000000"]],["endLine",["number","4030000000000000"]]]]],["2",["object",929,"object-prototype",[["id",["string","gardens/seed-starting#seed-starting-give-seedlings-what-they-need"]],["headingPath",["string","Seed Starting > Give Seedlings What They Need"]],["text",["string","Place seedlings under a light close enough to prevent leaning, and run it on a consistent daily schedule. Water from below or with a gentle stream when the surface begins to dry. A small fan moving air across the leaves encourages sturdy stems and lowers disease risk. Thin crowded starts by snipping extras rather than pulling neighboring roots."]],["startLine",["number","4032000000000000"]],["endLine",["number","4034000000000000"]]]]],["3",["object",930,"object-prototype",[["id",["string","gardens/seed-starting#seed-starting-harden-off"]],["headingPath",["string","Seed Starting > Harden Off"]],["text",["string","About a week before planting, move trays outdoors for increasing periods in sheltered shade. Gradually introduce sun and wind, and bring them in during cold nights. Transplant after the bed is prepared and the weather matches the crop, then water thoroughly."]],["startLine",["number","4036000000000000"]],["endLine",["number","4038000000000000"]]]]]]]],["diagnostics",["array",931,0,[]]],["document",["object",932,"object-prototype",[["id",["string","gardens/seed-starting"]],["type",["string","concept"]],["title",["string","Seed Starting"]],["tags",["array",933,3,[["0",["string","seeds"]],["1",["string","seedlings"]],["2",["string","propagation"]]]]],["sources",["array",934,0,[]]],["verified",["array",935,0,[]]],["body",["string","# Seed Starting\n\nStarting seeds indoors lets a community garden grow reliable transplants and spread limited greenhouse space fairly. Match the start date to the crop and the local last-frost estimate, then count backward from the date seedlings should move outside. Keep a log of variety, sowing date, germination, and transplant performance.\n\n## Sow Evenly\n\nUse clean trays or reused pots with drainage holes and a fine, damp seed-starting mix. Plant at the depth on the packet; tiny seeds often need light or only a dusting of mix. Label every container with crop and variety. Cover trays to hold moisture, but uncover them as soon as most seedlings emerge so stems do not stretch or rot.\n\n## Give Seedlings What They Need\n\nPlace seedlings under a light close enough to prevent leaning, and run it on a consistent daily schedule. Water from below or with a gentle stream when the surface begins to dry. A small fan moving air across the leaves encourages sturdy stems and lowers disease risk. Thin crowded starts by snipping extras rather than pulling neighboring roots.\n\n## Harden Off\n\nAbout a week before planting, move trays outdoors for increasing periods in sheltered shade. Gradually introduce sun and wind, and bring them in during cold nights. Transplant after the bed is prepared and the weather matches the crop, then water thoroughly."]],["extensions",["object",936,"object-prototype",[]]],["status",["string","stable"]]]]]]]],["21",["object",937,"object-prototype",[["conformance",["string","strict"]],["identity",["object",938,"object-prototype",[["path",["string","gardens/soil-health.md"]],["documentId",["string","gardens/soil-health"]]]]],["type",["string","concept"]],["metadata",["object",939,"object-prototype",[["title",["string","Soil Health"]],["tags",["array",940,3,[["0",["string","soil"]],["1",["string","nutrients"]],["2",["string","gardening"]]]]],["sourceText",["string",""]]]]],["facets",["object",941,"object-prototype",[["status",["object",942,"object-prototype",[["classified",["boolean",true]],["value",["string","stable"]]]]],["trust",["object",943,"object-prototype",[["classified",["boolean",true]],["value",["string","unverified"]]]]],["staleness",["object",944,"object-prototype",[["classified",["boolean",true]]]]]]]],["sections",["array",945,4,[["0",["object",946,"object-prototype",[["id",["string","gardens/soil-health#soil-health"]],["headingPath",["string","Soil Health"]],["text",["string","Healthy soil gives the community garden strong roots, steady growth, and fewer irrigation problems. Treat it as a living system rather than an inert planting medium. Observe it each season, add organic matter gradually, and avoid practices that leave beds bare or compacted."]],["startLine",["number","4024000000000000"]],["endLine",["number","4028000000000000"]]]]],["1",["object",947,"object-prototype",[["id",["string","gardens/soil-health#soil-health-check-before-planting"]],["headingPath",["string","Soil Health > Check Before Planting"]],["text",["string","Squeeze a handful of moist soil. It should form a loose ball that crumbles when tapped, not a sticky lump or dusty handful. Look for earthworms, roots, and a range of crumb sizes. A simple soil test can show pH and available nutrients; record results by bed so amendments are based on evidence. Never add lime, sulfur, or fertilizer just because a neighboring plot used it."]],["startLine",["number","402c000000000000"]],["endLine",["number","4030000000000000"]]]]],["2",["object",948,"object-prototype",[["id",["string","gardens/soil-health#soil-health-build-fertility"]],["headingPath",["string","Soil Health > Build Fertility"]],["text",["string","Spread finished compost in a thin, even layer before planting, then mix it into only the top few inches if the bed needs incorporation. Use cover crops or leaf mulch to feed organisms and reduce erosion. Rotate heavy feeders such as tomatoes with beans or leafy greens, and leave plant residue only when it is healthy."]],["startLine",["number","4032000000000000"]],["endLine",["number","4034000000000000"]]]]],["3",["object",949,"object-prototype",[["id",["string","gardens/soil-health#soil-health-protect-the-bed"]],["headingPath",["string","Soil Health > Protect the Bed"]],["text",["string","Keep foot traffic on paths, not growing soil. Use boards or designated stepping stones for shared beds. Mulch exposed ground, and water slowly enough that soil structure stays intact."]],["startLine",["number","4036000000000000"]],["endLine",["number","4038000000000000"]]]]]]]],["diagnostics",["array",950,0,[]]],["document",["object",951,"object-prototype",[["id",["string","gardens/soil-health"]],["type",["string","concept"]],["title",["string","Soil Health"]],["tags",["array",952,3,[["0",["string","soil"]],["1",["string","nutrients"]],["2",["string","gardening"]]]]],["sources",["array",953,0,[]]],["verified",["array",954,0,[]]],["body",["string","# Soil Health\n\nHealthy soil gives the community garden strong roots, steady growth, and fewer irrigation problems. Treat it as a living system rather than an inert planting medium. Observe it each season, add organic matter gradually, and avoid practices that leave beds bare or compacted.\n\n## Check Before Planting\n\nSqueeze a handful of moist soil. It should form a loose ball that crumbles when tapped, not a sticky lump or dusty handful. Look for earthworms, roots, and a range of crumb sizes. A simple soil test can show pH and available nutrients; record results by bed so amendments are based on evidence. Never add lime, sulfur, or fertilizer just because a neighboring plot used it.\n\n## Build Fertility\n\nSpread finished compost in a thin, even layer before planting, then mix it into only the top few inches if the bed needs incorporation. Use cover crops or leaf mulch to feed organisms and reduce erosion. Rotate heavy feeders such as tomatoes with beans or leafy greens, and leave plant residue only when it is healthy.\n\n## Protect the Bed\n\nKeep foot traffic on paths, not growing soil. Use boards or designated stepping stones for shared beds. Mulch exposed ground, and water slowly enough that soil structure stays intact."]],["extensions",["object",955,"object-prototype",[]]],["status",["string","stable"]]]]]]]],["22",["object",956,"object-prototype",[["conformance",["string","strict"]],["identity",["object",957,"object-prototype",[["path",["string","observatory/calibration-frames.md"]],["documentId",["string","observatory/calibration-frames"]]]]],["type",["string","concept"]],["metadata",["object",958,"object-prototype",[["title",["string","Calibration Frames"]],["tags",["array",959,3,[["0",["string","calibration"]],["1",["string","imaging"]],["2",["string","detectors"]]]]],["sourceText",["string",""]]]]],["facets",["object",960,"object-prototype",[["status",["object",961,"object-prototype",[["classified",["boolean",true]],["value",["string","draft"]]]]],["trust",["object",962,"object-prototype",[["classified",["boolean",true]],["value",["string","unverified"]]]]],["staleness",["object",963,"object-prototype",[["classified",["boolean",true]]]]]]]],["sections",["array",964,4,[["0",["object",965,"object-prototype",[["id",["string","observatory/calibration-frames#calibration-frames"]],["headingPath",["string","Calibration Frames"]],["text",["string",""]],["startLine",["number","4024000000000000"]],["endLine",["number","4024000000000000"]]]]],["1",["object",966,"object-prototype",[["id",["string","observatory/calibration-frames#calibration-frames-purpose"]],["headingPath",["string","Calibration Frames > Purpose"]],["text",["string","Calibration frames measure detector behavior so science exposures can be corrected without erasing real signal. The observatory collects bias, dark, flat, and, when spectroscopy is scheduled, arc-lamp frames for each relevant instrument setup."]],["startLine",["number","4028000000000000"]],["endLine",["number","402a000000000000"]]]]],["2",["object",967,"object-prototype",[["id",["string","observatory/calibration-frames#calibration-frames-collection"]],["headingPath",["string","Calibration Frames > Collection"]],["text",["string","Take at least  fifteen bias frames at the shortest supported exposure and the same readout mode as the science run. Capture five darks at each science exposure time, with the shutter closed and the detector at its regulated temperature. For flats, aim for counts near half the detector's linear range; take twilight flats for imaging and lamp flats for a stable spectrograph response. Change the calibration set whenever filter, binning, gain, grating, or detector temperature changes. Never combine frames from different hardware configurations merely because their filenames look similar."]],["startLine",["number","402e000000000000"]],["endLine",["number","4030000000000000"]]]]],["3",["object",968,"object-prototype",[["id",["string","observatory/calibration-frames#calibration-frames-quality-checks"]],["headingPath",["string","Calibration Frames > Quality checks"]],["text",["string","Inspect the median level, saturated-pixel fraction, gradients, and cosmic-ray contamination. Reject frames with shutter motion, unstable lamp output, or counts outside the linear range. Store masters with their input list, combine method, temperature, and processing version. Link the master set to the science sequence before handoff so reduction software can select it reproducibly."]],["startLine",["number","4032000000000000"]],["endLine",["number","4033000000000000"]]]]]]]],["diagnostics",["array",969,0,[]]],["document",["object",970,"object-prototype",[["id",["string","observatory/calibration-frames"]],["type",["string","concept"]],["title",["string","Calibration Frames"]],["tags",["array",971,3,[["0",["string","calibration"]],["1",["string","imaging"]],["2",["string","detectors"]]]]],["sources",["array",972,0,[]]],["verified",["array",973,0,[]]],["body",["string","# Calibration Frames\n\n## Purpose\nCalibration frames measure detector behavior so science exposures can be corrected without erasing real signal. The observatory collects bias, dark, flat, and, when spectroscopy is scheduled, arc-lamp frames for each relevant instrument setup.\n\n## Collection\nTake at least  fifteen bias frames at the shortest supported exposure and the same readout mode as the science run. Capture five darks at each science exposure time, with the shutter closed and the detector at its regulated temperature. For flats, aim for counts near half the detector's linear range; take twilight flats for imaging and lamp flats for a stable spectrograph response. Change the calibration set whenever filter, binning, gain, grating, or detector temperature changes. Never combine frames from different hardware configurations merely because their filenames look similar.\n\n## Quality checks\nInspect the median level, saturated-pixel fraction, gradients, and cosmic-ray contamination. Reject frames with shutter motion, unstable lamp output, or counts outside the linear range. Store masters with their input list, combine method, temperature, and processing version. Link the master set to the science sequence before handoff so reduction software can select it reproducibly.\n"]],["extensions",["object",974,"object-prototype",[]]],["status",["string","draft"]]]]]]]],["23",["object",975,"object-prototype",[["conformance",["string","strict"]],["identity",["object",976,"object-prototype",[["path",["string","observatory/data-handoff.md"]],["documentId",["string","observatory/data-handoff"]]]]],["type",["string","concept"]],["metadata",["object",977,"object-prototype",[["title",["string","Data Handoff"]],["tags",["array",978,3,[["0",["string","data"]],["1",["string","provenance"]],["2",["string","handoff"]]]]],["sourceText",["string",""]]]]],["facets",["object",979,"object-prototype",[["status",["object",980,"object-prototype",[["classified",["boolean",true]],["value",["string","stable"]]]]],["trust",["object",981,"object-prototype",[["classified",["boolean",true]],["value",["string","unverified"]]]]],["staleness",["object",982,"object-prototype",[["classified",["boolean",true]]]]]]]],["sections",["array",983,4,[["0",["object",984,"object-prototype",[["id",["string","observatory/data-handoff#data-handoff"]],["headingPath",["string","Data Handoff"]],["text",["string",""]],["startLine",["number","4024000000000000"]],["endLine",["number","4024000000000000"]]]]],["1",["object",985,"object-prototype",[["id",["string","observatory/data-handoff#data-handoff-purpose"]],["headingPath",["string","Data Handoff > Purpose"]],["text",["string","A data handoff transfers an observing night from the control room to the person reducing or archiving it. The handoff should let another operator identify what happened without relying on memory or private messages."]],["startLine",["number","4028000000000000"]],["endLine",["number","402a000000000000"]]]]],["2",["object",986,"object-prototype",[["id",["string","observatory/data-handoff#data-handoff-package-contents"]],["headingPath",["string","Data Handoff > Package contents"]],["text",["string","Place raw files in a read-only night directory named with the observing date and run identifier. Include the final schedule, night log, target list, calibration frames, instrument configuration, weather summary, and any alert messages. Generate a file manifest with relative paths, byte sizes, checksums, and acquisition timestamps. Separate successful, partial, and failed sequences, but retain failed files when they explain a gap or instrument problem."]],["startLine",["number","402e000000000000"]],["endLine",["number","4030000000000000"]]]]],["3",["object",987,"object-prototype",[["id",["string","observatory/data-handoff#data-handoff-review-and-receipt"]],["headingPath",["string","Data Handoff > Review and receipt"]],["text",["string","The observer reviews filenames, target coordinates, filters, exposure times, and detector temperature before announcing the package. Add a README that states known defects, missing calibrations, clock concerns, and the software version used at acquisition. The recipient verifies the checksum and confirms receipt in the handoff record. Corrections are made by adding a dated note or replacement file; do not rewrite raw data in place. Keep the original package and its receipt together in the archive."]],["startLine",["number","4032000000000000"]],["endLine",["number","4033000000000000"]]]]]]]],["diagnostics",["array",988,0,[]]],["document",["object",989,"object-prototype",[["id",["string","observatory/data-handoff"]],["type",["string","concept"]],["title",["string","Data Handoff"]],["tags",["array",990,3,[["0",["string","data"]],["1",["string","provenance"]],["2",["string","handoff"]]]]],["sources",["array",991,0,[]]],["verified",["array",992,0,[]]],["body",["string","# Data Handoff\n\n## Purpose\nA data handoff transfers an observing night from the control room to the person reducing or archiving it. The handoff should let another operator identify what happened without relying on memory or private messages.\n\n## Package contents\nPlace raw files in a read-only night directory named with the observing date and run identifier. Include the final schedule, night log, target list, calibration frames, instrument configuration, weather summary, and any alert messages. Generate a file manifest with relative paths, byte sizes, checksums, and acquisition timestamps. Separate successful, partial, and failed sequences, but retain failed files when they explain a gap or instrument problem.\n\n## Review and receipt\nThe observer reviews filenames, target coordinates, filters, exposure times, and detector temperature before announcing the package. Add a README that states known defects, missing calibrations, clock concerns, and the software version used at acquisition. The recipient verifies the checksum and confirms receipt in the handoff record. Corrections are made by adding a dated note or replacement file; do not rewrite raw data in place. Keep the original package and its receipt together in the archive.\n"]],["extensions",["object",993,"object-prototype",[]]],["status",["string","stable"]]]]]]]],["24",["object",994,"object-prototype",[["conformance",["string","strict"]],["identity",["object",995,"object-prototype",[["path",["string","observatory/dome-shutdown.md"]],["documentId",["string","observatory/dome-shutdown"]]]]],["type",["string","concept"]],["metadata",["object",996,"object-prototype",[["title",["string","Dome Shutdown"]],["tags",["array",997,3,[["0",["string","dome"]],["1",["string","shutdown"]],["2",["string","safety"]]]]],["sourceText",["string",""]]]]],["facets",["object",998,"object-prototype",[["status",["object",999,"object-prototype",[["classified",["boolean",true]],["value",["string","stable"]]]]],["trust",["object",1000,"object-prototype",[["classified",["boolean",true]],["value",["string","unverified"]]]]],["staleness",["object",1001,"object-prototype",[["classified",["boolean",true]]]]]]]],["sections",["array",1002,4,[["0",["object",1003,"object-prototype",[["id",["string","observatory/dome-shutdown#dome-shutdown"]],["headingPath",["string","Dome Shutdown"]],["text",["string",""]],["startLine",["number","4024000000000000"]],["endLine",["number","4024000000000000"]]]]],["1",["object",1004,"object-prototype",[["id",["string","observatory/dome-shutdown#dome-shutdown-purpose"]],["headingPath",["string","Dome Shutdown > Purpose"]],["text",["string","Dome shutdown leaves the observatory dry, powered safely, and ready for the next crew. The duty astronomer starts it after the final exposure, or immediately when a weather gate requires closure."]],["startLine",["number","4028000000000000"]],["endLine",["number","402a000000000000"]]]]],["2",["object",1005,"object-prototype",[["id",["string","observatory/dome-shutdown#dome-shutdown-sequence"]],["headingPath",["string","Dome Shutdown > Sequence"]],["text",["string","Stop acquisitions and wait for the camera to finish writing. Park the mount at its marked home position, return the instrument selector to its safe setting, and cap the telescope and finder. Remove temporary cables from walkways and secure the remaining loops so the dome cannot catch them. Close and latch the shutter, then switch off nonessential instrument power. Keep ventilation or environmental controls running when required by the camera or building plan; shutdown is not the same as turning off every breaker."]],["startLine",["number","402e000000000000"]],["endLine",["number","4030000000000000"]]]]],["3",["object",1006,"object-prototype",[["id",["string","observatory/dome-shutdown#dome-shutdown-verification"]],["headingPath",["string","Dome Shutdown > Verification"]],["text",["string","Walk the room with a flashlight: check that no person or loose object remains in the rotation path, the floor is dry, emergency access is clear, and the rain sensor is unobstructed. Confirm the shutter indicator agrees with the physical latch and record the final humidity, temperature, mount state, and time. If any step fails, leave the affected system in its safest known state, call the site lead, and document the fault before leaving."]],["startLine",["number","4032000000000000"]],["endLine",["number","4033000000000000"]]]]]]]],["diagnostics",["array",1007,0,[]]],["document",["object",1008,"object-prototype",[["id",["string","observatory/dome-shutdown"]],["type",["string","concept"]],["title",["string","Dome Shutdown"]],["tags",["array",1009,3,[["0",["string","dome"]],["1",["string","shutdown"]],["2",["string","safety"]]]]],["sources",["array",1010,0,[]]],["verified",["array",1011,0,[]]],["body",["string","# Dome Shutdown\n\n## Purpose\nDome shutdown leaves the observatory dry, powered safely, and ready for the next crew. The duty astronomer starts it after the final exposure, or immediately when a weather gate requires closure.\n\n## Sequence\nStop acquisitions and wait for the camera to finish writing. Park the mount at its marked home position, return the instrument selector to its safe setting, and cap the telescope and finder. Remove temporary cables from walkways and secure the remaining loops so the dome cannot catch them. Close and latch the shutter, then switch off nonessential instrument power. Keep ventilation or environmental controls running when required by the camera or building plan; shutdown is not the same as turning off every breaker.\n\n## Verification\nWalk the room with a flashlight: check that no person or loose object remains in the rotation path, the floor is dry, emergency access is clear, and the rain sensor is unobstructed. Confirm the shutter indicator agrees with the physical latch and record the final humidity, temperature, mount state, and time. If any step fails, leave the affected system in its safest known state, call the site lead, and document the fault before leaving.\n"]],["extensions",["object",1012,"object-prototype",[]]],["status",["string","stable"]]]]]]]],["25",["object",1013,"object-prototype",[["conformance",["string","strict"]],["identity",["object",1014,"object-prototype",[["path",["string","observatory/equipment-inventory.md"]],["documentId",["string","observatory/equipment-inventory"]]]]],["type",["string","concept"]],["metadata",["object",1015,"object-prototype",[["title",["string","Equipment Inventory"]],["tags",["array",1016,3,[["0",["string","equipment"]],["1",["string","inventory"]],["2",["string","maintenance"]]]]],["sourceText",["string",""]]]]],["facets",["object",1017,"object-prototype",[["status",["object",1018,"object-prototype",[["classified",["boolean",true]],["value",["string","stable"]]]]],["trust",["object",1019,"object-prototype",[["classified",["boolean",true]],["value",["string","unverified"]]]]],["staleness",["object",1020,"object-prototype",[["classified",["boolean",true]]]]]]]],["sections",["array",1021,4,[["0",["object",1022,"object-prototype",[["id",["string","observatory/equipment-inventory#equipment-inventory"]],["headingPath",["string","Equipment Inventory"]],["text",["string",""]],["startLine",["number","4024000000000000"]],["endLine",["number","4024000000000000"]]]]],["1",["object",1023,"object-prototype",[["id",["string","observatory/equipment-inventory#equipment-inventory-purpose"]],["headingPath",["string","Equipment Inventory > Purpose"]],["text",["string","The equipment inventory is the current map of observatory hardware, consumables, and loaned accessories. It lets operators find a compatible component quickly and gives maintenance staff a traceable history when something fails."]],["startLine",["number","4028000000000000"]],["endLine",["number","402a000000000000"]]]]],["2",["object",1024,"object-prototype",[["id",["string","observatory/equipment-inventory#equipment-inventory-required-fields"]],["headingPath",["string","Equipment Inventory > Required fields"]],["text",["string","Give every item a stable inventory identifier and record manufacturer, model, serial number, location, owner, condition, and last inspection date. For cameras and mounts, include firmware version and the cables or adapters required for normal operation. Mark safety-critical items such as ladders, extinguishers, and lifting straps with inspection due dates. A missing serial number is recorded as unknown rather than guessed from a label or purchase order."]],["startLine",["number","402e000000000000"]],["endLine",["number","4030000000000000"]]]]],["3",["object",1025,"object-prototype",[["id",["string","observatory/equipment-inventory#equipment-inventory-updates-and-checks"]],["headingPath",["string","Equipment Inventory > Updates and checks"]],["text",["string","Update the record when an item moves, is repaired, loaned, retired, or found damaged. Attach a short note and work-order reference to each change. At the start of each quarter, compare the list with a physical count and resolve duplicate identifiers. Quarantine unsafe equipment with a visible tag and remove it from the available pool. Before an observing run, the operator checks the requested setup against this inventory and reports substitutions in the night log."]],["startLine",["number","4032000000000000"]],["endLine",["number","4033000000000000"]]]]]]]],["diagnostics",["array",1026,0,[]]],["document",["object",1027,"object-prototype",[["id",["string","observatory/equipment-inventory"]],["type",["string","concept"]],["title",["string","Equipment Inventory"]],["tags",["array",1028,3,[["0",["string","equipment"]],["1",["string","inventory"]],["2",["string","maintenance"]]]]],["sources",["array",1029,0,[]]],["verified",["array",1030,0,[]]],["body",["string","# Equipment Inventory\n\n## Purpose\nThe equipment inventory is the current map of observatory hardware, consumables, and loaned accessories. It lets operators find a compatible component quickly and gives maintenance staff a traceable history when something fails.\n\n## Required fields\nGive every item a stable inventory identifier and record manufacturer, model, serial number, location, owner, condition, and last inspection date. For cameras and mounts, include firmware version and the cables or adapters required for normal operation. Mark safety-critical items such as ladders, extinguishers, and lifting straps with inspection due dates. A missing serial number is recorded as unknown rather than guessed from a label or purchase order.\n\n## Updates and checks\nUpdate the record when an item moves, is repaired, loaned, retired, or found damaged. Attach a short note and work-order reference to each change. At the start of each quarter, compare the list with a physical count and resolve duplicate identifiers. Quarantine unsafe equipment with a visible tag and remove it from the available pool. Before an observing run, the operator checks the requested setup against this inventory and reports substitutions in the night log.\n"]],["extensions",["object",1031,"object-prototype",[]]],["status",["string","stable"]]]]]]]],["26",["object",1032,"object-prototype",[["conformance",["string","strict"]],["identity",["object",1033,"object-prototype",[["path",["string","observatory/night-schedule.md"]],["documentId",["string","observatory/night-schedule"]]]]],["type",["string","concept"]],["metadata",["object",1034,"object-prototype",[["title",["string","Night Schedule"]],["tags",["array",1035,3,[["0",["string","scheduling"]],["1",["string","observing"]],["2",["string","operations"]]]]],["sourceText",["string",""]]]]],["facets",["object",1036,"object-prototype",[["status",["object",1037,"object-prototype",[["classified",["boolean",true]],["value",["string","stable"]]]]],["trust",["object",1038,"object-prototype",[["classified",["boolean",true]],["value",["string","unverified"]]]]],["staleness",["object",1039,"object-prototype",[["classified",["boolean",true]]]]]]]],["sections",["array",1040,4,[["0",["object",1041,"object-prototype",[["id",["string","observatory/night-schedule#night-schedule"]],["headingPath",["string","Night Schedule"]],["text",["string",""]],["startLine",["number","4024000000000000"]],["endLine",["number","4024000000000000"]]]]],["1",["object",1042,"object-prototype",[["id",["string","observatory/night-schedule#night-schedule-purpose"]],["headingPath",["string","Night Schedule > Purpose"]],["text",["string","A night schedule turns competing science, maintenance, and outreach requests into an executable sequence. The duty astronomer owns the schedule after the afternoon handoff and may reorder it when conditions or instrument health change."]],["startLine",["number","4028000000000000"]],["endLine",["number","402a000000000000"]]]]],["2",["object",1043,"object-prototype",[["id",["string","observatory/night-schedule#night-schedule-planning"]],["headingPath",["string","Night Schedule > Planning"]],["text",["string","Reserve the first 30 minutes for startup, alignment, and a focus check. Group targets by sky position to limit dome rotation and unnecessary slews. Place time-critical observations, such as an exoplanet ingress or a transient follow-up, ahead of flexible deep fields. Add a ten-minute buffer after every instrument change and a longer weather buffer near the horizon. Each block names a target, filter or grating, exposure plan, cadence, estimated duration, and required calibration."]],["startLine",["number","402e000000000000"]],["endLine",["number","4030000000000000"]]]]],["3",["object",1044,"object-prototype",[["id",["string","observatory/night-schedule#night-schedule-handoff"]],["headingPath",["string","Night Schedule > Handoff"]],["text",["string","Publish the schedule before sunset in the observatory workspace and print one copy for the control room. During the night, mark each block complete, skipped, or interrupted with a short reason. A replacement block must state what it displaces. At dawn, attach the final schedule to the data handoff so analysts can distinguish planned gaps from instrument failures."]],["startLine",["number","4032000000000000"]],["endLine",["number","4033000000000000"]]]]]]]],["diagnostics",["array",1045,0,[]]],["document",["object",1046,"object-prototype",[["id",["string","observatory/night-schedule"]],["type",["string","concept"]],["title",["string","Night Schedule"]],["tags",["array",1047,3,[["0",["string","scheduling"]],["1",["string","observing"]],["2",["string","operations"]]]]],["sources",["array",1048,0,[]]],["verified",["array",1049,0,[]]],["body",["string","# Night Schedule\n\n## Purpose\nA night schedule turns competing science, maintenance, and outreach requests into an executable sequence. The duty astronomer owns the schedule after the afternoon handoff and may reorder it when conditions or instrument health change.\n\n## Planning\nReserve the first 30 minutes for startup, alignment, and a focus check. Group targets by sky position to limit dome rotation and unnecessary slews. Place time-critical observations, such as an exoplanet ingress or a transient follow-up, ahead of flexible deep fields. Add a ten-minute buffer after every instrument change and a longer weather buffer near the horizon. Each block names a target, filter or grating, exposure plan, cadence, estimated duration, and required calibration.\n\n## Handoff\nPublish the schedule before sunset in the observatory workspace and print one copy for the control room. During the night, mark each block complete, skipped, or interrupted with a short reason. A replacement block must state what it displaces. At dawn, attach the final schedule to the data handoff so analysts can distinguish planned gaps from instrument failures.\n"]],["extensions",["object",1050,"object-prototype",[]]],["status",["string","stable"]]]]]]]],["27",["object",1051,"object-prototype",[["conformance",["string","strict"]],["identity",["object",1052,"object-prototype",[["path",["string","observatory/spectral-imaging.md"]],["documentId",["string","observatory/spectral-imaging"]]]]],["type",["string","concept"]],["metadata",["object",1053,"object-prototype",[["title",["string","Spectral Imaging"]],["tags",["array",1054,4,[["0",["string","spectroscopy"]],["1",["string","imaging"]],["2",["string","filters"]],["3",["string","analysis"]]]]],["sourceText",["string",""]]]]],["facets",["object",1055,"object-prototype",[["status",["object",1056,"object-prototype",[["classified",["boolean",true]],["value",["string","stable"]]]]],["trust",["object",1057,"object-prototype",[["classified",["boolean",true]],["value",["string","unverified"]]]]],["staleness",["object",1058,"object-prototype",[["classified",["boolean",true]]]]]]]],["sections",["array",1059,4,[["0",["object",1060,"object-prototype",[["id",["string","observatory/spectral-imaging#spectral-imaging"]],["headingPath",["string","Spectral Imaging"]],["text",["string",""]],["startLine",["number","4026000000000000"]],["endLine",["number","4026000000000000"]]]]],["1",["object",1061,"object-prototype",[["id",["string","observatory/spectral-imaging#spectral-imaging-purpose"]],["headingPath",["string","Spectral Imaging > Purpose"]],["text",["string","Spectral imaging combines a spatial image with measurements through selected filters or a low-resolution disperser. At the observatory it is used to compare nebular structure, stellar colors, and emission-line regions with a consistent instrument setup."]],["startLine",["number","402a000000000000"]],["endLine",["number","402c000000000000"]]]]],["2",["object",1062,"object-prototype",[["id",["string","observatory/spectral-imaging#spectral-imaging-acquisition"]],["headingPath",["string","Spectral Imaging > Acquisition"]],["text",["string","Before the sequence, record the filter wheel position, grating or prism state, detector mode, focus, and target orientation. Take a short acquisition image, then center the target without changing the planned position angle. Observe filters in an order that limits airmass changes, and include a nearby standard star when calibrated fluxes are needed. Keep individual exposures below the detector's saturation limit; use repeated exposures rather than one very long frame so cosmic rays and tracking failures can be identified."]],["startLine",["number","4030000000000000"]],["endLine",["number","4031000000000000"]]]]],["3",["object",1063,"object-prototype",[["id",["string","observatory/spectral-imaging#spectral-imaging-reduction-notes"]],["headingPath",["string","Spectral Imaging > Reduction notes"]],["text",["string","Apply bias or dark correction, flat-field each filter separately, and correct bad pixels before alignment. For dispersed data, save the wavelength solution and arc exposure with the image set. Record atmospheric conditions and any filter changes in the sequence metadata. Do not compare raw counts between filters without accounting for exposure time, detector response, and standard-star calibration. Export a preview with scale, orientation, and units so a collaborator can interpret it without opening the control software."]],["startLine",["number","4033000000000000"]],["endLine",["number","4034000000000000"]]]]]]]],["diagnostics",["array",1064,0,[]]],["document",["object",1065,"object-prototype",[["id",["string","observatory/spectral-imaging"]],["type",["string","concept"]],["title",["string","Spectral Imaging"]],["tags",["array",1066,4,[["0",["string","spectroscopy"]],["1",["string","imaging"]],["2",["string","filters"]],["3",["string","analysis"]]]]],["sources",["array",1067,0,[]]],["verified",["array",1068,0,[]]],["body",["string","# Spectral Imaging\n\n## Purpose\nSpectral imaging combines a spatial image with measurements through selected filters or a low-resolution disperser. At the observatory it is used to compare nebular structure, stellar colors, and emission-line regions with a consistent instrument setup.\n\n## Acquisition\nBefore the sequence, record the filter wheel position, grating or prism state, detector mode, focus, and target orientation. Take a short acquisition image, then center the target without changing the planned position angle. Observe filters in an order that limits airmass changes, and include a nearby standard star when calibrated fluxes are needed. Keep individual exposures below the detector's saturation limit; use repeated exposures rather than one very long frame so cosmic rays and tracking failures can be identified.\n\n## Reduction notes\nApply bias or dark correction, flat-field each filter separately, and correct bad pixels before alignment. For dispersed data, save the wavelength solution and arc exposure with the image set. Record atmospheric conditions and any filter changes in the sequence metadata. Do not compare raw counts between filters without accounting for exposure time, detector response, and standard-star calibration. Export a preview with scale, orientation, and units so a collaborator can interpret it without opening the control software.\n"]],["extensions",["object",1069,"object-prototype",[]]],["status",["string","stable"]]]]]]]],["28",["object",1070,"object-prototype",[["conformance",["string","strict"]],["identity",["object",1071,"object-prototype",[["path",["string","observatory/telescope-alignment.md"]],["documentId",["string","observatory/telescope-alignment"]]]]],["type",["string","concept"]],["metadata",["object",1072,"object-prototype",[["title",["string","Telescope Alignment"]],["tags",["array",1073,3,[["0",["string","telescope"]],["1",["string","alignment"]],["2",["string","optics"]]]]],["sourceText",["string",""]]]]],["facets",["object",1074,"object-prototype",[["status",["object",1075,"object-prototype",[["classified",["boolean",true]],["value",["string","stable"]]]]],["trust",["object",1076,"object-prototype",[["classified",["boolean",true]],["value",["string","unverified"]]]]],["staleness",["object",1077,"object-prototype",[["classified",["boolean",true]]]]]]]],["sections",["array",1078,4,[["0",["object",1079,"object-prototype",[["id",["string","observatory/telescope-alignment#telescope-alignment"]],["headingPath",["string","Telescope Alignment"]],["text",["string",""]],["startLine",["number","4024000000000000"]],["endLine",["number","4024000000000000"]]]]],["1",["object",1080,"object-prototype",[["id",["string","observatory/telescope-alignment#telescope-alignment-purpose"]],["headingPath",["string","Telescope Alignment > Purpose"]],["text",["string","Telescope alignment keeps the small observatory's pointing model trustworthy across the night. The operator should align after a mount restart, a pier intervention, or any move that could disturb the optical tube. A good alignment reduces reacquisition time and prevents a target from drifting out of a narrow instrument field."]],["startLine",["number","4028000000000000"]],["endLine",["number","402a000000000000"]]]]],["2",["object",1081,"object-prototype",[["id",["string","observatory/telescope-alignment#telescope-alignment-procedure"]],["headingPath",["string","Telescope Alignment > Procedure"]],["text",["string","Level the pier check, confirm the tube and finder are firmly seated, and focus on a bright star near the night's first target. Center that star with the mount controls, then record two additional stars separated in azimuth and altitude. Save the resulting model under the date and mount serial number rather than overwriting yesterday's file. Verify the model on a fourth star: slew to it, let tracking settle for two minutes, and measure the offset in arcseconds. If the offset exceeds 30 arcseconds, repeat the alignment and inspect balance, time settings, and cable drag before observing."]],["startLine",["number","402e000000000000"]],["endLine",["number","4030000000000000"]]]]],["3",["object",1082,"object-prototype",[["id",["string","observatory/telescope-alignment#telescope-alignment-record"]],["headingPath",["string","Telescope Alignment > Record"]],["text",["string","Log the operator, alignment stars, residual offset, and model filename in the night log. Keep failed models for troubleshooting, but mark them inactive."]],["startLine",["number","4032000000000000"]],["endLine",["number","4033000000000000"]]]]]]]],["diagnostics",["array",1083,0,[]]],["document",["object",1084,"object-prototype",[["id",["string","observatory/telescope-alignment"]],["type",["string","concept"]],["title",["string","Telescope Alignment"]],["tags",["array",1085,3,[["0",["string","telescope"]],["1",["string","alignment"]],["2",["string","optics"]]]]],["sources",["array",1086,0,[]]],["verified",["array",1087,0,[]]],["body",["string","# Telescope Alignment\n\n## Purpose\nTelescope alignment keeps the small observatory's pointing model trustworthy across the night. The operator should align after a mount restart, a pier intervention, or any move that could disturb the optical tube. A good alignment reduces reacquisition time and prevents a target from drifting out of a narrow instrument field.\n\n## Procedure\nLevel the pier check, confirm the tube and finder are firmly seated, and focus on a bright star near the night's first target. Center that star with the mount controls, then record two additional stars separated in azimuth and altitude. Save the resulting model under the date and mount serial number rather than overwriting yesterday's file. Verify the model on a fourth star: slew to it, let tracking settle for two minutes, and measure the offset in arcseconds. If the offset exceeds 30 arcseconds, repeat the alignment and inspect balance, time settings, and cable drag before observing.\n\n## Record\nLog the operator, alignment stars, residual offset, and model filename in the night log. Keep failed models for troubleshooting, but mark them inactive.\n"]],["extensions",["object",1088,"object-prototype",[]]],["status",["string","stable"]]]]]]]],["29",["object",1089,"object-prototype",[["conformance",["string","strict"]],["identity",["object",1090,"object-prototype",[["path",["string","observatory/transient-alerts.md"]],["documentId",["string","observatory/transient-alerts"]]]]],["type",["string","concept"]],["metadata",["object",1091,"object-prototype",[["title",["string","Transient Alerts"]],["tags",["array",1092,3,[["0",["string","transients"]],["1",["string","alerts"]],["2",["string","follow-up"]]]]],["sourceText",["string",""]]]]],["facets",["object",1093,"object-prototype",[["status",["object",1094,"object-prototype",[["classified",["boolean",true]],["value",["string","draft"]]]]],["trust",["object",1095,"object-prototype",[["classified",["boolean",true]],["value",["string","unverified"]]]]],["staleness",["object",1096,"object-prototype",[["classified",["boolean",true]]]]]]]],["sections",["array",1097,4,[["0",["object",1098,"object-prototype",[["id",["string","observatory/transient-alerts#transient-alerts"]],["headingPath",["string","Transient Alerts"]],["text",["string",""]],["startLine",["number","4024000000000000"]],["endLine",["number","4024000000000000"]]]]],["1",["object",1099,"object-prototype",[["id",["string","observatory/transient-alerts#transient-alerts-purpose"]],["headingPath",["string","Transient Alerts > Purpose"]],["text",["string","A transient alert is a time-sensitive notice that a variable or newly appearing object may merit observation. The observatory accepts alerts from approved networks and from the on-call scientist, then records enough context to make a quick, defensible decision."]],["startLine",["number","4028000000000000"]],["endLine",["number","402a000000000000"]]]]],["2",["object",1100,"object-prototype",[["id",["string","observatory/transient-alerts#transient-alerts-triage"]],["headingPath",["string","Transient Alerts > Triage"]],["text",["string","The on-call astronomer checks the alert timestamp, coordinates, uncertainty region, brightness estimate, visibility, and scientific priority. Reject duplicates, stale positions, targets below the local horizon, and requests that conflict with a protected safety hold. If the target is observable, compare its required cadence and filters with the current schedule. The astronomer may interrupt a flexible block, but must document whose time was displaced and why."]],["startLine",["number","402e000000000000"]],["endLine",["number","4030000000000000"]]]]],["3",["object",1101,"object-prototype",[["id",["string","observatory/transient-alerts#transient-alerts-response"]],["headingPath",["string","Transient Alerts > Response"]],["text",["string","Create an observation block with the alert identifier, ephemeris source, coordinate epoch, exposure sequence, and deadline. Use a short acquisition exposure before committing to a long series. Notify the instrument operator and record whether the target was detected, missed, or clouded out. Preserve the original alert alongside any revised coordinates; never silently edit incoming metadata. Within one day, send the requester a concise result with timestamps, limiting magnitude or measured brightness, and links to the raw data and observing log."]],["startLine",["number","4032000000000000"]],["endLine",["number","4033000000000000"]]]]]]]],["diagnostics",["array",1102,0,[]]],["document",["object",1103,"object-prototype",[["id",["string","observatory/transient-alerts"]],["type",["string","concept"]],["title",["string","Transient Alerts"]],["tags",["array",1104,3,[["0",["string","transients"]],["1",["string","alerts"]],["2",["string","follow-up"]]]]],["sources",["array",1105,0,[]]],["verified",["array",1106,0,[]]],["body",["string","# Transient Alerts\n\n## Purpose\nA transient alert is a time-sensitive notice that a variable or newly appearing object may merit observation. The observatory accepts alerts from approved networks and from the on-call scientist, then records enough context to make a quick, defensible decision.\n\n## Triage\nThe on-call astronomer checks the alert timestamp, coordinates, uncertainty region, brightness estimate, visibility, and scientific priority. Reject duplicates, stale positions, targets below the local horizon, and requests that conflict with a protected safety hold. If the target is observable, compare its required cadence and filters with the current schedule. The astronomer may interrupt a flexible block, but must document whose time was displaced and why.\n\n## Response\nCreate an observation block with the alert identifier, ephemeris source, coordinate epoch, exposure sequence, and deadline. Use a short acquisition exposure before committing to a long series. Notify the instrument operator and record whether the target was detected, missed, or clouded out. Preserve the original alert alongside any revised coordinates; never silently edit incoming metadata. Within one day, send the requester a concise result with timestamps, limiting magnitude or measured brightness, and links to the raw data and observing log.\n"]],["extensions",["object",1107,"object-prototype",[]]],["status",["string","draft"]]]]]]]],["30",["object",1108,"object-prototype",[["conformance",["string","strict"]],["identity",["object",1109,"object-prototype",[["path",["string","observatory/visitor-nights.md"]],["documentId",["string","observatory/visitor-nights"]]]]],["type",["string","concept"]],["metadata",["object",1110,"object-prototype",[["title",["string","Visitor Nights"]],["tags",["array",1111,3,[["0",["string","outreach"]],["1",["string","visitors"]],["2",["string","safety"]]]]],["sourceText",["string",""]]]]],["facets",["object",1112,"object-prototype",[["status",["object",1113,"object-prototype",[["classified",["boolean",true]],["value",["string","stable"]]]]],["trust",["object",1114,"object-prototype",[["classified",["boolean",true]],["value",["string","unverified"]]]]],["staleness",["object",1115,"object-prototype",[["classified",["boolean",true]]]]]]]],["sections",["array",1116,4,[["0",["object",1117,"object-prototype",[["id",["string","observatory/visitor-nights#visitor-nights"]],["headingPath",["string","Visitor Nights"]],["text",["string",""]],["startLine",["number","4024000000000000"]],["endLine",["number","4024000000000000"]]]]],["1",["object",1118,"object-prototype",[["id",["string","observatory/visitor-nights#visitor-nights-purpose"]],["headingPath",["string","Visitor Nights > Purpose"]],["text",["string","A visitor night gives the public a safe view of observing work without turning the control room into an uncontrolled workspace. The outreach lead coordinates the group, while the duty astronomer retains authority over equipment and weather decisions."]],["startLine",["number","4028000000000000"]],["endLine",["number","402a000000000000"]]]]],["2",["object",1119,"object-prototype",[["id",["string","observatory/visitor-nights#visitor-nights-before-arrival"]],["headingPath",["string","Visitor Nights > Before arrival"]],["text",["string","Limit groups to the posted room capacity and collect accessibility needs in advance. Prepare a short target list with bright, forgiving objects that remain visible if the schedule shifts. Test the demonstration eyepiece, red lights, handrails, and restroom route before sunset. Keep visitors away from the pier, power strips, moving dome, and exposed cables; do not allow food, drink, or unescorted access in the equipment area."]],["startLine",["number","402e000000000000"]],["endLine",["number","4030000000000000"]]]]],["3",["object",1120,"object-prototype",[["id",["string","observatory/visitor-nights#visitor-nights-during-the-visit"]],["headingPath",["string","Visitor Nights > During the visit"]],["text",["string","Give a five-minute safety briefing, including the location of exits and the rule to stop when staff says stop. Use red light and preserve dark adaptation; explain why phone screens stay covered. One staff member supervises the viewing area while another operates the mount. If wind, rain, or equipment motion makes the visit unsafe, move indoors to a prepared image session. After departure, count loaned items, close doors, and note attendance, incidents, and useful feedback in the outreach log."]],["startLine",["number","4032000000000000"]],["endLine",["number","4033000000000000"]]]]]]]],["diagnostics",["array",1121,0,[]]],["document",["object",1122,"object-prototype",[["id",["string","observatory/visitor-nights"]],["type",["string","concept"]],["title",["string","Visitor Nights"]],["tags",["array",1123,3,[["0",["string","outreach"]],["1",["string","visitors"]],["2",["string","safety"]]]]],["sources",["array",1124,0,[]]],["verified",["array",1125,0,[]]],["body",["string","# Visitor Nights\n\n## Purpose\nA visitor night gives the public a safe view of observing work without turning the control room into an uncontrolled workspace. The outreach lead coordinates the group, while the duty astronomer retains authority over equipment and weather decisions.\n\n## Before arrival\nLimit groups to the posted room capacity and collect accessibility needs in advance. Prepare a short target list with bright, forgiving objects that remain visible if the schedule shifts. Test the demonstration eyepiece, red lights, handrails, and restroom route before sunset. Keep visitors away from the pier, power strips, moving dome, and exposed cables; do not allow food, drink, or unescorted access in the equipment area.\n\n## During the visit\nGive a five-minute safety briefing, including the location of exits and the rule to stop when staff says stop. Use red light and preserve dark adaptation; explain why phone screens stay covered. One staff member supervises the viewing area while another operates the mount. If wind, rain, or equipment motion makes the visit unsafe, move indoors to a prepared image session. After departure, count loaned items, close doors, and note attendance, incidents, and useful feedback in the outreach log.\n"]],["extensions",["object",1126,"object-prototype",[]]],["status",["string","stable"]]]]]]]],["31",["object",1127,"object-prototype",[["conformance",["string","strict"]],["identity",["object",1128,"object-prototype",[["path",["string","observatory/weather-gates.md"]],["documentId",["string","observatory/weather-gates"]]]]],["type",["string","concept"]],["metadata",["object",1129,"object-prototype",[["title",["string","Weather Gates"]],["tags",["array",1130,3,[["0",["string","weather"]],["1",["string","safety"]],["2",["string","thresholds"]]]]],["sourceText",["string",""]]]]],["facets",["object",1131,"object-prototype",[["status",["object",1132,"object-prototype",[["classified",["boolean",true]],["value",["string","stable"]]]]],["trust",["object",1133,"object-prototype",[["classified",["boolean",true]],["value",["string","unverified"]]]]],["staleness",["object",1134,"object-prototype",[["classified",["boolean",true]]]]]]]],["sections",["array",1135,4,[["0",["object",1136,"object-prototype",[["id",["string","observatory/weather-gates#weather-gates"]],["headingPath",["string","Weather Gates"]],["text",["string",""]],["startLine",["number","4024000000000000"]],["endLine",["number","4024000000000000"]]]]],["1",["object",1137,"object-prototype",[["id",["string","observatory/weather-gates#weather-gates-purpose"]],["headingPath",["string","Weather Gates > Purpose"]],["text",["string","Weather gates define when the dome may open, remain open, or close. They protect people and equipment while giving the duty astronomer predictable decisions instead of relying on intuition during a changing night."]],["startLine",["number","4028000000000000"]],["endLine",["number","402a000000000000"]]]]],["2",["object",1138,"object-prototype",[["id",["string","observatory/weather-gates#weather-gates-opening-gate"]],["headingPath",["string","Weather Gates > Opening gate"]],["text",["string","Open only when the forecast and local sensors agree on a dry, stable window. Require no precipitation, relative humidity below 80 percent, wind below 25 km/h, and a temperature-dew-point spread above 3 °C. Check the all-sky camera for cloud movement and inspect the rain sensor for debris. A clear forecast never overrides a wet sensor or a rapidly approaching storm cell."]],["startLine",["number","402e000000000000"]],["endLine",["number","4030000000000000"]]]]],["3",["object",1139,"object-prototype",[["id",["string","observatory/weather-gates#weather-gates-monitoring-and-closure"]],["headingPath",["string","Weather Gates > Monitoring and closure"]],["text",["string","Sample sensors every minute and display both current values and ten-minute trends. Enter a warning state at 75 percent humidity, 20 km/h wind, or a 4 °C dew-point spread; pause new exposures and prepare to close. Close the dome immediately for detected rain, lightning within 15 km, wind above 35 km/h, or a failing sensor that prevents safe assessment. Record the trigger, timestamp, and recovery decision. Reopening requires ten dry minutes and a fresh walk-around inspection."]],["startLine",["number","4032000000000000"]],["endLine",["number","4033000000000000"]]]]]]]],["diagnostics",["array",1140,0,[]]],["document",["object",1141,"object-prototype",[["id",["string","observatory/weather-gates"]],["type",["string","concept"]],["title",["string","Weather Gates"]],["tags",["array",1142,3,[["0",["string","weather"]],["1",["string","safety"]],["2",["string","thresholds"]]]]],["sources",["array",1143,0,[]]],["verified",["array",1144,0,[]]],["body",["string","# Weather Gates\n\n## Purpose\nWeather gates define when the dome may open, remain open, or close. They protect people and equipment while giving the duty astronomer predictable decisions instead of relying on intuition during a changing night.\n\n## Opening gate\nOpen only when the forecast and local sensors agree on a dry, stable window. Require no precipitation, relative humidity below 80 percent, wind below 25 km/h, and a temperature-dew-point spread above 3 °C. Check the all-sky camera for cloud movement and inspect the rain sensor for debris. A clear forecast never overrides a wet sensor or a rapidly approaching storm cell.\n\n## Monitoring and closure\nSample sensors every minute and display both current values and ten-minute trends. Enter a warning state at 75 percent humidity, 20 km/h wind, or a 4 °C dew-point spread; pause new exposures and prepare to close. Close the dome immediately for detected rain, lightning within 15 km, wind above 35 km/h, or a failing sensor that prevents safe assessment. Record the trigger, timestamp, and recovery decision. Reopening requires ten dry minutes and a fresh walk-around inspection.\n"]],["extensions",["object",1145,"object-prototype",[]]],["status",["string","stable"]]]]]]]],["32",["object",1146,"object-prototype",[["conformance",["string","strict"]],["identity",["object",1147,"object-prototype",[["path",["string","railway/brake-test.md"]],["documentId",["string","railway/brake-test"]]]]],["type",["string","concept"]],["metadata",["object",1148,"object-prototype",[["title",["string","Brake Test"]],["description",["string","A repeatable train-brake check used before departure, after changes, and whenever brake integrity is uncertain."]],["tags",["array",1149,3,[["0",["string","brakes"]],["1",["string","departure"]],["2",["string","rollingstock"]]]]],["sourceText",["string",""]]]]],["facets",["object",1150,"object-prototype",[["status",["object",1151,"object-prototype",[["classified",["boolean",true]],["value",["string","stable"]]]]],["trust",["object",1152,"object-prototype",[["classified",["boolean",true]],["value",["string","unverified"]]]]],["staleness",["object",1153,"object-prototype",[["classified",["boolean",true]]]]]]]],["sections",["array",1154,4,[["0",["object",1155,"object-prototype",[["id",["string","railway/brake-test#brake-test"]],["headingPath",["string","Brake Test"]],["text",["string",""]],["startLine",["number","4028000000000000"]],["endLine",["number","4028000000000000"]]]]],["1",["object",1156,"object-prototype",[["id",["string","railway/brake-test#brake-test-set-up"]],["headingPath",["string","Brake Test > Set up"]],["text",["string","Confirm the consist list, brake system, cab controls, and required test type with the driver or train preparer. Apply handbrakes or other stationary protection before connecting or separating vehicles. Walk the train with a radio and identify the last vehicle. Check that hoses are coupled, cocks are correctly positioned, reservoirs are charged, and visible brake indicators agree with the intended state. Keep clear of couplers and moving equipment."]],["startLine",["number","402c000000000000"]],["endLine",["number","402e000000000000"]]]]],["2",["object",1157,"object-prototype",[["id",["string","railway/brake-test#brake-test-test-and-verify"]],["headingPath",["string","Brake Test > Test and verify"]],["text",["string","The driver applies and releases the train brake using the prescribed sequence. The tester observes the response at the leading, middle, and last vehicle or at the locations specified for that stock. Report a delayed application, incomplete release, audible leak, isolated vehicle, or conflicting indicator immediately. Repeat a test only after the cause and protection are understood; a repeated pass does not erase an unexplained failure."]],["startLine",["number","4031000000000000"]],["endLine",["number","4032000000000000"]]]]],["3",["object",1158,"object-prototype",[["id",["string","railway/brake-test#brake-test-release-the-train"]],["headingPath",["string","Brake Test > Release the train"]],["text",["string","Record test type, time, consist, tester, result, and any restriction. The driver signs acceptance only when the full train is protected by the tested brake system. If the result is incomplete, hold the departure and call control for technical advice."]],["startLine",["number","4034000000000000"]],["endLine",["number","4035000000000000"]]]]]]]],["diagnostics",["array",1159,0,[]]],["document",["object",1160,"object-prototype",[["id",["string","railway/brake-test"]],["type",["string","concept"]],["title",["string","Brake Test"]],["tags",["array",1161,3,[["0",["string","brakes"]],["1",["string","departure"]],["2",["string","rollingstock"]]]]],["sources",["array",1162,0,[]]],["verified",["array",1163,0,[]]],["body",["string","\n# Brake Test\n\n## Set up\nConfirm the consist list, brake system, cab controls, and required test type with the driver or train preparer. Apply handbrakes or other stationary protection before connecting or separating vehicles. Walk the train with a radio and identify the last vehicle. Check that hoses are coupled, cocks are correctly positioned, reservoirs are charged, and visible brake indicators agree with the intended state. Keep clear of couplers and moving equipment.\n\n## Test and verify\nThe driver applies and releases the train brake using the prescribed sequence. The tester observes the response at the leading, middle, and last vehicle or at the locations specified for that stock. Report a delayed application, incomplete release, audible leak, isolated vehicle, or conflicting indicator immediately. Repeat a test only after the cause and protection are understood; a repeated pass does not erase an unexplained failure.\n\n## Release the train\nRecord test type, time, consist, tester, result, and any restriction. The driver signs acceptance only when the full train is protected by the tested brake system. If the result is incomplete, hold the departure and call control for technical advice.\n"]],["extensions",["object",1164,"object-prototype",[]]],["description",["string","A repeatable train-brake check used before departure, after changes, and whenever brake integrity is uncertain."]],["status",["string","stable"]]]]]]]],["33",["object",1165,"object-prototype",[["conformance",["string","strict"]],["identity",["object",1166,"object-prototype",[["path",["string","railway/depot-closeout.md"]],["documentId",["string","railway/depot-closeout"]]]]],["type",["string","concept"]],["metadata",["object",1167,"object-prototype",[["title",["string","Depot Closeout"]],["description",["string","An end-of-shift routine that leaves a regional railway depot safe, accounted for, and ready for the next crew."]],["tags",["array",1168,3,[["0",["string","depot"]],["1",["string","closeout"]],["2",["string","maintenance"]]]]],["sourceText",["string",""]]]]],["facets",["object",1169,"object-prototype",[["status",["object",1170,"object-prototype",[["classified",["boolean",true]],["value",["string","stable"]]]]],["trust",["object",1171,"object-prototype",[["classified",["boolean",true]],["value",["string","unverified"]]]]],["staleness",["object",1172,"object-prototype",[["classified",["boolean",true]]]]]]]],["sections",["array",1173,4,[["0",["object",1174,"object-prototype",[["id",["string","railway/depot-closeout#depot-closeout"]],["headingPath",["string","Depot Closeout"]],["text",["string",""]],["startLine",["number","4028000000000000"]],["endLine",["number","4028000000000000"]]]]],["1",["object",1175,"object-prototype",[["id",["string","railway/depot-closeout#depot-closeout-secure-the-site"]],["headingPath",["string","Depot Closeout > Secure the site"]],["text",["string","The duty lead checks that every vehicle is on its assigned road, stationary protection is applied, and movements are complete. Confirm that pits, walkways, fueling points, lifting equipment, and isolation boundaries are clear. Remove tools and temporary barriers only when the responsible maintainer releases them. Close doors and gates, inspect lighting and alarms, and report spills, leaks, or damaged fencing before leaving the area."]],["startLine",["number","402c000000000000"]],["endLine",["number","402e000000000000"]]]]],["2",["object",1176,"object-prototype",[["id",["string","railway/depot-closeout#depot-closeout-reconcile-the-work"]],["headingPath",["string","Depot Closeout > Reconcile the work"]],["text",["string","Compare the vehicle position board with the actual consist and record mileage, fuel or charge state, defects, isolation tags, and deferred work. Each open defect needs an owner, safe operating limit, and next action; “watch” is not a substitute for a decision. Attach test sheets and parts information to the correct vehicle record. Escalate anything that could affect the first diagram to control and the incoming maintenance lead."]],["startLine",["number","4031000000000000"]],["endLine",["number","4032000000000000"]]]]],["3",["object",1177,"object-prototype",[["id",["string","railway/depot-closeout#depot-closeout-hand-over"]],["headingPath",["string","Depot Closeout > Hand over"]],["text",["string","Brief the next shift on vehicles unavailable, equipment isolated, unusual noises, contractors on site, and inspections due. Read back critical restrictions, sign the closeout log, and keep the radio available until the handoff is accepted."]],["startLine",["number","4034000000000000"]],["endLine",["number","4035000000000000"]]]]]]]],["diagnostics",["array",1178,0,[]]],["document",["object",1179,"object-prototype",[["id",["string","railway/depot-closeout"]],["type",["string","concept"]],["title",["string","Depot Closeout"]],["tags",["array",1180,3,[["0",["string","depot"]],["1",["string","closeout"]],["2",["string","maintenance"]]]]],["sources",["array",1181,0,[]]],["verified",["array",1182,0,[]]],["body",["string","\n# Depot Closeout\n\n## Secure the site\nThe duty lead checks that every vehicle is on its assigned road, stationary protection is applied, and movements are complete. Confirm that pits, walkways, fueling points, lifting equipment, and isolation boundaries are clear. Remove tools and temporary barriers only when the responsible maintainer releases them. Close doors and gates, inspect lighting and alarms, and report spills, leaks, or damaged fencing before leaving the area.\n\n## Reconcile the work\nCompare the vehicle position board with the actual consist and record mileage, fuel or charge state, defects, isolation tags, and deferred work. Each open defect needs an owner, safe operating limit, and next action; “watch” is not a substitute for a decision. Attach test sheets and parts information to the correct vehicle record. Escalate anything that could affect the first diagram to control and the incoming maintenance lead.\n\n## Hand over\nBrief the next shift on vehicles unavailable, equipment isolated, unusual noises, contractors on site, and inspections due. Read back critical restrictions, sign the closeout log, and keep the radio available until the handoff is accepted.\n"]],["extensions",["object",1183,"object-prototype",[]]],["description",["string","An end-of-shift routine that leaves a regional railway depot safe, accounted for, and ready for the next crew."]],["status",["string","stable"]]]]]]]],["34",["object",1184,"object-prototype",[["conformance",["string","strict"]],["identity",["object",1185,"object-prototype",[["path",["string","railway/incident-radio.md"]],["documentId",["string","railway/incident-radio"]]]]],["type",["string","concept"]],["metadata",["object",1186,"object-prototype",[["title",["string","Incident Radio"]],["description",["string","A short radio protocol for reporting operational incidents without losing the essential facts."]],["tags",["array",1187,3,[["0",["string","radio"]],["1",["string","incidents"]],["2",["string","control"]]]]],["sourceText",["string",""]]]]],["facets",["object",1188,"object-prototype",[["status",["object",1189,"object-prototype",[["classified",["boolean",true]],["value",["string","stable"]]]]],["trust",["object",1190,"object-prototype",[["classified",["boolean",true]],["value",["string","unverified"]]]]],["staleness",["object",1191,"object-prototype",[["classified",["boolean",true]]]]]]]],["sections",["array",1192,4,[["0",["object",1193,"object-prototype",[["id",["string","railway/incident-radio#incident-radio"]],["headingPath",["string","Incident Radio"]],["text",["string",""]],["startLine",["number","4028000000000000"]],["endLine",["number","4028000000000000"]]]]],["1",["object",1194,"object-prototype",[["id",["string","railway/incident-radio#incident-radio-make-the-first-call"]],["headingPath",["string","Incident Radio > Make the first call"]],["text",["string","Use the designated emergency channel when there is immediate danger; otherwise use the operating channel. Start with the recipient, your role and location, then say “emergency” or “priority” and wait for acknowledgment when required. State what happened, which line or platform is affected, whether anyone is injured, and whether a train or vehicle is moving. If you do not know a detail, say so instead of guessing."]],["startLine",["number","402c000000000000"]],["endLine",["number","402e000000000000"]]]]],["2",["object",1195,"object-prototype",[["id",["string","railway/incident-radio#incident-radio-keep-instructions-clear"]],["headingPath",["string","Incident Radio > Keep instructions clear"]],["text",["string","The signaller or control lead repeats the protection instruction. Read it back word for word, including signal numbers, line limits, and any prohibition on movement. Use plain verbs such as stop, protect, evacuate, or hold. Do not transmit while another person is giving a protection instruction. If the channel is busy, send the minimum message needed to stop harm and then move to the assigned channel."]],["startLine",["number","4031000000000000"]],["endLine",["number","4032000000000000"]]]]],["3",["object",1196,"object-prototype",[["id",["string","railway/incident-radio#incident-radio-update-and-close"]],["headingPath",["string","Incident Radio > Update and close"]],["text",["string","Give updates when the situation, access, or casualty status changes. Identify each new message with the incident reference and current location. Control closes the event only after the responsible lead confirms that people are safe, the route is protected or released, and the written incident record is complete."]],["startLine",["number","4034000000000000"]],["endLine",["number","4035000000000000"]]]]]]]],["diagnostics",["array",1197,0,[]]],["document",["object",1198,"object-prototype",[["id",["string","railway/incident-radio"]],["type",["string","concept"]],["title",["string","Incident Radio"]],["tags",["array",1199,3,[["0",["string","radio"]],["1",["string","incidents"]],["2",["string","control"]]]]],["sources",["array",1200,0,[]]],["verified",["array",1201,0,[]]],["body",["string","\n# Incident Radio\n\n## Make the first call\nUse the designated emergency channel when there is immediate danger; otherwise use the operating channel. Start with the recipient, your role and location, then say “emergency” or “priority” and wait for acknowledgment when required. State what happened, which line or platform is affected, whether anyone is injured, and whether a train or vehicle is moving. If you do not know a detail, say so instead of guessing.\n\n## Keep instructions clear\nThe signaller or control lead repeats the protection instruction. Read it back word for word, including signal numbers, line limits, and any prohibition on movement. Use plain verbs such as stop, protect, evacuate, or hold. Do not transmit while another person is giving a protection instruction. If the channel is busy, send the minimum message needed to stop harm and then move to the assigned channel.\n\n## Update and close\nGive updates when the situation, access, or casualty status changes. Identify each new message with the incident reference and current location. Control closes the event only after the responsible lead confirms that people are safe, the route is protected or released, and the written incident record is complete.\n"]],["extensions",["object",1202,"object-prototype",[]]],["description",["string","A short radio protocol for reporting operational incidents without losing the essential facts."]],["status",["string","stable"]]]]]]]],["35",["object",1203,"object-prototype",[["conformance",["string","strict"]],["identity",["object",1204,"object-prototype",[["path",["string","railway/passenger-recovery.md"]],["documentId",["string","railway/passenger-recovery"]]]]],["type",["string","concept"]],["metadata",["object",1205,"object-prototype",[["title",["string","Passenger Recovery"]],["description",["string","A practical playbook for restoring passenger journeys after a regional railway disruption."]],["tags",["array",1206,3,[["0",["string","passengers"]],["1",["string","disruption"]],["2",["string","recovery"]]]]],["sourceText",["string",""]]]]],["facets",["object",1207,"object-prototype",[["status",["object",1208,"object-prototype",[["classified",["boolean",true]],["value",["string","stable"]]]]],["trust",["object",1209,"object-prototype",[["classified",["boolean",true]],["value",["string","unverified"]]]]],["staleness",["object",1210,"object-prototype",[["classified",["boolean",true]]]]]]]],["sections",["array",1211,4,[["0",["object",1212,"object-prototype",[["id",["string","railway/passenger-recovery#passenger-recovery"]],["headingPath",["string","Passenger Recovery"]],["text",["string",""]],["startLine",["number","4028000000000000"]],["endLine",["number","4028000000000000"]]]]],["1",["object",1213,"object-prototype",[["id",["string","railway/passenger-recovery#passenger-recovery-stabilize-the-service"]],["headingPath",["string","Passenger Recovery > Stabilize the service"]],["text",["string","Control first establishes what is safe: affected trains, blocked routes, available platforms, crew locations, and the next credible movement. Avoid promising a time before the signaller and operating lead agree on the plan. Station teams provide water, accessibility support, and a calm explanation of what is known. Record passengers needing an assisted transfer, medical help, or a connection that cannot wait."]],["startLine",["number","402c000000000000"]],["endLine",["number","402e000000000000"]]]]],["2",["object",1214,"object-prototype",[["id",["string","railway/passenger-recovery#passenger-recovery-offer-usable-alternatives"]],["headingPath",["string","Passenger Recovery > Offer usable alternatives"]],["text",["string","Choose options that can actually be staffed and dispatched: a short turnback, an accepting route, a replacement bus, or an endorsed ticket arrangement. Give passengers the action, destination, departure point, and next update time. Keep the message consistent across announcements, displays, web updates, and staff briefings. Do not send people onto an alternative route until capacity, accessibility, and ticket acceptance are confirmed."]],["startLine",["number","4031000000000000"]],["endLine",["number","4032000000000000"]]]]],["3",["object",1215,"object-prototype",[["id",["string","railway/passenger-recovery#passenger-recovery-recover-and-learn"]],["headingPath",["string","Passenger Recovery > Recover and learn"]],["text",["string","When trains resume, prioritize stranded passengers and publish the revised stopping pattern. Tell station teams when normal service is genuinely restored. Close the response with a count of cancellations, assisted passengers, substitute vehicles, and unresolved claims; capture passenger reports while details are fresh."]],["startLine",["number","4034000000000000"]],["endLine",["number","4035000000000000"]]]]]]]],["diagnostics",["array",1216,0,[]]],["document",["object",1217,"object-prototype",[["id",["string","railway/passenger-recovery"]],["type",["string","concept"]],["title",["string","Passenger Recovery"]],["tags",["array",1218,3,[["0",["string","passengers"]],["1",["string","disruption"]],["2",["string","recovery"]]]]],["sources",["array",1219,0,[]]],["verified",["array",1220,0,[]]],["body",["string","\n# Passenger Recovery\n\n## Stabilize the service\nControl first establishes what is safe: affected trains, blocked routes, available platforms, crew locations, and the next credible movement. Avoid promising a time before the signaller and operating lead agree on the plan. Station teams provide water, accessibility support, and a calm explanation of what is known. Record passengers needing an assisted transfer, medical help, or a connection that cannot wait.\n\n## Offer usable alternatives\nChoose options that can actually be staffed and dispatched: a short turnback, an accepting route, a replacement bus, or an endorsed ticket arrangement. Give passengers the action, destination, departure point, and next update time. Keep the message consistent across announcements, displays, web updates, and staff briefings. Do not send people onto an alternative route until capacity, accessibility, and ticket acceptance are confirmed.\n\n## Recover and learn\nWhen trains resume, prioritize stranded passengers and publish the revised stopping pattern. Tell station teams when normal service is genuinely restored. Close the response with a count of cancellations, assisted passengers, substitute vehicles, and unresolved claims; capture passenger reports while details are fresh.\n"]],["extensions",["object",1221,"object-prototype",[]]],["description",["string","A practical playbook for restoring passenger journeys after a regional railway disruption."]],["status",["string","stable"]]]]]]]],["36",["object",1222,"object-prototype",[["conformance",["string","strict"]],["identity",["object",1223,"object-prototype",[["path",["string","railway/platform-dispatch.md"]],["documentId",["string","railway/platform-dispatch"]]]]],["type",["string","concept"]],["metadata",["object",1224,"object-prototype",[["title",["string","Platform Dispatch"]],["description",["string","A disciplined departure check that aligns the dispatcher, driver, and platform before a train leaves."]],["tags",["array",1225,3,[["0",["string","dispatch"]],["1",["string","platform"]],["2",["string","passenger"]]]]],["sourceText",["string",""]]]]],["facets",["object",1226,"object-prototype",[["status",["object",1227,"object-prototype",[["classified",["boolean",true]],["value",["string","stable"]]]]],["trust",["object",1228,"object-prototype",[["classified",["boolean",true]],["value",["string","unverified"]]]]],["staleness",["object",1229,"object-prototype",[["classified",["boolean",true]]]]]]]],["sections",["array",1230,4,[["0",["object",1231,"object-prototype",[["id",["string","railway/platform-dispatch#platform-dispatch"]],["headingPath",["string","Platform Dispatch"]],["text",["string",""]],["startLine",["number","4028000000000000"]],["endLine",["number","4028000000000000"]]]]],["1",["object",1232,"object-prototype",[["id",["string","railway/platform-dispatch#platform-dispatch-prepare-the-departure"]],["headingPath",["string","Platform Dispatch > Prepare the departure"]],["text",["string","The dispatcher checks the working timetable, platform indicator, booked calling points, and any active operating notice. Confirm that the driver has the correct service identity and departure time. Scan the platform from the rear of the train toward the stopping point: passengers must be clear of doors, ramps must be withdrawn, and no object may project into the train path. Resolve a crowd, unattended item, or door fault before giving a signal."]],["startLine",["number","402c000000000000"]],["endLine",["number","402e000000000000"]]]]],["2",["object",1233,"object-prototype",[["id",["string","railway/platform-dispatch#platform-dispatch-give-and-monitor-the-signal"]],["headingPath",["string","Platform Dispatch > Give and monitor the signal"]],["text",["string","Make the departure indication using the agreed hand signal, lamp, or dispatch equipment while maintaining a clear view of the train. The driver acknowledges using the prescribed method. Watch the first vehicle roll away and check that every door is closed, the platform edge is clear, and no person is running alongside. If visibility is lost, the driver reports an abnormal condition, or a door reopens, instruct the driver to stop and inform the signaller."]],["startLine",["number","4031000000000000"]],["endLine",["number","4032000000000000"]]]]],["3",["object",1234,"object-prototype",[["id",["string","railway/platform-dispatch#platform-dispatch-record-exceptions"]],["headingPath",["string","Platform Dispatch > Record exceptions"]],["text",["string","Log late dispatch, assistance provided, door defects, and any stop instruction. Pass a concise update to control so the next station knows what to expect. Never trade a safety check for timetable recovery."]],["startLine",["number","4034000000000000"]],["endLine",["number","4035000000000000"]]]]]]]],["diagnostics",["array",1235,0,[]]],["document",["object",1236,"object-prototype",[["id",["string","railway/platform-dispatch"]],["type",["string","concept"]],["title",["string","Platform Dispatch"]],["tags",["array",1237,3,[["0",["string","dispatch"]],["1",["string","platform"]],["2",["string","passenger"]]]]],["sources",["array",1238,0,[]]],["verified",["array",1239,0,[]]],["body",["string","\n# Platform Dispatch\n\n## Prepare the departure\nThe dispatcher checks the working timetable, platform indicator, booked calling points, and any active operating notice. Confirm that the driver has the correct service identity and departure time. Scan the platform from the rear of the train toward the stopping point: passengers must be clear of doors, ramps must be withdrawn, and no object may project into the train path. Resolve a crowd, unattended item, or door fault before giving a signal.\n\n## Give and monitor the signal\nMake the departure indication using the agreed hand signal, lamp, or dispatch equipment while maintaining a clear view of the train. The driver acknowledges using the prescribed method. Watch the first vehicle roll away and check that every door is closed, the platform edge is clear, and no person is running alongside. If visibility is lost, the driver reports an abnormal condition, or a door reopens, instruct the driver to stop and inform the signaller.\n\n## Record exceptions\nLog late dispatch, assistance provided, door defects, and any stop instruction. Pass a concise update to control so the next station knows what to expect. Never trade a safety check for timetable recovery.\n"]],["extensions",["object",1240,"object-prototype",[]]],["description",["string","A disciplined departure check that aligns the dispatcher, driver, and platform before a train leaves."]],["status",["string","stable"]]]]]]]],["37",["object",1241,"object-prototype",[["conformance",["string","strict"]],["identity",["object",1242,"object-prototype",[["path",["string","railway/signal-inspection.md"]],["documentId",["string","railway/signal-inspection"]]]]],["type",["string","concept"]],["metadata",["object",1243,"object-prototype",[["title",["string","Signal Inspection"]],["description",["string","A field check for confirming that signals display, communicate, and recover as expected."]],["tags",["array",1244,3,[["0",["string","signals"]],["1",["string","inspection"]],["2",["string","safety"]]]]],["sourceText",["string",""]]]]],["facets",["object",1245,"object-prototype",[["status",["object",1246,"object-prototype",[["classified",["boolean",true]],["value",["string","stable"]]]]],["trust",["object",1247,"object-prototype",[["classified",["boolean",true]],["value",["string","unverified"]]]]],["staleness",["object",1248,"object-prototype",[["classified",["boolean",true]]]]]]]],["sections",["array",1249,4,[["0",["object",1250,"object-prototype",[["id",["string","railway/signal-inspection#signal-inspection"]],["headingPath",["string","Signal Inspection"]],["text",["string",""]],["startLine",["number","4028000000000000"]],["endLine",["number","4028000000000000"]]]]],["1",["object",1251,"object-prototype",[["id",["string","railway/signal-inspection#signal-inspection-before-approaching"]],["headingPath",["string","Signal Inspection > Before approaching"]],["text",["string","Review the work notice, signal identification, control area, and protection granted by the signaller. Carry a radio, identification light, and the latest defect sheet. Do not enter the track or signal structure until the signaller repeats the protection and the inspector reads it back. Treat an unexpected aspect, dark lamp, or conflicting instruction as a failure rather than a nuisance."]],["startLine",["number","402c000000000000"]],["endLine",["number","402e000000000000"]]]]],["2",["object",1252,"object-prototype",[["id",["string","railway/signal-inspection#signal-inspection-field-checks"]],["headingPath",["string","Signal Inspection > Field checks"]],["text",["string","From the normal viewing distance, verify the signal head, route indicator, sighting line, and surrounding obstruction. Compare the displayed aspect with the signaller’s stated route; do not test by requesting a movement unless the work notice authorizes it. Check lenses for damage, condensation, dirt, or a loose hood. At the equipment case, look for water ingress, heat marks, exposed wiring, and an unlocked door. Record signal ID, aspect, time, weather, and exact symptom. Photograph only when permitted by the work instruction."]],["startLine",["number","4031000000000000"]],["endLine",["number","4032000000000000"]]]]],["3",["object",1253,"object-prototype",[["id",["string","railway/signal-inspection#signal-inspection-closeout"]],["headingPath",["string","Signal Inspection > Closeout"]],["text",["string","Report defects immediately over the designated channel, then repeat the restriction or protection that follows. Leave covers secured and tools counted. The signaller closes the inspection only after receiving the written finding and confirming whether the signal is serviceable, restricted, or out of use."]],["startLine",["number","4034000000000000"]],["endLine",["number","4035000000000000"]]]]]]]],["diagnostics",["array",1254,0,[]]],["document",["object",1255,"object-prototype",[["id",["string","railway/signal-inspection"]],["type",["string","concept"]],["title",["string","Signal Inspection"]],["tags",["array",1256,3,[["0",["string","signals"]],["1",["string","inspection"]],["2",["string","safety"]]]]],["sources",["array",1257,0,[]]],["verified",["array",1258,0,[]]],["body",["string","\n# Signal Inspection\n\n## Before approaching\nReview the work notice, signal identification, control area, and protection granted by the signaller. Carry a radio, identification light, and the latest defect sheet. Do not enter the track or signal structure until the signaller repeats the protection and the inspector reads it back. Treat an unexpected aspect, dark lamp, or conflicting instruction as a failure rather than a nuisance.\n\n## Field checks\nFrom the normal viewing distance, verify the signal head, route indicator, sighting line, and surrounding obstruction. Compare the displayed aspect with the signaller’s stated route; do not test by requesting a movement unless the work notice authorizes it. Check lenses for damage, condensation, dirt, or a loose hood. At the equipment case, look for water ingress, heat marks, exposed wiring, and an unlocked door. Record signal ID, aspect, time, weather, and exact symptom. Photograph only when permitted by the work instruction.\n\n## Closeout\nReport defects immediately over the designated channel, then repeat the restriction or protection that follows. Leave covers secured and tools counted. The signaller closes the inspection only after receiving the written finding and confirming whether the signal is serviceable, restricted, or out of use.\n"]],["extensions",["object",1259,"object-prototype",[]]],["description",["string","A field check for confirming that signals display, communicate, and recover as expected."]],["status",["string","stable"]]]]]]]],["38",["object",1260,"object-prototype",[["conformance",["string","strict"]],["identity",["object",1261,"object-prototype",[["path",["string","railway/switch-freeze-response.md"]],["documentId",["string","railway/switch-freeze-response"]]]]],["type",["string","concept"]],["metadata",["object",1262,"object-prototype",[["title",["string","Switch Freeze Response"]],["description",["string","A response pattern for points that cannot move because of ice, snow, or compacted debris."]],["tags",["array",1263,3,[["0",["string","points"]],["1",["string","winter"]],["2",["string","disruption"]]]]],["sourceText",["string",""]]]]],["facets",["object",1264,"object-prototype",[["status",["object",1265,"object-prototype",[["classified",["boolean",true]],["value",["string","stable"]]]]],["trust",["object",1266,"object-prototype",[["classified",["boolean",true]],["value",["string","unverified"]]]]],["staleness",["object",1267,"object-prototype",[["classified",["boolean",true]]]]]]]],["sections",["array",1268,4,[["0",["object",1269,"object-prototype",[["id",["string","railway/switch-freeze-response#switch-freeze-response"]],["headingPath",["string","Switch Freeze Response"]],["text",["string",""]],["startLine",["number","4028000000000000"]],["endLine",["number","4028000000000000"]]]]],["1",["object",1270,"object-prototype",[["id",["string","railway/switch-freeze-response#switch-freeze-response-protect-the-movement"]],["headingPath",["string","Switch Freeze Response > Protect the movement"]],["text",["string","When points fail to prove or move in freezing conditions, stop movements over the affected route. Tell the signaller the point number, last known position, attempted command, and whether a vehicle is fouling the clearance. The signaller protects the route and confirms the restriction. Do not force a switch with a locomotive, kick snow into the mechanism, or rely on an unverified indication."]],["startLine",["number","402c000000000000"]],["endLine",["number","402e000000000000"]]]]],["2",["object",1271,"object-prototype",[["id",["string","railway/switch-freeze-response#switch-freeze-response-clear-and-assess"]],["headingPath",["string","Switch Freeze Response > Clear and assess"]],["text",["string","A qualified points technician or authorized response crew inspects the switch under the granted protection. Remove loose snow and ice with approved tools, keeping hands clear of the stretcher and drive rods. Check the stock rail, stretcher, detection contacts, and drainage for compacted material or standing water. If heat tracing is fitted, confirm its status through the approved control rather than opening electrical equipment in wet conditions. Attempt a movement only after the technician and signaller agree that the route is protected."]],["startLine",["number","4031000000000000"]],["endLine",["number","4032000000000000"]]]]],["3",["object",1272,"object-prototype",[["id",["string","railway/switch-freeze-response#switch-freeze-response-restore-service"]],["headingPath",["string","Switch Freeze Response > Restore service"]],["text",["string","Run the prescribed proving cycle and record the final position. If detection remains unreliable, impose the published temporary speed or hand-signalling arrangement. Notify control, affected crews, and passenger information staff with the next review time."]],["startLine",["number","4034000000000000"]],["endLine",["number","4035000000000000"]]]]]]]],["diagnostics",["array",1273,0,[]]],["document",["object",1274,"object-prototype",[["id",["string","railway/switch-freeze-response"]],["type",["string","concept"]],["title",["string","Switch Freeze Response"]],["tags",["array",1275,3,[["0",["string","points"]],["1",["string","winter"]],["2",["string","disruption"]]]]],["sources",["array",1276,0,[]]],["verified",["array",1277,0,[]]],["body",["string","\n# Switch Freeze Response\n\n## Protect the movement\nWhen points fail to prove or move in freezing conditions, stop movements over the affected route. Tell the signaller the point number, last known position, attempted command, and whether a vehicle is fouling the clearance. The signaller protects the route and confirms the restriction. Do not force a switch with a locomotive, kick snow into the mechanism, or rely on an unverified indication.\n\n## Clear and assess\nA qualified points technician or authorized response crew inspects the switch under the granted protection. Remove loose snow and ice with approved tools, keeping hands clear of the stretcher and drive rods. Check the stock rail, stretcher, detection contacts, and drainage for compacted material or standing water. If heat tracing is fitted, confirm its status through the approved control rather than opening electrical equipment in wet conditions. Attempt a movement only after the technician and signaller agree that the route is protected.\n\n## Restore service\nRun the prescribed proving cycle and record the final position. If detection remains unreliable, impose the published temporary speed or hand-signalling arrangement. Notify control, affected crews, and passenger information staff with the next review time.\n"]],["extensions",["object",1278,"object-prototype",[]]],["description",["string","A response pattern for points that cannot move because of ice, snow, or compacted debris."]],["status",["string","stable"]]]]]]]],["39",["object",1279,"object-prototype",[["conformance",["string","strict"]],["identity",["object",1280,"object-prototype",[["path",["string","railway/timetable-change.md"]],["documentId",["string","railway/timetable-change"]]]]],["type",["string","concept"]],["metadata",["object",1281,"object-prototype",[["title",["string","Timetable Change"]],["description",["string","A controlled method for turning an amended working timetable into an understood operating plan."]],["tags",["array",1282,3,[["0",["string","timetable"]],["1",["string","planning"]],["2",["string","communications"]]]]],["sourceText",["string",""]]]]],["facets",["object",1283,"object-prototype",[["status",["object",1284,"object-prototype",[["classified",["boolean",true]],["value",["string","draft"]]]]],["trust",["object",1285,"object-prototype",[["classified",["boolean",true]],["value",["string","unverified"]]]]],["staleness",["object",1286,"object-prototype",[["classified",["boolean",true]]]]]]]],["sections",["array",1287,4,[["0",["object",1288,"object-prototype",[["id",["string","railway/timetable-change#timetable-change"]],["headingPath",["string","Timetable Change"]],["text",["string",""]],["startLine",["number","4028000000000000"]],["endLine",["number","4028000000000000"]]]]],["1",["object",1289,"object-prototype",[["id",["string","railway/timetable-change#timetable-change-validate-the-change"]],["headingPath",["string","Timetable Change > Validate the change"]],["text",["string","Control compares the amended timetable with the current service plan, traincrew diagrams, rollingstock availability, engineering possessions, and station staffing. Confirm the effective date, service identifiers, public calling pattern, pathing constraints, and any temporary speed restrictions. A change is not operationally usable until a named owner confirms the source and version."]],["startLine",["number","402c000000000000"]],["endLine",["number","402e000000000000"]]]]],["2",["object",1290,"object-prototype",[["id",["string","railway/timetable-change#timetable-change-brief-the-railway"]],["headingPath",["string","Timetable Change > Brief the railway"]],["text",["string","Publish one controlled notice rather than sending conflicting extracts. Brief signallers, drivers, guards, dispatchers, station teams, maintenance leads, and passenger-information staff using the same service identity and times. Ask each role to repeat the part that affects its work: crossing arrangements, platform, turnback, connections, or last-train commitment. Mark superseded copies and remove them from shared boards and cab packs."]],["startLine",["number","4031000000000000"]],["endLine",["number","4032000000000000"]]]]],["3",["object",1291,"object-prototype",[["id",["string","railway/timetable-change#timetable-change-monitor-first-use"]],["headingPath",["string","Timetable Change > Monitor first use"]],["text",["string","For the first affected operating period, control watches departures, platform conflicts, crew readiness, and passenger messages. Log deviations against the amended plan, not against an obsolete schedule. At the review point, retain the approved notice, record unresolved impacts, and either confirm the change or issue a corrected version with a new effective time."]],["startLine",["number","4034000000000000"]],["endLine",["number","4035000000000000"]]]]]]]],["diagnostics",["array",1292,0,[]]],["document",["object",1293,"object-prototype",[["id",["string","railway/timetable-change"]],["type",["string","concept"]],["title",["string","Timetable Change"]],["tags",["array",1294,3,[["0",["string","timetable"]],["1",["string","planning"]],["2",["string","communications"]]]]],["sources",["array",1295,0,[]]],["verified",["array",1296,0,[]]],["body",["string","\n# Timetable Change\n\n## Validate the change\nControl compares the amended timetable with the current service plan, traincrew diagrams, rollingstock availability, engineering possessions, and station staffing. Confirm the effective date, service identifiers, public calling pattern, pathing constraints, and any temporary speed restrictions. A change is not operationally usable until a named owner confirms the source and version.\n\n## Brief the railway\nPublish one controlled notice rather than sending conflicting extracts. Brief signallers, drivers, guards, dispatchers, station teams, maintenance leads, and passenger-information staff using the same service identity and times. Ask each role to repeat the part that affects its work: crossing arrangements, platform, turnback, connections, or last-train commitment. Mark superseded copies and remove them from shared boards and cab packs.\n\n## Monitor first use\nFor the first affected operating period, control watches departures, platform conflicts, crew readiness, and passenger messages. Log deviations against the amended plan, not against an obsolete schedule. At the review point, retain the approved notice, record unresolved impacts, and either confirm the change or issue a corrected version with a new effective time.\n"]],["extensions",["object",1297,"object-prototype",[]]],["description",["string","A controlled method for turning an amended working timetable into an understood operating plan."]],["status",["string","draft"]]]]]]]],["40",["object",1298,"object-prototype",[["conformance",["string","strict"]],["identity",["object",1299,"object-prototype",[["path",["string","railway/track-walk.md"]],["documentId",["string","railway/track-walk"]]]]],["type",["string","concept"]],["metadata",["object",1300,"object-prototype",[["title",["string","Track Walk"]],["description",["string","A protected walking inspection for finding defects before they become a train movement hazard."]],["tags",["array",1301,3,[["0",["string","track"]],["1",["string","inspection"]],["2",["string","patrol"]]]]],["sourceText",["string",""]]]]],["facets",["object",1302,"object-prototype",[["status",["object",1303,"object-prototype",[["classified",["boolean",true]],["value",["string","draft"]]]]],["trust",["object",1304,"object-prototype",[["classified",["boolean",true]],["value",["string","unverified"]]]]],["staleness",["object",1305,"object-prototype",[["classified",["boolean",true]]]]]]]],["sections",["array",1306,4,[["0",["object",1307,"object-prototype",[["id",["string","railway/track-walk#track-walk"]],["headingPath",["string","Track Walk"]],["text",["string",""]],["startLine",["number","4028000000000000"]],["endLine",["number","4028000000000000"]]]]],["1",["object",1308,"object-prototype",[["id",["string","railway/track-walk#track-walk-plan-and-protect"]],["headingPath",["string","Track Walk > Plan and protect"]],["text",["string","The patrol leader checks the work order, limits, weather, nearby movements, and safe access point. Obtain formal protection from the signaller and read back the exact track section, line, and release condition. Carry a charged radio, high-visibility clothing, torch, measuring tools, and a way to mark location. Enter only after the signaller confirms that protection is active; keep the escape route visible throughout the walk."]],["startLine",["number","402c000000000000"]],["endLine",["number","402e000000000000"]]]]],["2",["object",1309,"object-prototype",[["id",["string","railway/track-walk#track-walk-inspect-systematically"]],["headingPath",["string","Track Walk > Inspect systematically"]],["text",["string","Walk in one direction and call out each reference point, such as a kilometre post, joint, crossing, or bridge. Look for rail cracks, displaced fasteners, broken sleepers, ballast washout, standing water, vegetation, trespass damage, and objects fouling the gauge. Compare unusual geometry with the permitted inspection limits rather than judging by appearance. Photograph or mark a defect only when the work instruction allows it, and never leave a marker that could be mistaken for railway equipment."]],["startLine",["number","4031000000000000"]],["endLine",["number","4032000000000000"]]]]],["3",["object",1310,"object-prototype",[["id",["string","railway/track-walk#track-walk-report-and-hand-back"]],["headingPath",["string","Track Walk > Report and hand back"]],["text",["string","Give control the location, defect, immediate risk, and recommended restriction in that order. Stop the patrol if weather, visibility, or radio coverage changes. Count tools, clear the line, and obtain explicit release confirmation before normal movements resume."]],["startLine",["number","4034000000000000"]],["endLine",["number","4035000000000000"]]]]]]]],["diagnostics",["array",1311,0,[]]],["document",["object",1312,"object-prototype",[["id",["string","railway/track-walk"]],["type",["string","concept"]],["title",["string","Track Walk"]],["tags",["array",1313,3,[["0",["string","track"]],["1",["string","inspection"]],["2",["string","patrol"]]]]],["sources",["array",1314,0,[]]],["verified",["array",1315,0,[]]],["body",["string","\n# Track Walk\n\n## Plan and protect\nThe patrol leader checks the work order, limits, weather, nearby movements, and safe access point. Obtain formal protection from the signaller and read back the exact track section, line, and release condition. Carry a charged radio, high-visibility clothing, torch, measuring tools, and a way to mark location. Enter only after the signaller confirms that protection is active; keep the escape route visible throughout the walk.\n\n## Inspect systematically\nWalk in one direction and call out each reference point, such as a kilometre post, joint, crossing, or bridge. Look for rail cracks, displaced fasteners, broken sleepers, ballast washout, standing water, vegetation, trespass damage, and objects fouling the gauge. Compare unusual geometry with the permitted inspection limits rather than judging by appearance. Photograph or mark a defect only when the work instruction allows it, and never leave a marker that could be mistaken for railway equipment.\n\n## Report and hand back\nGive control the location, defect, immediate risk, and recommended restriction in that order. Stop the patrol if weather, visibility, or radio coverage changes. Count tools, clear the line, and obtain explicit release confirmation before normal movements resume.\n"]],["extensions",["object",1316,"object-prototype",[]]],["description",["string","A protected walking inspection for finding defects before they become a train movement hazard."]],["status",["string","draft"]]]]]]]],["41",["object",1317,"object-prototype",[["conformance",["string","strict"]],["identity",["object",1318,"object-prototype",[["path",["string","railway/yard-handoff.md"]],["documentId",["string","railway/yard-handoff"]]]]],["type",["string","concept"]],["metadata",["object",1319,"object-prototype",[["title",["string","Yard Handoff"]],["description",["string","A concise transfer routine for making shunting work visible and safe between crews."]],["tags",["array",1320,3,[["0",["string","yard"]],["1",["string","handoff"]],["2",["string","safety"]]]]],["sourceText",["string",""]]]]],["facets",["object",1321,"object-prototype",[["status",["object",1322,"object-prototype",[["classified",["boolean",true]],["value",["string","stable"]]]]],["trust",["object",1323,"object-prototype",[["classified",["boolean",true]],["value",["string","unverified"]]]]],["staleness",["object",1324,"object-prototype",[["classified",["boolean",true]]]]]]]],["sections",["array",1325,4,[["0",["object",1326,"object-prototype",[["id",["string","railway/yard-handoff#yard-handoff"]],["headingPath",["string","Yard Handoff"]],["text",["string",""]],["startLine",["number","4028000000000000"]],["endLine",["number","4028000000000000"]]]]],["1",["object",1327,"object-prototype",[["id",["string","railway/yard-handoff#yard-handoff-purpose"]],["headingPath",["string","Yard Handoff > Purpose"]],["text",["string","A yard handoff transfers responsibility, not just a list of tasks. The outgoing shunter briefs the incoming operator beside the working plan before either person moves a vehicle. Confirm the current track, locomotive, consist, brake status, protection limits, and any vehicles that must not be coupled. State which routes are occupied, which points are clipped or locked, and whether a signal, radio, or crossing fault changes the plan."]],["startLine",["number","402c000000000000"]],["endLine",["number","402e000000000000"]]]]],["2",["object",1328,"object-prototype",[["id",["string","railway/yard-handoff#yard-handoff-handoff-sequence"]],["headingPath",["string","Yard Handoff > Handoff sequence"]],["text",["string","Read back the movement order and repeat the next intended move in plain language. Walk to the control board or yard diagram and mark completed, active, and deferred work. The incoming operator checks the radio channel, handbrake count, and visible clearance at the first route. If information is missing, stop the transfer and call the yard controller; never infer that an old note still applies."]],["startLine",["number","4031000000000000"]],["endLine",["number","4032000000000000"]]]]],["3",["object",1329,"object-prototype",[["id",["string","railway/yard-handoff#yard-handoff-record"]],["headingPath",["string","Yard Handoff > Record"]],["text",["string","Log the time, names, locomotive number, outstanding hazards, and agreed next move. The outgoing operator remains available until the incoming operator confirms acceptance."]],["startLine",["number","4034000000000000"]],["endLine",["number","4035000000000000"]]]]]]]],["diagnostics",["array",1330,0,[]]],["document",["object",1331,"object-prototype",[["id",["string","railway/yard-handoff"]],["type",["string","concept"]],["title",["string","Yard Handoff"]],["tags",["array",1332,3,[["0",["string","yard"]],["1",["string","handoff"]],["2",["string","safety"]]]]],["sources",["array",1333,0,[]]],["verified",["array",1334,0,[]]],["body",["string","\n# Yard Handoff\n\n## Purpose\nA yard handoff transfers responsibility, not just a list of tasks. The outgoing shunter briefs the incoming operator beside the working plan before either person moves a vehicle. Confirm the current track, locomotive, consist, brake status, protection limits, and any vehicles that must not be coupled. State which routes are occupied, which points are clipped or locked, and whether a signal, radio, or crossing fault changes the plan.\n\n## Handoff sequence\nRead back the movement order and repeat the next intended move in plain language. Walk to the control board or yard diagram and mark completed, active, and deferred work. The incoming operator checks the radio channel, handbrake count, and visible clearance at the first route. If information is missing, stop the transfer and call the yard controller; never infer that an old note still applies.\n\n## Record\nLog the time, names, locomotive number, outstanding hazards, and agreed next move. The outgoing operator remains available until the incoming operator confirms acceptance.\n"]],["extensions",["object",1335,"object-prototype",[]]],["description",["string","A concise transfer routine for making shunting work visible and safe between crews."]],["status",["string","stable"]]]]]]]]]]],["sections",["number","4064400000000000"]],["degradedDocuments",["number","3ff0000000000000"]]]]]]]
